window.MODULES.CT4111 = {"name":"Generative AI for Software Engineering","evidence":"past-papers","exam":{"summary":["2 hours, written exam (70% of the module); you MUST get 40% in this exam to pass the module (else INC).","Section 1: answer 2 of 3 questions (Q1-Q3). Section 2: answer 2 of 3 questions (Q4-Q6). So 4 questions in total.","Every question is worth 25 marks, 'marked equally'. Questions have lettered parts with marks in brackets, e.g. (a) [10], (b) [5], (c) [10].","Roughly 4.8 minutes per 5 marks: about 28-30 minutes per question, leaving ~5 minutes spare. Aim for 1 distinct, named point per mark."],"structure":[{"label":"Section 1 (Q1-Q3), answer 2","detail":"Foundations and early lifecycle: modes of AI use, definitions, requirements engineering (AI4RE), prompts, ADRs and design, SDLC phases, SE principles, RAG."},{"label":"Section 2 (Q4-Q6), answer 2","detail":"Later lifecycle and society: testing and TDD, maintenance (modification types, fault localisation, library migration), static analysis agents, Sutton's bitter lesson, legal and ethical risks."},{"label":"Typical part types","detail":"Define/distinguish [8-10]; recommend + justify [5]; list/describe components [3-4]; 'propose a detailed prompt' [12]; 'outline with a diagram' [9-10]; opinion with two reasons [8]."},{"label":"Bonus practice question","detail":"The sample paper adds a 4th Section 2 question (mutation testing, need for GenAI in static analysis, bias/stereotypes, verifiable tasks). Treat it as fair game."}],"freq":[{"topic":"Modes of AI use (conversational / editing / agent)","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"Definitions (GenAI, ML, guardrails, self-play, HITL)","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"AI4RE process and resources","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"ADR components and GenAI benefits","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"SDLC six phases and where to use GenAI","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"Changed SE principles (DRY, abstraction, reqs before code, IaC)","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"RAG","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"GenAI + TDD, test-suite prompting","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"Modification types, fault localisation and repair, library migration","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"Agentic static analysis triage and repair","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"Bitter lesson","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"Legal risks (IP, GDPR, liability, EU AI Act)","count":1,"of":1},{"topic":"Mutation testing, bias, verifiable tasks (bonus Q)","count":1,"of":1}],"years":[{"year":"Sample paper (the only paper available)","themes":["Q1 Modes of AI use [10] + recommend a mode per scenario [5] + five definitions [10]","Q2 AI4RE process and resources [10], programmatic prompt scenario [3], ADR components [4], GenAI benefits for ADRs [8]","Q3 One SDLC phase for GenAI, refer to all six [12], changed SE principles [8], RAG [5]","Q4 GenAI in TDD [9], detailed prompt for ManagePrint test suite [12], prompt changes that matter [4]","Q5 Modification request types and GenAI [9], fault localisation and repair with diagram [10], library migration + sample prompt [6]","Q6 Agentic static-analysis triage and repair with diagram [9], bitter lesson [8], legal risks for Irish loan startup [8]","Bonus: mutation testing [6], need for GenAI in static analysis [5], bias and open weights [8], verifiable tasks [6]"]}],"tips":["Mark-per-point rule: if a part is worth 10, write about 8-10 distinct, named points; if 5, write 5. Use headed sub-parts (i), (ii), (iii) exactly as the question lists them.","When a question says 'refer to X, Y and Z' (e.g. background vs foreground, sync vs async, human role) tick off each named item explicitly or you lose those marks.","'Define and distinguish' means definition of each + a comparison on the same axes (use a table-like layout: role of human, context, output artefact, sync/async).","'Propose a detailed prompt' questions: write the actual prompt text, with role, context, task, constraints, output format, examples. Then add 1-2 lines explaining why each part is there.","Diagram questions: draw boxes for the human and for each GenAI agent, label the arrows (what is passed), and show the loop back. Label who does what.","'In your opinion' questions: take a clear stance, give exactly the number of reasons asked (e.g. two), and acknowledge one counter-argument.","Use lecture vocabulary: human in the loop, guardrails, context window, RAG, red-green-refactor, killed/surviving mutants, ADR, C4, modification request, GDPR principles, EU AI Act risk tiers.","You can choose questions: pick the 2+2 where you can hit every sub-part. Read all six before choosing; do not pick a question whose final part you cannot answer."],"note":"There is only ONE (sample) past paper, so topic priority combines it with lecture emphasis: every theme in the sample paper is ranked first, then topics that received heavy lecture time (testing, maintenance, ethics) and finally topics with little or no examinable content (agent classification, tool set-up). The sample paper's freq entries are '1/1'; lecturer slides also list 'Some exam questions' (SDLC phases, jagged intelligence, chain of thought, tools for LLMs, help in the feedback phase), which are folded in."},"hints":null,"code":"CT4111","topics":[{"id":"modes-of-ai-use","title":"Modes of AI use in coding","priority":1,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q1(a)(b) + Topic 2 lecture","marks":"10 + 5 marks","summary":"**The ways of coding with AI differ in who is driving, how much the AI can see, and whether you watch it work. The more it does alone, the more you become a checker instead of a writer.**","mnemonic":"Ask - Edit - Delegate: the human goes Author, Director, Task-specifier","source":["CT4111_Notes_02_Topic_2","CT4111_Notes_01b_Getting_Started_AI_Coding","CT4111_Notes_06_Implementation_Workflows"],"sections":[{"heading":"The three exam modes (define and distinguish)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Mode 1: Conversational (a separate chat)"},"You chat with a bot (ChatGPT, Claude app) outside your code.","It only sees what you paste or upload.","It explains code and errors, and suggests snippets.","You are the **author**. You copy and paste what you want.",{"type":"p","text":"Mode 2: Conversational editing (assistant in your editor)"},"A chat panel inside your code editor (Copilot Chat, Cline, Cursor chat, Gemini in Colab).","It sees your file or project and suggests small edits.","You see the change and click Keep or Undo.","You are the **director**: you instruct and approve each change.",{"type":"p","text":"Mode 3: Repository agent (you hand over a task)"},"You give it a task, usually an issue.","It works alone in its own safe copy of the project, running commands and tests.","It comes back with a **branch, commits or a pull request**.","You **specify and approve**, reviewing it like a teammate's work.",{"type":"table","head":["Aspect","Conversational","Conversational editing","Repository agent"],"rows":[["Where it runs","Separate chat window or app","Inside the IDE","Cloud or CLI sandbox on the repo"],["Foreground / background","Foreground (you are in the chat)","Foreground (you watch the diff)","Background (you work on something else)"],["Sync / async","Synchronous turn-taking","Synchronous, short loop (seconds)","Asynchronous: you return later to a PR"],["Context the AI has","Only what you paste or upload","Current file or project workspace","Whole repository plus sandbox tools and tests"],["Typical output","Explanation, snippet, pseudocode","Patch or modified file","Branch, commits, pull request"],["Human role","Author (you write and integrate)","Director (you instruct, approve each diff)","Task specifier and approver (you review the PR)"],["Control / risk","Highest control, slowest, lowest risk","Medium control, quick checking","Lowest per-step control; reviewing is the bottleneck"]]}]},{"heading":"Foreground vs background, sync vs async (the vocabulary)","items":[{"type":"note","text":"What the slides actually say: only the interactive agent is labelled foreground, the repository agent is said to work asynchronously, and interactive loops take seconds to minutes. Calling chat and editing foreground and synchronous is a standard inference, not printed in the table."},"**Foreground**: you watch and can interrupt. Your attention is taken (chat, editing, interactive agent).","**Background**: it works while you do something else (repository agent, automated review).","**Synchronous**: you wait for each reply, turn by turn.","**Asynchronous**: you submit and get told when it is done, perhaps hours later, usually as a pull request.",{"type":"p","text":"Other modes"},"**Interactive coding agent**: in between. It reads the project, edits several files and runs tests while you watch (Claude Code CLI, Copilot agent mode, Cursor agent). You are the **supervisor**.","**Inline completion**: guesses the next words as you type. You are the author.","**Automated review**: the other way round. You write, the AI comments when you push. You stay responsible, and a human review is still needed."]},{"heading":"Spectrum diagram","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"Advisory chat","sub":"Author","color":0},{"label":"Inline completion","sub":"Author","color":0},{"label":"Conversational editing","sub":"Director","color":1},{"label":"Interactive agent","sub":"Supervisor (foreground)","color":1},{"label":"Repository agent","sub":"Task specifier + approver (background)","color":2}],"direction":"row"},"caption":"More autonomy to the right: less human control per step, more reliance on tests, specification and review."}]},{"heading":"Which mode for which scenario (recommendation recipe)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Use conversational when..."},"You are learning, decoding old code, reading an error or comparing designs.","Why: you stay in charge, low risk, no access to your project needed.",{"type":"p","text":"Use conversational editing when..."},"The change is small and quick to check: add a function, write tests, rename, boilerplate.","Why: faster than copy-paste, and you see the changes straight away.",{"type":"p","text":"Use a repository agent when..."},"The task is clearly defined and well tested: a labelled bug with a failing test, upgrading a library, routine migration, documentation, many similar tickets.","Why: runs in the background, handles many at once, and the pull request records it.","Avoid it when judgement about the product is needed or tests are weak.","**Rule of thumb**: give more freedom only when the task is clear, tests are good and mistakes are cheap.","A sensible order: chat to design, edit to build, agent for routine bulk work.","Results depend on you: your prompt, how precise the task is, how carefully you read the output."]},{"heading":"Tool examples (taxonomy table)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Chat tools (slide table, Aug 2026)"},"ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini apps, Copilot Chat on github.com, Cursor chat, and local models (Ollama, LM Studio).","You can give them pasted code, files, a folder, a repository or error screenshots.",{"type":"p","text":"Completion and editing tools"},"**Completion**: Copilot, Cursor Tab, Antigravity, Gemini in Colab, Continue, Tabby.","**Editing**: Codex IDE extension, Claude Code in IDE, Antigravity IDE, Copilot Chat, Cursor chat, Cline, Roo Code. The lecture's example is Gemini in Colab.",{"type":"p","text":"Agent tools"},"**Interactive**: Claude Code CLI and Code view, Codex CLI, Antigravity CLI, Copilot agent mode and CLI, Cursor agent, Aider, OpenCode.","**Repository**: Claude Code on the web, Codex cloud, Jules, Copilot cloud agent, Cursor background agents, self-hosted CI runners.","The lecture example is ChatGPT Codex on a shopping-planner repository.","Results also depend on the project's context, tests, tools and how true to life the environment is.","**Review**: Codex code review, Claude Code Review and GitHub Action, Code Assist for GitHub, Copilot code review, Bugbot, CodeRabbit, Qodo, Greptile.","These catch missing error handling, unsafe input and forgotten edge cases."]}],"pitfalls":["For each mode give the human's role, foreground or background, and sync or async.","A repository agent still needs your approval of its pull request.","If asked, give a different scenario and reason for each mode.","Automated review checks code. A repository agent writes it."]},{"id":"genai-definitions","title":"Core definitions: GenAI, ML, guardrails, self-play, human in the loop","priority":2,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q1(c) + Topic 1 lecture","marks":"10 marks (2 per term)","summary":"**Be able to define each key AI term in one sentence and give one example.**","mnemonic":"AI > ML > DL > GenAI/LLM nesting; guardrails guard, self-play self-teaches, HITL holds responsibility","source":["CT4111_Notes_01a_Overview","CT4111_Notes_02_Topic_2","CT4111_Notes_06_Implementation_Workflows","CT4111_Notes_11c_Article_Bitter_Lesson"],"sections":[{"heading":"The five sample-paper terms (two to three lines each)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Generative AI"},"AI that makes **new content** (text, code, images, audio) by learning patterns from lots of data.","Example: Claude writing a function from your request.",{"type":"p","text":"Machine learning"},"Computers learning **without being explicitly programmed** (Samuel, 1959).","Behaviour comes from data, not hand-written rules.","Example: a spam filter trained on labelled messages.",{"type":"p","text":"Guardrails"},"Limits that keep an AI behaving **safely and as intended**.","Technical: types and tests in the prompt, permission settings, a filter that strips personal data, a sandbox, version control.","Process: review, audit trails, approved tool policies.",{"type":"p","text":"Self-play"},"A model improves by competing with, or setting and checking tasks for, **copies of itself**.","The reward comes from the outcome (win, tests pass), with no human examples.","Examples: AlphaGo and chess; learning code and maths problems that can be checked.",{"type":"p","text":"Human in the loop (HITL)"},"A person **reviews, decides or approves** at key points, and is responsible for the result.","The person needs to be skilled.","Debate: real safety check, or false comfort (people lose skills and rubber-stamp)?"]},{"heading":"The AI terminology ladder","items":["Each term below fits inside the one before it.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"Artificial Intelligence","sub":"1950s","color":0},{"label":"Machine Learning","sub":"1980s","color":0},{"label":"Deep Learning","sub":"2010s","color":1},{"label":"Generative AI / LLMs","sub":"2020s","color":2}],"direction":"row"},"caption":"Each is a subset of the one before; LLMs can also do non-generative tasks like sentiment classification."},"**AI** (EU AI Act): a machine system with some autonomy, which may adapt, and which works out from input how to produce predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that affect the world.","**Deep learning**: machine learning with many-layered neural networks, such as CNNs and Transformers. The trained network is a rough summary of its data.","**Foundation model**: a large network trained on one general task, then adapted to others.","**LLM**: a foundation model that predicts the next token. A chatbot = LLM + web page + tools + rules.","**Token**: a word or part of a word.","**Context window**: how much earlier text the model can look at.","**Agentic AI**: an LLM wrapped so it acts by itself towards a goal, using tools and memory. \"From chatting to doing.\"","**RLHF** (reinforcement learning with human feedback): teaches the model to take turns, be polite and stay on topic.","LLMs can also do non-creative jobs, such as judging whether a review is positive."]},{"heading":"How an LLM is trained (for 'define' questions)","items":["**1. Pre-training**: learn to predict the next token from web pages, books and code.","**2. RLHF**: shape behaviour using people's preferences.","**3. More practice**: feedback from AI, from answers that can be proven right (code, maths), or from self-play.","Today: **train, prompt, predict**. You give a new task by asking, not by retraining.","The old way needed many labelled examples for every task."]}],"pitfalls":["Guardrails are not just content filters. They include prompt limits, tests, version control and privacy filters.","Self-play is a model against copies of itself, not two people.","For human in the loop, say the human is responsible.","Two clear sentences and an example earn the marks. One word does not."]},{"id":"ai4re-process","title":"AI for Requirements Engineering (AI4RE): process and resources","priority":3,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q2(a) + Topic 4 lecture (long)","marks":"10 marks","summary":"**AI4RE means using AI to help with requirements engineering, with a human firmly in charge. It uses fill-in-the-blank prompt templates, and an engineer checks everything.**","mnemonic":"EDVM: Elicit, Document, Validate, Manage; and always HITL","source":["CT4111_Notes_04_Requirements_Engineering"],"sections":[{"heading":"What AI4RE is","items":["**RE (requirements engineering)** is working out what a system must do.","**IREB** is a non-profit RE board (since 2006, runs the CPRE certificate, 70+ countries).","**AI4RE** is its programme for using generative AI in RE.",{"type":"p","text":"Three principles"},"**Practical and responsible**: use what LLMs, chatbots and RAG can do, and be clear about limits.","**Human in the loop**: AI assists but never has the final say. People filter out noise, made-up content and bias.","**Risk management built in**: protect data and confidential material, and check output critically.","AI does not replace the requirements engineer. Their job grows to include **overseeing** the AI."]},{"heading":"The process (what you do in the lab)","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"1 Define placeholders","sub":"[DOMAIN] [SYSTEM] [FORMAT]"},{"label":"2 Run Prompt 1","sub":"stakeholder groups, Onion layers"},{"label":"3 Attach org chart","sub":"optional, as a file"},{"label":"4 Run Prompt 2","sub":"map groups to individuals/roles"},{"label":"5 Validate with experts","sub":"groups, mappings, assumptions"}],"direction":"row"},"caption":"The five lecture steps for the Identify Stakeholders templates. (Generic AI4RE pattern: pick RE task, choose template, fill placeholders, run, validate, refine; that framing is general knowledge.)"},{"type":"p","text":"Example: find stakeholders for an Italian restaurant delivery service"},"**Prompt 1**: list stakeholder groups, sorted by influence, sorted into the **Onion Model**, with assumptions listed separately, as a table.","**Prompt 2**: match each group to real people or roles using an attached organisation chart. State any guesses, and keep the same group names.",{"type":"p","text":"The Onion Model (rings from the middle out)"},"**1.** The product itself.","**2.** The system: users, developers, operators, maintainers.","**3.** The containing system: customers, sponsors, connected systems.","**4.** The wider world: regulators, public, competitors.","It is a checklist so indirect stakeholders are not missed.",{"type":"p","text":"What happened"},"The AI spotted a hidden stakeholder (the owner's son, who wants to reuse the system) from a side remark.","Without a real organisation chart, Prompt 2 is only illustrative. **Check with people who know the business.**",{"type":"p","text":"Parts of the prompt"},"The AI's role (domain expert), your role (requirements engineer), the purpose (prepare interviews), the task, the input file and the output format.",{"type":"p","text":"Output of Prompt 1"},"**System layer**: customers, restaurant owner, delivery riders, kitchen staff.","**Immediate**: payment providers (high influence), Google Maps, IT team.","**Wider**: owner's son (low), regulators (medium), competitors (low).","Assumptions: payment and maps count because they set limits; regulators stay general until the country is known; no till system was mentioned.",{"type":"p","text":"Four ways AI supports RE"},"**Elicitation**: interview questions, pretend personas, brainstorming.","**Documentation**: formatting, user stories, translating, drafting.","**Validation**: checking consistency and flagging contradictions.","**Management**: sorting requirements, prioritising, tracing.","The Prompt Guide warns against prompting each thing in isolation (\"prompt chaos\")."]},{"heading":"Resources AI4RE provides","items":["**Syllabus and study guide** (IREB, 2026), with certificate levels (Foundation, Advanced, Expert, micro-credentials).","**Prompt Guide**: regularly updated templates for four RE phases.",{"type":"table","head":["Template group","What it supports"],"rows":[["Elicitation","Simulated interviews, role-playing stakeholder personas, domain questionnaires, stakeholder identification, brainstorming"],["Documentation","Formatting and spelling, user stories, translating technical terms, turning raw notes into formal specs"],["Validation","Rules to scan drafts for ambiguity, vagueness, internal conflict; automated syntax and consistency checks; early quality feedback"],["Management","Classify and tag requirements, prioritisation matrices (e.g. MoSCoW), tracing downstream"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Each Prompt Guide page has six parts"},"**1.** The RE task.","**2.** How AI helps.","**3.** Conclusion: the value, and where a human must still check.","**4.** Templates with [PLACEHOLDERS] for AI role, your role, task and format.","**5.** A filled-in example with real output.","**6.** Pitfalls and tips (privacy, tool limits).","Tie prompts to real project documents (use cases, models, limits).",{"type":"p","text":"Warnings given"},"Do not upload charts or personal data to public AI tools without approval (GDPR). Anonymise.","Free tiers may not accept files (about 20 MB limit on paid ones, so split them).","Chatbots cannot read Teams recordings. Transcribe them first."]},{"heading":"Four human roles in the AI-augmented RE (IREB AI4RE syllabus)","items":["**Process designer**: chooses tools and fits them into the RE process.","**Prompt designer and reviewer**: refines prompts and judges the output.","**Risk and compliance manager**: protects data, avoids bias, follows the rules.","**Communicator and trust builder**: explains the AI's use, benefits and limits to stakeholders."]},{"heading":"Opportunities and risks of AI in RE","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Opportunities"},"Pull requirements from reviews and interviews.","Group feedback into topics.","Help with interview guides and follow-up questions.","Summarise meetings.",{"type":"p","text":"Risks"},"Made-up requirements.","Bias: loud users get over-represented.","Privacy and confidentiality (GDPR).","Time spent checking."]}],"pitfalls":["Do not just say \"use ChatGPT\". Name the Prompt Guide, the four phases, placeholders and expert checking.","Mention human in the loop and risk management.","Say what the resources contain, not only their names."]},{"id":"adr-genai","title":"Architecture Decision Records and GenAI benefits","priority":4,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q2(c)(d) + Topic 5 lecture","marks":"4 + 8 marks","summary":"**An ADR is a short record of one design decision: what was decided, why, what else was considered and what follows. AI can help write it, but a human must check it.**","mnemonic":"ADR = the WHY behind the code. Benefits: C R O G (Consistency, Research, Onboarding, Governance)","source":["CT4111_Notes_05_Design"],"sections":[{"heading":"Why ADRs exist","items":["**ADR** = Architecture Decision Record.","**The old way**: a senior developer says \"we will do microservices\" and everyone agrees.","The problems: if that person leaves, the knowledge leaves.","Trade-offs are never written down.","Six months later nobody knows why, or what else was possible.","**An ADR** is kept in the project (for example docs/architecture/adr/001-title.md).","It records the decision, background, options and results."]},{"heading":"Main components of an ADR","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Component","Content"],"rows":[["Title","Short decision title, numbered (ADR-001)"],["Status","Proposed, Accepted, Deprecated or Superseded"],["Date, Author, Deciders","Who proposed it, who is accountable, when"],["Context","Problem being solved, requirements, constraints, current system"],["Decision","The single clear choice and why"],["Alternatives considered","Options A, B, C each with pros and cons (winner marked)"],["Consequences","What we gain and what we accept (trade-offs, costs, risks, follow-ups)"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Fields in the slide template"},"Title (such as ADR-001).","**Status**: Proposed, Accepted, Deprecated or Superseded.","Date, Author, Deciders.","**Context**, **Decision**, and **Alternatives Considered** (Options A, B, C with pros and cons, winner marked).","Consequences appear in the final example.","In the lecture, the first version is Proposed (2026-05-10) and the final one is Accepted.","With AI help there is also an **AI analysis** note, such as scoring options in a weighted table.","**Workflow**: AI drafts it as Proposed, the team discusses, edits, then accepts.","**Weighing options**: background, criteria, options, strengths and risks, decision, consequences."]},{"heading":"Advantages of GenAI for ADRs (answer each of the four)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Aspect","Traditional","AI-enhanced"],"rows":[["Consistency","Manually checked, prone to human error and varied formats","Auto-detects conflicts with previous decisions; enforces the template and terminology across all ADRs"],["Research","Hours of manual trade-off analysis","Generates instant pros and cons based on technology trends; proposes 3-5 alternatives and a score matrix"],["Onboarding","New hires must read hundreds of files","Semantic search / Q&A: 'Why don't we use NoSQL?' gives the rationale and links the ADR"],["Governance","Documentation goes out of date; drift unnoticed","Proactive alerts when code drifts from ADR intent; audit trail of decisions and status changes"]]}]},{"heading":"Risks and disadvantages","items":["**Made-up technical facts**: AI writes what sounds right. It may invent features or suggest outdated patterns.","**Missing context**: it does not know unwritten knowledge, team skills, budget or old code. (In the example, the team's Spring Boot skill decided the winner.)","**Nobody owns it**: if people rubber-stamp AI text, no one is responsible when it fails.","**Co-pilot, not pilot**: AI drafts, humans decide and sign."]},{"heading":"Worked example: sports court booking system","items":["A sports court booking system.","**Needs**: 20k users, 2k bookings a day, 95% of responses (P95) under 250 ms, 99.9% uptime, GDPR, an audit log.","**Options**: monolith, microservices, serverless, hybrid.","**Weights**: 30/25/25/20.","**Scores**: Monolith 7.45, Microservices 7.0, Serverless 7.75, Hybrid 8.00.","**Winner: hybrid**. A Spring Boot core, AWS Lambda for background notifications, and managed PostgreSQL for GDPR and audit.","It takes 3 weeks, against 8 for microservices.","**Final results**: P95 of 180 ms, audit log in Postgres, 25% cheaper than microservices, and the team must learn Lambda."]}],"pitfalls":["Include status, alternatives with pros and cons, date and deciders, not only context, decision and consequences.","Give each of the four benefits its own specific point, not just \"saves time\".","Say a human still approves the ADR."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"An ADR records","items":["Title, status, date, deciders","Context","Decision","Alternatives, pros and cons","Consequences"]},{"title":"GenAI helps","items":["Consistency","Research","Onboarding","Governance"]},{"title":"Risk","items":["Hallucination","Accountability gap"]}]}}},{"id":"sdlc-genai","title":"SDLC phases and where to use GenAI","priority":5,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q3(a) + Topic 1 lecture exam questions","marks":"12 marks","summary":"**There are six phases of building software. If asked to use AI in only one, pick where work is repetitive, easy to check and cheap to get wrong, and say why not the other five.**","mnemonic":"R-D-C-T-D-F: Requirements, Design, Coding, Testing, Deployment, Feedback","source":["CT4111_Notes_01a_Overview"],"sections":[{"heading":"Six phases: activities and AI contributions","items":["**SDLC** = software development life cycle.","The six phases: requirements, design, coding, testing, deployment, feedback.",{"type":"table","head":["Phase","Activities","GenAI contributions"],"rows":[["1 Requirements gathering","Planning, scope, objectives, resources; functional and technical requirements; reviews and approval","Summarise meeting notes, web-search existing products, generate initial user stories and a project timeline"],["2 Design","Architecture, database design and relationships, component logic, APIs, tables","Discuss alternatives iteratively; summarise into design documents and acceptance criteria; ADRs, C4 diagrams"],["3 Coding","Writing code, code review, static analysis, version control, pull requests","Code generation and completion, review of PRs, pull-request descriptions"],["4 Testing","Test cases, unit, integration, system, user acceptance tests","Auto-generate tests, test data, mocks; analyse failures"],["5 Deployment","Build and DevOps, smoke-testing the live environment","Debug and review devops config files, generate pipelines"],["6 Feedback","Interact with stakeholders, bug reports, customer input","Summarise customer feedback, classify and prioritise bug reports"]]},"**Agile reminder**: short sprints, each producing something usable, with frequent feedback.","Also: continuous integration and delivery, a definition of done, technical debt and retrospectives.","User story: \"As a [role], I want [feature], so that [benefit].\""]},{"heading":"How to answer 'only one phase' (12 marks)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Steps for a 12-mark answer"},"**1.** State your choice and your tests for choosing: lots of repetitive work, easy to check, small damage if wrong, quick to review, big time saving.","**2.** Give 3-4 reasons with a concrete example.","**3.** Cover **each of the other five phases**: why not, and what you would do instead.","**4.** Add safeguards (review, tests, version control) and one limitation.",{"type":"p","text":"Model choice A: Testing"},"Tests are generated fast (unit, edge cases, regression, data, mocks).","Results are checkable (run them, mutation score, coverage).","It removes the \"tests take too long\" excuse.","**Risk**: AI may not know what correct means (the oracle problem), so tests can be shallow. Humans define the business rules.",{"type":"p","text":"Alternative choice B: Coding"},"It is the most work, and where AI is strongest, since code can be compiled and tested.","**Risks**: security, licensing, more duplicated code and a review bottleneck.",{"type":"p","text":"Why not the others"},"**Requirements**: needs human judgement and politics. Wrong ones are costly later.","**Design**: needs unwritten knowledge and long-term responsibility. AI does not know the team.","**Deployment**: mistakes hit the live system and involve passwords and access. Drafting config under review is fine.","**Feedback**: needs empathy and priorities. Summarising is low risk, so it is a good second choice."]},{"heading":"Related lecture exam questions (Topic 1)","items":["Name and define the six phases (see the table).",{"type":"p","text":"How can an LLM help with feedback?"},"Summarise and group comments, sort bug reports, draft replies, spot trends. A human sets priorities.",{"type":"p","text":"An LLM makes one token at a time. How is it made useful?"},"Run it in a loop, give good prompts and context (RAG), add tools, step-by-step reasoning and an agent wrapper."]}],"pitfalls":["Mention all six phases, not just your choice.","Do not justify with \"AI is good at it\". Give reasons about checking, risk and responsibility.","Software engineering is more than coding: requirements, documents, testing, maintenance, deployment, law and ethics."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Requirements","color":0},{"label":"Design","color":1},{"label":"Coding","color":2},{"label":"Testing","color":3},{"label":"Deployment","color":4},{"label":"Feedback","color":0}]}}},{"id":"se-principles-changed","title":"SE principles changed by GenAI","priority":6,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q3(b) + Topic 1 lecture","marks":"8 marks (2 per principle)","summary":"**For each principle say whether it changed, how and why: DRY weakens (copies are cheap to make and fix, but duplication still harms review and context), abstraction shifts to specifications and modular context, requirements-before-code is strengthened (prompt = specification), infrastructure as code extends to prompts, skills and agent config.**","mnemonic":"DRY relaxed, Abstraction moved, Requirements raised, IaC extended","source":["CT4111_Notes_01a_Overview","CT4111_Notes_06_Implementation_Workflows","CT4111_Notes_08_Maintenance","CT4111_Notes_11e_Article_Code_Review"],"sections":[{"heading":"Principles listed in the lecture","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Group","Principles"],"rows":[["Design","Separation of concerns, abstraction / information hiding, high cohesion, DRY, KISS / YAGNI, design for change, fail fast"],["Process","Requirements before code, user stories, iterative development with continuous testing, version control everything, code review as quality gate, measure before optimising"],["Team and organisation","Conway's law, shared code ownership, CI/CD"],["Operations","Automate repetitive tasks, observability, infrastructure as code"],["Documentation","Document decisions (ADRs), single source of truth"]]},"Module philosophy: GenAI has changed some things and the change will not stop, so learn what changed, what is still changing and what stays the same."]},{"heading":"Words used when discussing these principles","items":["**Refactor**: improve code's structure without changing what it does.","**Churn**: code that gets rewritten or thrown away soon after it was written.","**Context window**: how much text an AI model can look at at once.","**Hallucinate**: the AI confidently makes something up.","**Guardrails**: checks that keep AI output safe and correct.","**Vibe coding**: letting the AI write code from loose requests without reading it closely."]},{"heading":"(i) Don't repeat yourself: weakened but not dead","items":["**DRY** (\"don't repeat yourself\") means each piece of logic is written in one place.",{"type":"p","text":"Why it mattered"},"Duplicated code means fixing a bug in many places, and humans dislike typing the same thing.",{"type":"p","text":"What changed"},"AI makes copies almost free to write, and can also merge them quickly.","So some teams accept duplication for clarity, and generated code is often regenerated rather than maintained.","Evidence: a study of 211 million lines shows duplication and churn up, and reuse down, with AI.",{"type":"p","text":"Why it still matters"},"Duplication hurts maintenance, review and the AI itself, leading to inconsistent fixes.","**Net**: DRY becomes something you clean up afterwards with AI's help (\"technical deflation\": cleaning code gets cheaper) rather than a rule enforced while writing."]},{"heading":"(ii) Abstraction / information hiding: shifts level","items":["**Information hiding** means putting details behind a simple **interface** (the list of things you can ask a part to do), so users of that part need not know how it works.",{"type":"p","text":"Why it mattered"},"Humans can only hold a little in their head at once.",{"type":"p","text":"What changed"},"**LLMs** (large language models, the AI behind chat and coding tools) can read large amounts of code and need less hidden from them.","Anthropic reports that \"nobody on the team holds a complete mental model\".","The level humans work at moves up to the **specification** or prompt (what you want) and the contract (tests and interfaces).",{"type":"p","text":"Why it still matters"},"Context windows are limited, and models make things up when given too little or too much.","So modular code and clear interfaces help AI too, and **types** and interfaces restrict what it can generate."]},{"heading":"(iii) Requirements before code: strengthened and reshaped","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Why it matters"},"With LLMs the **what** (what to build) and the **how** (the code) come apart.","The AI cannot guess what to build, so the developer must specify it thoroughly. Prompting is a form of specification, so requirements work matters more.",{"type":"p","text":"The new pressure"},"Prototypes and vibe coding make code cheap, which tempts you to code first. Then unclear wishes produce the wrong software very quickly.","Tests written first (**TDD**) and user stories become requirements that can be run.","\"Ask questions and state assumptions before coding\" is a prompting principle."]},{"heading":"(iv) Infrastructure as code: extended","items":["**Infrastructure as code** (IaC) means describing servers and setup in files kept in version control.",{"type":"p","text":"Why it mattered"},"Environments that are repeatable, reviewable and versioned.",{"type":"p","text":"What changed"},"GenAI writes and reviews these files and deployment configuration quickly.","**Agents** (AI that carries out actions itself) with access to a command line can change infrastructure directly.","So IaC and version control become the guardrail: every AI change can be inspected and undone.",{"type":"p","text":"The \"as code\" idea spreads"},"Prompts, skills, instruction files (`CLAUDE.md`, `REVIEW.md`) and agent settings should be versioned like code, to avoid **prompt debt** (a pile of unexplained prompts nobody can maintain)."]},{"heading":"Other principles that changed (bonus examples)","items":["**Code review as a quality gate**: AI review now runs on every pull request; how much human approval is needed depends on the risk (Anthropic).","**Shared ownership and Conway's law** (software structure mirrors how the team is organised): agents are now team members, and their organisation can arise by itself (Mollick).","**Automate repetitive tasks**: now extends to boilerplate (routine standard code), documentation and tests.","**Document decisions**: AI drafts the decision records (ADRs), but humans stay accountable.",{"type":"p","text":"Unchanged"},"**Separation of concerns** (each part handles one thing), **design for change**, **fail fast** (report errors at once) and **measure before optimising** still hold. AI works better when the architecture is clean."]}],"pitfalls":["Answering 'all four changed completely' or 'none changed': give a nuanced which/how/why for each of the four.","No 'why': each principle needs the original reason and the GenAI-driven reason it moved.","Presenting your reasoning as slide fact: it is fine to argue, but anchor in lecture points (decoupled what/how, duplication study, prompts as artefacts)."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Weakens","items":["DRY: copies are cheap"]},{"title":"Shifts","items":["Abstraction: to specs and modular context"]},{"title":"Strengthens","items":["Requirements before code: prompt = spec"]},{"title":"Extends","items":["Infrastructure as code: prompts, skills, agent config"]}]}}},{"id":"tdd-genai","title":"Test-driven development and GenAI","priority":7,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q4(a) + Topics 6, 7","marks":"9 marks (3 x 3)","summary":"**TDD is red (failing test), green (minimal code), refactor; GenAI augments each stage: prompt-to-test for RED, auto-implementation for GREEN, AI code review and refactoring for REFACTOR, with the human verifying tests and architecture because writing a test is now the way you instruct the AI.**","mnemonic":"RED = AI writes tests, GREEN = AI writes code, REFACTOR = AI cleans, HUMAN verifies","source":["CT4111_Notes_07_Testing","CT4111_Notes_06_Implementation_Workflows"],"sections":[{"heading":"TDD basics","items":["**TDD** (test-driven development): unit tests are written **before** the code. SWEBOK v4 (the standard guide to software engineering knowledge) calls it a construction method, which turns testing from an end check into a **design tool**.","No code until there is a failing test. The tests define what the code must do.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"RED","sub":"write failing test","color":0},{"label":"GREEN","sub":"minimal code to pass","color":2},{"label":"REFACTOR","sub":"clean, tests stay green","color":1}],"direction":"row","loopBack":"next requirement / test"},"caption":"The TDD micro-cycle."},"**RED**: write a small test for a new function. It must fail, which proves the test can catch a bug.","**GREEN**: write the least code that passes. Do not worry about elegance.","**REFACTOR**: tidy up and remove duplication without changing behaviour. The tests are your safety net.","Mantra: \"If it is not green, do not optimise. If you have no tests, do not refactor.\"","Value: less **technical debt** (future work caused by shortcuts), documentation that cannot go out of date, confident refactoring, and a more modular design. Slower at first, faster in the long run."]},{"heading":"Three ways GenAI augments the TDD cycle (exam answer)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"1. Prompt-to-test (RED)"},"From a plain-English requirement or user story, the AI proposes test cases (valid, invalid, boundary, null, business rules) and writes the failing tests.","Example: \"Users must create a secure password\" gives TC-01 3 characters returns false, TC-02 exactly 8 returns true, TC-03 no digit returns false, TC-04 null returns false.","This removes the \"tests take too long\" excuse.",{"type":"p","text":"2. Auto-implementation (GREEN)"},"The AI, or an **agent** looping \"run tests, read failure, edit\", writes the least code to pass and repeats until everything is green.","The tests act as the specification and automatically check the AI's output.",{"type":"p","text":"3. AI review and refactoring (REFACTOR)"},"The AI cleans up the messy passing code (pull out methods, remove duplication, rename) while the tests check behaviour is unchanged.","It can also review the tests for weak assertions and missing **edge cases** (unusual or extreme inputs).",{"type":"p","text":"Human checking"},"A senior developer confirms the design makes sense and reviews the assertions. This matters because of the **oracle problem**: the AI may not know what the correct answer is.","Humans define the test goals, business rules, edge cases and **invariants** (things that must always be true)."]},{"heading":"The lecture's examples of AI-assisted TDD","items":[{"type":"p","text":"QA workflow (4 steps)"},"(1) Business requirement. (2) GenAI defines quality criteria, the code paths and the edge cases (the core step; it can use the **AST**, the code's structure as a tree). (3) Red-green-refactor. (4) Robust, checked code.","Example: a password rule (at least 8 characters, contains a digit). A messy passing `isValid` with three ifs is refactored into `PasswordValidator` with `isInvalidInput` and `containsDigit` helpers.",{"type":"p","text":"Further help"},"AI can generate extra edge cases and **properties** that should hold for any input, explain failing tests, and support **mutation testing** (deliberately planting small bugs to see whether the tests notice)."]},{"heading":"The multi-agent TDD figure","items":["An **agentic** workflow uses several AI agents with different jobs, with **human-in-the-loop** checkpoints where a person decides.",{"type":"p","text":"Stage 1: context and design"},"Agent 1 analyses requirements, Agent 2 designs test scenarios, Agent 3 criticises them. **Human checkpoint 1**: approve the scenarios (go or no-go).",{"type":"p","text":"Stage 2: automatic loop"},"One side produces, another criticises (**actor-critic**). Agent 4 writes unit tests, Agent 5 writes code (fail then pass), Agent 6 runs it and measures coverage, Agent 8 audits security with **SAST** (scanning source code), and a critic reviews refactoring.",{"type":"p","text":"Stage 3: quality gates"},"Agent 9 checks metrics against limits: **LCOM** (lack of cohesion; lower is better) under 0.2, and **cyclomatic complexity** (number of paths through code) under 10. Agent 10 manages the release. **Human checkpoint 2**: final review and merge of the pull request."]},{"heading":"Agentic TDD workflow and risks","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Workflow (Topic 6)"},"A developer writes or reviews the tests. A group of agents (one per test, a reviewer, an integrator, and a coordinator that calls a human when stuck) loops until all tests pass. Then a pull request and human review.",{"type":"p","text":"Pros"},"Eases the bottleneck of humans reviewing code.","Requirements are enforced by tests.","Less need for code that humans can read.",{"type":"p","text":"Cons"},"Needs thorough tests up front.","The LLM may **cheat**: change the tests, or special-case them, so they pass without solving the problem.","**Lock-in**: code only an LLM can work with.","**Token** cost (models charge per chunk of text).","Guardrail: make the tests read-only for the agent, and check with mutation testing and review.","Willison's red-green rule for agents: make the agent write the failing test first, see it fail, then fix, which confirms each fix."]}],"pitfalls":["Listing three ways but not tying each to a TDD stage (red / green / refactor).","Forgetting the human role and the cheating risk (AI changes tests to make them pass).","Saying TDD is 'testing after coding': tests come first."]},{"id":"test-suite-prompting","title":"Prompting for a test suite (ManagePrint example)","priority":8,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q4(b)(c) + Topic 7 prompts","marks":"12 + 4 marks","summary":"**A strong test-generation prompt states role, task and class, language and framework, context, business rules, scenarios to cover, what to avoid (shallow tests), quality rules and output format; the changes that matter most are naming business rules and edge cases, forbidding shallow asserts, and fixing the framework.**","mnemonic":"ROSES: Role, Objective, Scenario, Expected solution, Steps (plus: Avoid and Output)","source":["CT4111_Notes_07_Testing","CT4111_Notes_02_Topic_2"],"sections":[{"heading":"The reusable template (Topic 7)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"Role: You are a senior software test engineer.\nTask: Generate tests for [CLASS / FUNCTION].\nLanguage/framework: [Java + JUnit 5 + Mockito, Python + pytest ...].\nContext: [short domain description].\nBusiness rules: [list the important rules].\nMust cover: [happy path, edge cases, errors, regression].\nAvoid: [trivial assertions, implementation details, shallow tests].\nOutput format: [test names + code + brief explanation]."},"**ROSES framework:** Role (e.g. senior SDET), Objective (single measurable goal), Scenario (stack, architecture, constraints), Expected solution (exact syntax, format, schema), Steps (algorithmic workflow for the reasoning).","**SDET** means software developer in test. **BVA** (boundary value analysis) means testing just below, at and just above a limit. **Mocks** are fake stand-ins for other parts of the system.","Instead of 'Write tests for BookingService', say: JUnit 5 and Mockito, do not only test happy paths, avoid shallow tests like assertNotNull, focus on business rules, edge cases and failure scenarios, assertions must verify meaningful behaviour and explain why each test matters, name the specific cases (overlap detection, invalid time ranges, authorisation, payment failure, audit log).","Variants: integration tests (list integration points, scenarios, quality rules: verify DB state and side effects, not only HTTP status); API tests (status codes and bodies, authn/authz failure, conflict); value tests (boundaries and invariants); a **skill file** (steps: read class, write test class, run tests, never weaken an assertion, check JaCoCo coverage at least 80%, report which rule each test verifies; constraints: Mockito for externals, no getter/setter tests, Given/When/Then Javadoc, independent tests)."]},{"heading":"Worked example: detailed prompt for ManagePrint (12 marks)","items":[{"type":"code","text":"Role: You are a senior Java QA engineer experienced in JUnit 5 and Mockito.\n\nTask: Generate a high-quality unit test suite for the class ManagePrint of a 3D-printing ordering system. Return test code only for the unit under test; use the mocks described below.\n\nContext: ManagePrint lets a user (1) select a print file, (2) select a printer type, (3) select a print material type, (4) set a deadline (day and time) and (5) pay. Collaborators to mock: FileService (validates and sizes the file), PrinterCatalog (supported printers and materials), PricingService, SchedulingService (estimates print duration and queue), PaymentGateway, AuditLog, Clock. I will paste the source of ManagePrint below.\n\nBusiness rules (assumed, correct me if wrong):\n- File must exist, be .stl or .3mf, non-empty and under 100 MB.\n- Material must be compatible with the printer type (e.g. FDM supports PLA/ABS/PETG, resin printers only resin).\n- Deadline must be in the future and at least the estimated print time plus queue time from now; use the injected Clock.\n- Price is computed from material usage; payment is taken only after all selections are valid.\n- If payment fails, no print job is queued and the user is told why. A print is queued exactly once even if pay is called twice.\n- Only authenticated users may order; every order and failure is written to the audit log.\n\nMust cover:\n1. Happy path for each printer and material combination.\n2. Invalid inputs: null or missing file, wrong extension, empty file, oversized file, null printer or material, incompatible printer/material.\n3. Boundary values for the file size limit (limit-1, limit, limit+1) and for the deadline (exactly feasible, 1 minute too early, in the past), time zone and daylight-saving edge cases.\n4. Payment: success, declined card, gateway timeout, double submission (idempotency); verify no job is queued on failure.\n5. Unauthorised user; scheduler unavailable; audit log entries for success and failure.\n6. Order of operations: payment is not attempted before validation passes.\n\nAvoid: shallow tests (assertNotNull, status only, 'method called once' with no outcome check), testing getters and setters, depending on test execution order, real clocks or network, asserting private implementation details.\n\nQuality rules: Arrange-Act-Assert; one scenario per test; descriptive names such as shouldRejectDeadline_WhenEarlierThanEstimatedPrintTime; Mockito for collaborators; parameterised tests for the compatibility matrix; every assertion checks a concrete value or state change.\n\nOutput format: a single ManagePrintTest class with grouped nested classes, followed by a table listing each test name, the business rule it verifies and why it matters. List any assumptions and any rule you could not test."},"Why each part is there (add 2-4 lines like these in the exam): role sets expertise level; context and rules give the oracle (AI cannot guess intended behaviour); explicit scenarios stop happy-path-only suites; BVA and time edge cases target classic defects; 'avoid' blocks shallow tests; framework fixes syntax; output format makes review easy; asking for assumptions exposes where the model guessed.","Process around the prompt: human reviews assertions, adds missing edge cases, asks AI to refine; run coverage (branch) and mutation testing; iterate."]},{"heading":"Prompt changes with the most impact (4 marks)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Naming business rules and expected outcomes"},"The most effective change. It gives the **oracle** (knowledge of the right answer); without it the AI asserts trivial things or just copies what the code does.",{"type":"p","text":"Asking beyond the happy path"},"\"Do not only test happy paths\" (the normal successful case) plus a list of scenarios (payment failure, room occupied, null input) produced failure tests and an edge test for yesterday's date.",{"type":"p","text":"Banning weak assertions"},"\"Avoid shallow tests such as assertNotNull\" forces meaningful checks.",{"type":"p","text":"Naming framework and style"},"\"JUnit 5 and Mockito\", **AAA** (arrange, act, assert) and a naming style give correct, consistent, readable tests.",{"type":"p","text":"Giving the real code"},"Attaching the source (or retrieving it, called **RAG**) beats naming the class. Asking for a reason per test makes review quick.",{"type":"p","text":"What helped less"},"Longer prose, politeness, repeating the same instruction."]}],"pitfalls":["Writing a one-line prompt: 12 marks need role, context, business rules, scenarios, avoid-list, format and a short justification.","Not covering all five ManagePrint inputs (file, printer type, material, deadline, payment).","Asking for tests but giving no business rules, so the oracle is missing.","Forgetting boundaries (deadline now, limit-1/limit/limit+1) and failure paths (payment declined)."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Role, task and class","color":0},{"label":"Language and framework","color":1},{"label":"Context and business rules","sub":"name them","color":2},{"label":"Scenarios and edge cases","color":3},{"label":"What to avoid","sub":"shallow asserts","color":4},{"label":"Quality rules and output format","color":0}]}}},{"id":"modification-types","title":"Modification request types and GenAI roles","priority":9,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q5(a) + Topic 8 (repeated slide)","marks":"9 marks (3 x 3)","summary":"**Modification requests split into Correction (corrective, preventive) and Enhancement (adaptive, perfective, additive); pick three and give two concrete GenAI contributions each.**","mnemonic":"CPAPA: Corrective, Preventive, Adaptive, Perfective, Additive","source":["CT4111_Notes_08_Maintenance","CT4111_Notes_09_Refactoring"],"sections":[{"heading":"Taxonomy (SWEBOK v4)","items":[{"type":"diagram","kind":"tree","data":{"root":{"label":"Modification request","children":[{"label":"Correction","children":[{"label":"Corrective"},{"label":"Preventive"}]},{"label":"Enhancement","children":[{"label":"Adaptive"},{"label":"Perfective"},{"label":"Additive"}]}]}},"caption":"The course focuses on corrective and preventive maintenance."},{"type":"table","head":["Type","Meaning","Two ways GenAI contributes"],"rows":[["Corrective","Fix runtime errors, security vulnerabilities, emergency patches","1) Analyse error logs and stack traces to pinpoint anomalies and root causes faster. 2) Surface similar past bugs from codebase history to speed resolution; generate a candidate patch and regression test."],["Preventive","Restructure and reduce technical debt to prevent future problems","1) Scan dependencies for outdated or vulnerable libraries. 2) Prioritise what to refactor by risk level; suggest or apply refactorings (extract method, de-duplicate) with tests as safety net."],["Adaptive","Adapt to a changing operating environment (OS, library, platform, regulation)","1) Monitor for compatibility issues before they cause problems (deprecations, API changes). 2) Scan the codebase and find all affected areas when a dependency changes; perform migration edits (e.g. library migration)."],["Perfective","Improve performance, maintainability or user experience","1) Suggest code optimisations (query rewrites, indexing strategies). 2) Generate code for common patterns and boilerplate; improve readability and documentation."],["Additive","Add major new functionality after delivery","1) Identify and prioritise candidate features from requests, usage data and environment. 2) Assess impact and assist with the necessary adaptations (generate code, tests, docs for the new feature)."]]}]},{"heading":"Answer recipe","items":["Choose three (corrective, preventive, adaptive are easiest). For each: one-line definition, then two numbered GenAI contributions with a concrete example, then (if time) the human check.","Add a safeguard sentence: tests and version control validate AI changes; human reviews impact.","**SWEBOK** is the standard guide to software engineering knowledge. A **patch** is a small fix; a **regression test** stops a fixed bug coming back; **deprecation** means a feature is marked for removal."]}],"pitfalls":["Mixing up perfective (improve existing qualities) and additive (add new functionality).","Preventive is not bug fixing: it is restructuring to prevent future problems (reduce tech debt).","Giving only one GenAI contribution per type: the question asks for two different ones."]},{"id":"fault-localisation-repair","title":"Fault localisation and repair with GenAI","priority":10,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q5(b) + Topic 8 (many slides)","marks":"10 marks","summary":"**Run a human-guided loop: humans report and triage the bug and approve the fix; GenAI structures the report, understands the repo, localises the fault, explains it, generates candidate patches, and verifies them against tests and linters, repeating until validated.**","mnemonic":"Understand, Localise, Patch, Validate, Human approves","source":["CT4111_Notes_08_Maintenance"],"sections":[{"heading":"Process diagram with roles","items":["**Fault localisation** is finding where in the code a bug is. **Repair** is fixing it.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"Fault localisation and repair loop (human vs GenAI)","directed":true,"r":24,"height":440,"nodes":[{"id":"h1","label":"Human","sub":"report, reproduce, triage","x":10,"y":10,"color":0},{"id":"g1","label":"GenAI","sub":"structure bug report","x":38,"y":10,"color":1},{"id":"g2","label":"GenAI agent","sub":"repo understanding + plan","x":66,"y":10,"color":1},{"id":"g3","label":"GenAI agent","sub":"fault localisation","x":90,"y":40,"color":1},{"id":"g4","label":"GenAI","sub":"patch generation","x":66,"y":70,"color":1},{"id":"t1","label":"Tools","sub":"tests, linters, build","x":38,"y":70,"color":2},{"id":"g5","label":"GenAI reviewer","sub":"verify vs intent","x":10,"y":70,"color":1},{"id":"h2","label":"Human","sub":"review, approve, merge","x":10,"y":40,"color":0}],"edges":[{"from":"h1","to":"g1","label":"unstructured report"},{"from":"g1","to":"g2","label":"description, steps, expected, env"},{"from":"g2","to":"g3","label":"resolution plan"},{"from":"g3","to":"g4","label":"buggy region"},{"from":"g4","to":"t1","label":"candidate patch"},{"from":"t1","to":"g4","label":"fail: errors fed back","curve":40},{"from":"t1","to":"g5","label":"pass"},{"from":"g5","to":"h2","label":"patch + explanation"},{"from":"h2","to":"h1","label":"reject: refine","curve":40}]},"caption":"Blue/purple nodes are GenAI, green is tooling; humans bracket the loop."},{"type":"p","text":"The human software engineer"},"Reports and reproduces the bug, supplies failing tests and the intent (commit message, expected behaviour), sets priorities, decides which AI output to trust, reviews and approves the **patch** (the fix), owns the outcome, and checks nothing else broke.",{"type":"p","text":"What GenAI does"},"(1) Tidies and summarises the bug report.","(2) Understands the repository (folder tree, method signatures, summaries) and writes a plan with a guess at the **root cause**.","(3) Locates the fault: searches files, classes and functions, follows dependencies, marks the buggy part.","(4) Explains the code line by line (like explaining to a rubber duck) and finds similar past fixes.","(5) Writes a patch.","(6) Checks it fixes the goal and matches what the programmer intended.",{"type":"p","text":"Ordinary tools (not AI)"},"Compiling, running tests, **linters** (style and mistake checkers) and **static analysis** (checking code without running it) give feedback. Repeat up to a limit, then pass to a human."]},{"heading":"Research approaches: SWE-GPT and PReMM (name at least two)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"SWE-GPT (process-centred)"},"Three agents: one understands the repository, one localises the fault, one writes code. Their tools include viewing the structure, searching classes and functions, and git apply and diff.","Stage 1: understand the repository and write a plan with root-cause analysis. Stage 2: search for the fault and reflect. Stage 3: generate a patch and check it with lint tools and error messages, repeating up to a limit.","Example: issue #4253 to pull request #4259 in a project called GraphScope.",{"type":"p","text":"PReMM"},"For bugs spread over **several methods**: split the problem up. The prompt includes the failing test and error, similar code from other files, the chain of calls from tests to faulty methods, and key words.",{"type":"p","text":"The fact selection problem"},"Which information matters most for repair? Mostly the error message and the issue description, but it depends on the case."]},{"heading":"Research approaches: ContrastRepair, RepairAgent, PATCH","items":[{"type":"p","text":"ContrastRepair"},"Repair by conversation, giving **contrasting pairs**: a failing test and a similar passing one, to focus the model.",{"type":"p","text":"RepairAgent"},"An autonomous agent that moves between three states:","**Understand the bug** (run tests, extract tests, run fault localisation).","**Collect information** (search the code, find similar calls, read lines, extract a method).","**Try to fix it** (write the fix, read lines).","It can state a hypothesis, discard it, collect more information, or declare the goal reached.",{"type":"p","text":"PATCH"},"Agents imitating a developer team, in four stages: bug reporting (tester), diagnosis and patch writing (developer), verification (reviewer), looping between the last two.","Extra context: commit messages, dependencies, the buggy method, similar code.","Roles include bug reporter, code explainer, pattern summariser (recalls similar fixes), patch writer and verifier. Collaboration beat a single prompt.",{"type":"p","text":"Design issues"},"A pipeline of agents: design-issue analysis (static analysis), planning, program analysis (how many things call or are called by each part: **fan-in/out**, and **coupling**, how tied together parts are), prompt building, and ranking of likely locations."]},{"heading":"Structured bug reports matter","items":["Unstructured reports mix up the description, steps and environment.","GenAI (a method called BRMDS) pulls out separate fields: environment, expected behaviour, actual behaviour, bug category and suggested solution, each with its own instruction (such as \"in no more than 15 words\").","Structured reports make finding the bug faster and later AI steps more accurate."]},{"heading":"Cautions about AI repair","items":["The model may **hallucinate** (invent) a fix or break other behaviour.","A patch that passes weak tests may still be wrong.","Keep tests as the judge of correctness (the oracle) and have a human review.","Do not touch highly **coupled** (interconnected) code without extra tests."]},{"heading":"Cautions","items":["Models may hallucinate fixes or break other behaviour; patches that pass weak tests may not be correct; keep tests as the oracle and human review; avoid touching highly coupled code without extra tests."]}],"pitfalls":["Drawing the loop without labelling who is human and who is GenAI: the question asks you to identify both.","Leaving out validation feedback (tests/lint) and the repeat-until-valid loop.","Omitting localisation (the question is localisation AND repair)."]},{"id":"library-migration","title":"Library migration at scale with GenAI","priority":11,"tier":"likely","freq":"sample paper Q5(c) + Topic 9","marks":"6 marks","summary":"**Large codebases age unevenly; migrations (e.g. JUnit 3 to 4, Joda-Time to java.time) are slow manual work, so split them into discrete steps: find locations, transform each file with a detailed role-and-rules prompt, validate with traditional tools and tests, and release in paced batches for human review.**","mnemonic":"Find, Transform, Validate, Review, Pace","source":["CT4111_Notes_09_Refactoring"],"sections":[{"heading":"Why and what","items":["A **library** is code written by others that you reuse. **Library migration** means moving your code from one library (or version) to another.","Libraries age: parts of a system fall behind with old versions, outdated interfaces and **deprecated** (due for removal) frameworks.","Google's **one-version policy**: every dependency is kept up to date across the whole code base, to limit drifting apart.","Updating cannot be fully automated, takes much manual work and often months or years, by which time it must be done again.","GenAI makes rapid large-scale updates practical (Google internal case study, Nikolov 2025)."]},{"heading":"Example answer: Joda-Time to java.time in a 5000-file Java codebase","items":["**Joda-Time** is an old date and time library; **java.time** is the one now built into Java.","**Task**: replace every use of `org.joda.time` classes (Instant, Duration, DateTime) with java.time equivalents, fix imports and keep behaviour identical, across thousands of files.",{"type":"p","text":"Pipeline"},"(1) **Discovery**: code search or static analysis lists the affected files and places.","(2) A **deterministic** tool (same input always gives the same output) does the trivial rewrites.","(3) The LLM transforms each file using the prompt below.","(4) **Validate**: compile, run the file's tests and linters, and check the changes for anything unrelated (stray comments, reformatting).","(5) Failed files are retried or given to a human.","(6) Small batches become pull requests, paced so reviewers are not swamped.","(7) Improve the prompt from the failures.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"Find affected files","sub":"search / static analysis"},{"label":"LLM transforms file","sub":"detailed migration prompt"},{"label":"Validate","sub":"build, tests, lint, diff checks"},{"label":"Human review","sub":"small paced PRs"}],"direction":"row","loopBack":"failures: fix prompt, retry"},"caption":"Library migration pipeline."}]},{"heading":"The migration prompt","items":[{"type":"code","text":"You are a senior Java engineer, expert in Joda-Time and java.time. Your task is to remove all usage of org.joda.time from the provided files and replace it with java.time equivalents, preserving behaviour exactly.\n\nRules:\n1. Replace joda.time.Instant with java.time.Instant. new Instant(Long.MAX_VALUE) becomes Instant.MAX; new Instant(0) becomes Instant.EPOCH; the millisecond constructor becomes Instant.ofEpochMilli(); getMillis() becomes toEpochMilli().\n2. Replace joda.time.Duration with java.time.Duration.\n3. Do not call Instant.now(); use our TimeSource.system().now().\n4. Remove all org.joda.time imports and add the needed java.time imports.\n5. Do NOT change anything unrelated: no renaming, no reformatting, no comment edits, no new logging.\n6. If a construct has no safe equivalent, leave it unchanged and add a TODO(joda) comment.\n\nOutput: only the full migrated file, followed by a list of any TODO(joda) items.\n\nFiles: [paste files]"},"It works because it gives the AI a role, explicit mapping rules, limits against stray edits and a fixed output format.","Prompts are kept in version control like code. A human writes the rules after migrating a sample by hand."]},{"heading":"Lessons (Google)","items":["Split migrations into discrete specialised steps (more reliable, easier to debug). Prefer traditional validation to catch model quirks (stray comments, unwanted edits); traditional steps are cheaper when touching thousands of files.","Pace migrations so review teams are not overwhelmed; build tooling for peripheral tasks (review triage, coordination).","**Triage** means sorting by urgency. **JUnit** is Java's testing library; version 4 uses annotations (labels starting with @) instead of inheriting from `TestCase`.","Second example prompt from the lecture: JUnit 3 to JUnit 4: remove junit.framework imports, add import static org.junit.Assert.*, drop the TestCase base class, replace with annotations."]}],"pitfalls":["Giving only 'ask ChatGPT to migrate': the answer needs the task details, pipeline with validation, and an actual prompt.","No validation and no human review step for the migrated code.","Vague prompt: mapping rules and 'do not change anything else' are the key."]},{"id":"static-analysis-agents","title":"Agentic classification and repair of static-analysis warnings","priority":12,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q6(a) + bonus Q(b) + Topic 9","marks":"9 + 5 marks","summary":"**A CodeCureAgent-style pipeline uses agents to (1) classify each warning as true or false positive using code-reading tools, (2) plan and generate a minimal fix, (3) validate with the analyser, compiler and tests, (4) rank or review the patch, and (5) hand it to a human; GenAI is needed because rule-based tools give many false positives, no context and no fixes.**","mnemonic":"Warn, Classify, Plan, Fix, Validate, Rank, Review","source":["CT4111_Notes_09_Refactoring","CT4111_Notes_08_Maintenance"],"sections":[{"heading":"Agentic approach and roles (diagram)","items":["**Static analysis** tools check code without running it and raise **warnings**. A **true positive** (TP) is a real problem; a **false positive** (FP) is a false alarm.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"Agents for static-analysis warning classification and repair","directed":true,"r":24,"height":480,"nodes":[{"id":"sa","label":"Static analyser","sub":"SonarQube / Pylint warnings","x":8,"y":10,"color":2},{"id":"cl","label":"Classifier agent","sub":"true or false positive?","x":38,"y":10,"color":1},{"id":"tl","label":"Tools","sub":"read code/docs, find refs, search","x":38,"y":42,"color":2},{"id":"fp","label":"False positive","sub":"suppress with justification","x":70,"y":10,"color":3},{"id":"pl","label":"Planner agent","sub":"fix strategy, minimal change","x":70,"y":42,"color":1},{"id":"fx","label":"Fixer agent","sub":"generate patch","x":70,"y":74,"color":1},{"id":"va","label":"Validation","sub":"compile, tests, re-run analyser","x":38,"y":74,"color":2},{"id":"rk","label":"Ranker / reviewer agent","sub":"score patches 0-3","x":10,"y":60,"color":1},{"id":"hu","label":"Human","sub":"approve PR or suppression","x":10,"y":88,"color":0}],"edges":[{"from":"sa","to":"cl","label":"warning"},{"from":"cl","to":"tl","label":"investigate"},{"from":"tl","to":"cl","label":"evidence","curve":40},{"from":"cl","to":"fp","label":"FP"},{"from":"cl","to":"pl","label":"TP"},{"from":"pl","to":"fx","label":"plan"},{"from":"fx","to":"va","label":"patch"},{"from":"va","to":"fx","label":"fail: feedback","curve":40},{"from":"va","to":"rk","label":"pass"},{"from":"rk","to":"hu","label":"best patch"},{"from":"fp","to":"hu","label":"confirm","curve":35}]},"caption":"Each agent keeps a history of previous actions and outcomes as memory."},{"type":"p","text":"Lecture structure (CodeCureAgent, Joos 2026)"},"(1) Run static analysis on the project.","(2) A classification agent (an LLM with tools) decides TP or FP.","(3) A repair agent proposes a fix.","(4) A change approver runs the build, the analysis and the tests. Rejected goes back to repair; approved is a plausible fix.","Tools available: read docs and code, find references and definitions, search patterns, propose a fix or **suppression** (silencing the warning with a reason), plan, classify, complete.","The separate planner, fixer and ranker boxes in the diagram combine CodeCureAgent with another system, CORE."]},{"heading":"The roles, and the CORE method","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Roles"},"**Classifier**: TP or FP, using tools. Its main risks are making things up and shallow context.","**Planner and fixer**: propose the smallest fix, or a justified suppression, one warning at a time and without creating new ones.","**Validation**: compile, test and re-run the analyser to confirm the warning has gone and none are new. Failures loop back with error summaries, a limited number of times.","**Ranker or reviewer**: a second LLM scores patches 0 to 3 (0 = strong reject for unrelated changes, 3 = strong accept: only edits that fix the warning with least impact). Deleting comments is allowed; adding print statements or renaming variables is not.","**Human**: gives final approval of the pull request or suppression, supplies project conventions and sets policy.",{"type":"p","text":"CORE (Wadhwa 2024), 5 steps"},"(1) Set up static tools in the build pipeline. (2) Process flagged files and the documentation. (3) A **proposer** LLM writes candidate fixes. (4) Discard those still flagged. (5) A **ranker** LLM scores the rest (strong reject, weak reject, weak accept, strong accept) for the developers."]},{"heading":"Prompt components for an agent (CodeCureAgent)","items":["**Role and objectives**: for example, decide TP or FP.","**Constraints**: do not ask the user for help, create no new warnings, one warning at a time.","**Available tools** and how to call them.","**Input warning**: such as \"class variable fields should not have public accessibility\".","**Agent history**: previous actions and results (its memory, which stops repetition).","**Response format**: JSON (structured text a program can read)."]},{"heading":"Related approaches and failure modes","items":["A **code smell** is a warning sign of poor design.","**iSMELL**: one LLM chooses which specialist tools to run for a smell; their results, the smell's definition and refactoring examples go to the LLM, which then refactors.","**CORE**: proposes fixes, then a reviewer LLM ranks them, with static analysis filtering.",{"type":"p","text":"Why static analysis alone struggles"},"False positives and low-priority warnings.","Fixing needs information from elsewhere in the code, and may affect many files.","Fixes can introduce new problems.","Different tools disagree.",{"type":"p","text":"Failure modes of the agents"},"Wrong classification (inventing, or ignoring context).","Too little context (poor descriptions of the warning).","Unusual cases and clashing project conventions.","Bad fixes: incorrect changes, fixing several warnings at once, or being unable to create, rename or delete files."]},{"heading":"Is GenAI needed for static analysis? (bonus part, 5 marks)","items":["**Yes, as a complement, not a replacement.**",{"type":"p","text":"Traditional tools (SonarQube, ESLint, Pylint, PMD)"},"They are deterministic, fast and cheap.","But they follow fixed rules with many false positives, see no whole-project context, cannot judge soft rules or intent, disagree with each other, and mostly cannot fix problems.",{"type":"p","text":"What GenAI adds"},"Sorting warnings by importance, judging true or false using context, plain-language explanations, recognising the same idea written different ways (smells, near-copies), enforcing project guidelines, and automatic repair.",{"type":"p","text":"Limits"},"Not deterministic, makes things up, costs money, limited context, may miss real bugs.","So use a **hybrid**: traditional tools detect and verify; GenAI interprets, ranks and repairs; a human approves. Traditional checking is also cheaper at scale."]}],"pitfalls":["Diagram with one box 'AI fixes warnings': the question wants different agents with distinct roles (classify, plan, fix, validate, rank) and a human.","Forgetting classification (false positives): the pipeline is classify THEN repair.","Answering 'no need' for GenAI without acknowledging false positives and missing repair."]},{"id":"bitter-lesson","title":"Sutton's bitter lesson","priority":13,"tier":"likely","freq":"sample paper Q6(b) + Topic 11 (3 article decks)","marks":"8 marks","summary":"**Sutton (2019): across 70 years of AI, general methods that leverage computation (search and learning) beat approaches that build in human knowledge; it is 'bitter' because it defeats the favoured human-centric approach; in SE with GenAI it predicts that hand-built scaffolding (prompt chains, retrieval, agent org charts) will be swept away by better models, while specifying and verifying remain.**","mnemonic":"Compute beats cleverness: Search + Learning scale","source":["CT4111_Notes_11c_Article_Bitter_Lesson","CT4111_Notes_11_Emerging_Topics","CT4111_Notes_11d_Article_Dot_and_Swarm"],"sections":[{"heading":"What the bitter lesson is (3-4 marks)","items":["The **bitter lesson** is an essay by Rich Sutton about how AI research progresses.","**Claim**: \"general methods that leverage computation are ultimately the most effective, and by a large margin.\" The cause is that computing keeps getting exponentially cheaper (**Moore's law**, broadly meant).",{"type":"p","text":"The pattern"},"Researchers act as if computing power were fixed, so they build in human knowledge. This helps at first and feels satisfying.","A few years later far more computing arrives, and the knowledge-based approach stalls or even gets in the way.","The breakthrough comes from **scaling computation**: **search** (trying many possibilities) and **learning** (from data)."]},{"heading":"The bitter lesson: examples and conclusion","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Domain","Human-knowledge approach","What succeeded"],"rows":[["Chess (1997)","Human understanding of chess structure","Massive deep search (beat Kasparov)"],["Go (about 20 years later)","Human knowledge used to avoid search","Search at scale plus learning by self-play"],["Speech (1970s on)","Words, phonemes, vocal tract knowledge","Statistical models (HMMs), then deep learning"],["Computer vision","Edges, generalised cylinders, SIFT","Deep convolutional networks"]]},"Words in the table: **self-play** = learning by playing against itself; **phonemes** = speech sounds; **HMMs** = hidden Markov models, a statistical method; **SIFT** and generalised cylinders = hand-designed ways of describing images.","**Why \"bitter\"**: the winner beats a favoured human-like approach, so the lesson is often not accepted. Researchers complained that brute force \"is not how people play chess\".","**Conclusion**: use general methods that keep improving with computing. Do not build in how we think we think. Build only methods that can find structure themselves.","Author: Rich Sutton, a founder of modern **reinforcement learning** (learning from rewards), co-author of its textbook, Turing Award 2024 with Barto."]},{"heading":"Critiques of the bitter lesson","items":["**Rodney Brooks (\"A Better Lesson\")**: human ingenuity moved rather than disappeared. People still design the networks (for example **CNNs**, image networks built to recognise a thing wherever it appears). Training costs shut out most researchers, and Moore's law is slowing.","**Dietterich**: the best systems combine built-in knowledge with brute force. The challenge is knowledge that rules out wrong answers but not right ones.","**Sutton himself (2025)** doubts that LLMs follow his lesson: they learn from human data rather than experience, so they are another human-knowledge approach that will be overtaken.","**Karpathy**: LLMs are \"ghosts, not animals\" (human data is limited and biased), but in practice they do improve with computing."]},{"heading":"Does it apply to SE with GenAI tools? Give two reasons (opinion answer)","items":["**Stance: largely yes, with limits.**",{"type":"p","text":"Reason 1: hand-built scaffolding is being replaced"},"\"Scaffolding\" means the extra machinery built around a model: retrieval systems, prompt templates and chains, planning prompts, arrangements of multiple agents.","Newer models search code themselves (a simple text search tool instead of elaborate retrieval), plan their own steps and start helper agents. Mollick calls it \"the bitter lesson applied to the org chart\".","Elaborate set-ups and **prompt debt** may be thrown away at each model upgrade.",{"type":"p","text":"Reason 2: code can be checked automatically"},"Tests and compilers say whether an answer works, which gives a **reward signal**. So training and parallel search at huge scale (a 10,000-agent swarm) improve ability faster than human-written rules, as in chess and Go.",{"type":"p","text":"Caveat"},"Humans still decide what to build, check it with tests and review, and are accountable.","Guardrails, version control, tests and legal compliance are lasting engineering, not scaffolding. Review at scale and cost still need design.","An engineer delivering this year should invest in the lasting parts (tests, specifications, decision records, verification) and keep fragile prompt tricks cheap and disposable.",{"type":"p","text":"If you argue \"no\""},"(1) Much software work cannot be checked automatically (requirements, design taste, ethics).","(2) Regulation, accountability and shared understanding need humans and processes."]},{"heading":"Discussion points from the lecture","items":["Is it a law, or just a pattern seen in four examples? What would count as a counter-example?","Where is human ingenuity in today's coding agents?","Which practices from the course will a better model make unnecessary, and which will last?","How should an engineer who must deliver this year act on a long-run claim?"]}],"pitfalls":["Defining it as 'bigger models are better' without 'general methods vs built-in human knowledge' and 'search and learning'.","Giving an opinion with no reasons or more or fewer than the number asked (two).","Not naming at least two of Sutton's four examples (chess, Go, speech, vision)."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"The lesson","items":["General methods that use computation win"]},{"title":"Those methods","items":["Search","Learning"]},{"title":"In SE","items":["Hand-built scaffolding gets swept away","Specifying and verifying remain"]}]}}},{"id":"legal-risks","title":"Legal risks: IP and licensing, GDPR, liability, EU AI Act","priority":14,"tier":"core","freq":"sample paper Q6(c) + Topic 10 + lab","marks":"8 marks (2 x 4)","summary":"**For a startup using GenAI to build loan-approval software, key legal risks are copyright/licence contamination of generated code, GDPR (personal data in prompts and in the system, automated decisions), EU AI Act high-risk obligations for credit scoring, and liability for harmful outputs; each needs a concrete mitigation.**","mnemonic":"IP, GDPR, AI Act, Liability: each risk gets a mitigation","source":["CT4111_Notes_10_Ethical_Legal","CT4111_Notes_10b_Lab"],"sections":[{"heading":"How to answer 'two legal risks, each with a mitigation' (8 marks)","items":["For each give: the **risk name**, **how it arises in this startup** (one specific sentence), the **consequence**, and the **mitigation** (what reduces the risk: 2 or 3 concrete actions). Two well-developed risks beat four thin ones."]},{"heading":"Risk 1: copyright and licensing (IP)","items":["**IP** (intellectual property) is the legal ownership of created work. A **licence** says what others may do with it.",{"type":"p","text":"How it arises"},"The LLM may reproduce memorised code identical to licensed code, without saying where it came from.","**Copyleft** licences (GPL, AGPL) require software built on them to share its source code too. Copying such code into the private loan engine (\"GPL contamination\") could force you to publish your source, or bring infringement claims.","Who owns AI-written code is legally unclear: a model is not a legal person, and there is no copyright in content you did not write. Providers such as OpenAI say you are responsible.",{"type":"p","text":"Mitigation"},"Scan for licences and similar code (**software composition analysis**, code-match filters).","Review any substantial generated functions.","Keep an **SBOM** (a list of all components), including AI-generated ones.","Use business tools that offer **IP indemnity** (the provider covers legal claims).","Replace flagged code. Rewriting it cosmetically does not remove the problem: write it independently without looking (**clean room**) or use a properly licensed alternative.","Record AI use in commit messages."]},{"heading":"Risk 2: data protection (GDPR)","items":["**GDPR** is the EU law protecting personal data.",{"type":"p","text":"How it arises"},"Developers paste customer data or private code into outside LLMs (**prompt leakage**, as happened at Samsung).","Generated code may collect or log more personal data than needed, breaking **data minimisation**, or make fully automated credit decisions, which is high-risk processing needing a **DPIA** (a formal assessment of data protection impact).","Applicants' financial data is highly sensitive, regulated in Ireland by the Data Protection Commission.",{"type":"p","text":"Mitigation"},"Do a DPIA before launch.","Use a private LLM or one that keeps nothing, and a filter (**proxy**) that removes personally identifying information (**PII**).","No real customer data in prompts.","Review what generated code collects and how long it keeps it.","Document the lawful reason for processing, and people's rights to an explanation and human review. Get the Data Protection Officer's sign-off."]},{"heading":"Risk 3: EU AI Act, and risk 4: liability","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Risk 3: EU AI Act (high risk)"},"The **EU AI Act** regulates AI by risk level. Credit scoring is **high risk**, so it needs risk management, good-quality data, technical documentation, logging, transparency, human oversight, accuracy and cybersecurity (Articles 8-17), with fines for breaking it.","Mitigation: classify the system early; keep documentation and decision logs linking input, version and rule; test for bias; give humans a way to override and suspend; do a **conformity assessment** (formal check that it meets the Act).",{"type":"p","text":"Risk 4: liability and accountability"},"Harm from wrong, biased or insecure decisions (discriminatory loan refusals, an attack through an invented dependency) is the company's responsibility. \"The AI did it\" is no defence (Moffatt v Air Canada, Mata v Avianca).","Mitigation: human review, tests, security scanning, checking dependencies are genuine, clear internal accountability, contracts and insurance, and an **audit trail** (a record of who did what)."]},{"heading":"Intellectual property and licences","items":["**IP** means legal rights protecting creations of the mind: patents (inventions), copyright (written and creative work, including code), trademarks (brand names and logos) and trade secrets. Copyright is automatic; patents must be registered.","A **permissive** licence lets you do almost anything. **Copyleft** requires what you build from it to stay under the same terms. A **derivative work** is one built from another.",{"type":"table","head":["Licence","Type","Key point"],"rows":[["MIT","Permissive","Use, modify, sell, even closed-source, if copyright notice kept"],["Apache 2.0","Permissive","As MIT plus explicit patent grant; terminates if you sue for patent infringement"],["GPL","Strong copyleft","Derivative works must also be released under GPL; effectively blocks closed-source reuse (AGPL extends to network use)"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Two problems with AI training and output"},"(1) The model may have been trained on copyrighted material without permission (a legal problem for the provider, an ethical one for you).","(2) It may memorise and reproduce code identical to protected software without credit (a legal problem for **you**).","Real case: in October 2022 a **class action** (a lawsuit on behalf of many people) was brought against GitHub Copilot for training on public code under 11 open-source licences that require credit. It was not closed as of 2026."]},{"heading":"GDPR (EU General Data Protection Regulation)","items":["Passed 2016, in force 2018. It applies whenever personal data of people in the EU is processed.","**Personal data** is anything that can identify a person directly or indirectly: names, emails, IP addresses, cookie IDs, location, purchase history, profiles. It is broader than the term PII.",{"type":"p","text":"Special category data (high risk)"},"Health, biometric and genetic data, sexual orientation, political, religious or philosophical beliefs, race or ethnic origin, trade union membership.","Extra protection also applies to systematic monitoring, **automated decisions with legal or similar effects**, children's data, and data that would cause harm if leaked.","A **DPIA** (Data Protection Impact Assessment) is required where high risk is likely, usually done by the data protection officer.",{"type":"p","text":"The principles"},"The **data subject** is the person the data is about; the **controller** is whoever decides how it is used.",{"type":"table","head":["Principle","Meaning"],"rows":[["Lawfulness, fairness, transparency","Lawful, fair, transparent to the data subject"],["Purpose limitation","Use data only for the stated legitimate purposes"],["Data minimisation","Collect only what is necessary"],["Accuracy","Keep data accurate and up to date"],["Storage limitation","Keep identifying data only as long as necessary"],["Integrity and confidentiality","Appropriate security (e.g. encryption)"],["Accountability","Controller must demonstrate compliance"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Link to GenAI"},"AI-written code you do not understand may collect and process personal data you do not need, breaking data minimisation. A US-influenced model may add tracking cookies without the consent pop-up the EU requires."]},{"heading":"EU AI Act (passed April 2024, fully effective August 2026)","items":["It regulates AI systems according to risk.","An **AI system** is a machine-based system working with some autonomy, possibly adapting, that works out from its input how to produce outputs affecting its surroundings.","It need not use machine learning: rule-based code can count. Using GenAI to **write** software does not by itself make that software an AI system.",{"type":"table","head":["Tier","Examples","Duty"],"rows":[["Unacceptable (prohibited)","Emotion recognition in education","Banned"],["High risk","Hiring/CV filtering, credit scoring, medical devices, critical infrastructure, admissions or grading","Full compliance (Articles 8-17): technical docs, logging, bias testing, human oversight"],["Transparency / limited risk","Chatbots, deepfakes, AI-generated content","Disclose that it is AI (Article 50); label generated content"],["Minimal risk","Spam filters, video game AI","No special duties beyond general law (GDPR)"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Other duties"},"Organisations using AI must help staff become **AI literate** (understand what they use).","**General-purpose model** providers must keep technical documentation (including energy use), give information to those building on the model, have a copyright policy respecting opt-outs from text and data mining, and publish a summary of training content.","The biggest, **systemic-risk** models (about 10^25 FLOPs, a measure of training computation) must also do adversarial testing (deliberately attacking them), report incidents and ensure cybersecurity.","If you build a high-risk system on top of such a model, the high-risk duties fall on **you**."]}],"pitfalls":["Naming risks without a mitigation (each of the two needs one) or with a generic mitigation ('be careful').","Not tying the risk to the loan-approval context (credit scoring is EU AI Act high-risk; financial personal data).","Saying the AI vendor is liable: legally ambiguous and the vendor usually disclaims it.","Confusing legal with ethical: not everything ethical is legal and vice versa."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Copyright and licences","items":["Contaminated generated code"]},{"title":"GDPR","items":["Personal data","Automated decisions"]},{"title":"EU AI Act","items":["Credit scoring is high-risk"]},{"title":"Liability","items":["Harmful outputs"]}]}}},{"id":"rag-context","title":"Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) and context management","priority":15,"tier":"likely","freq":"sample paper Q3(c) + Topics 1, 3, 5","marks":"5 marks","summary":"**RAG gives an LLM relevant external knowledge at query time: documents are chunked and embedded into a vector store at setup, the question is embedded, nearest chunks are retrieved and pasted into the prompt, then the LLM generates a grounded answer; goals are fewer hallucinations, up-to-date and private knowledge, and fitting a finite context window.**","mnemonic":"Chunk, Embed, Store; then Embed query, Retrieve, Paste, Generate","source":["CT4111_Notes_03_Prompting_and_Context","CT4111_Notes_01a_Overview","CT4111_Notes_05_Design"],"sections":[{"heading":"The problem RAG solves","items":["An LLM only \"knows\" what was learned in training (stored in its **weights**) plus what we put in its **context window** (the text it can see right now). Everything else, such as your code, documents and new libraries, must be provided.",{"type":"p","text":"The naive approach"},"Paste in the whole file, all function documentation and the task. This breaks as code grows:","Context windows are limited.","Content in the middle gets forgotten (models favour the start and end: **primacy and recency bias**).","Both too much and too little information cause **hallucination** (made-up answers).","The code base changes, so the context goes out of date."]},{"heading":"How RAG works (goals and workings)","items":["**RAG** (retrieval augmented generation): first **retrieve** the relevant pieces of your own material, then give them to the model to generate its answer.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"Chunk documents","sub":"setup time"},{"label":"Embed chunks","sub":"small model gives vectors"},{"label":"Store in vector DB","sub":"reused at runtime"},{"label":"Embed the question","sub":"query time","color":1},{"label":"Retrieve nearest chunks","sub":"embedding similarity","color":1},{"label":"Paste into prompt","sub":"context window","color":1},{"label":"LLM generates answer","sub":"grounded in chunks","color":2}],"direction":"row"},"caption":"RAG pipeline: set-up (first three) then each query (last four)."},"**Embeddings** are lists of numbers representing the meaning of text. Comparing them (for example by **cosine similarity**) finds relevant chunks even without matching words.",{"type":"p","text":"Goals"},"Base answers on current, private or specialist data.","Reduce made-up answers.","Fit only relevant content into the limited context window.","Avoid retraining the model.",{"type":"p","text":"In practice"},"Keep the searchable material up to date (**re-indexing** it is easy with libraries).","RAG is like a \"Google search over your codebase\": the AI decides to search and the results enter its context.","Consumer chatbots often just use web search as a tool instead, with no embeddings.","The Cursor editor indexes your project with embeddings; Claude Code often looks things up with **grep** (plain text search)."]},{"heading":"Context management patterns","items":["**Context as prompt (naive)**: include everything. Breaks at scale.","**Context on request**: the model asks for what it needs, for example by issuing a LOOKUP **tool call**; the program fetches the functions and their descriptions and adds them. The model decides when to ask.","**RAG**: retrieve only the relevant chunks.","**Graph RAG**: retrieve from a **knowledge graph**, a network of things (requirements, decision records, components, incidents) and the links between them, stored as triples (subject, relation, object). It can answer \"what did we reject before?\".",{"type":"p","text":"Keeping context clean"},"Outdated documents cause hallucinations.","Long chats cost more and may be automatically summarised.","Write notes (such as `TODO.md`) and start a new session.","Prefer plain text (`.md` over `.docx`).","Supply documentation for unusual libraries (for example with a tool called context7).","Good results need context that is **complete, correct and well presented**."]}],"pitfalls":["Describing RAG as 'the model searches the internet': the answer needs retrieval of relevant chunks and insertion into the prompt, with embeddings and a vector store.","Omitting the goal (less hallucination, current data, limited context).","Saying RAG retrains the model: it does not change weights."]},{"id":"programmatic-prompts","title":"Programmatic prompts: LLM as an API","priority":16,"tier":"likely","freq":"sample paper Q2(b) + Topic 3","marks":"3 marks","summary":"**A programmatic prompt is one built by a program and consumed by a program: your code assembles a prompt, calls the LLM through an API, and parses the structured output into the next step, e.g. automatic bug-report classification in a CI script.**","mnemonic":"Program in, program out","source":["CT4111_Notes_03_Prompting_and_Context","CT4111_Notes_04_Requirements_Engineering"],"sections":[{"heading":"Three workflows and who creates / consumes the prompt","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Setting","Prompt and context created by","Output consumed by"],"rows":[["Standalone chatbot (chat UI)","Human","Human"],["Chatbot integrated with codebase (IDE prompt)","Human (plus tool-added context)","Human"],["Programmatic prompt (LLM as an API)","Program","Program"]]},"Example from lecture: using the IDE interactively is human/human; 'our team's scripts for bug classification' is program/program."]},{"heading":"Scenario answer (3 marks)","items":["**Scenario:** a team's CI script receives each new bug report, builds a prompt from a template (instruction, project context, the report text, a few labelled examples, output format 'respond only with JSON {severity, component, duplicate_of}'), calls the LLM API, validates and parses the JSON, then sets labels and assignee in the issue tracker; low-confidence or invalid results go to a human.","**Why it fits:** repetitive, high-volume, well-defined text task; no human in the loop for each call; the LLM replaces training a custom classifier (new task = new prompt).","**Other scenarios:** NFR classification of requirements documents; extracting API endpoints into JSON; generating commit messages or release notes; summarising logs; PII redaction; test-data generation; code-review comments on every PR; migrating thousands of files with a fixed prompt (library migration).",{"type":"p","text":"Design points"},"State the output format (such as JSON, a structured text format) and check it, retrying if wrong, because nothing guarantees compliance.","Use system prompts and templates (a **system prompt** sets standing instructions) and **few-shot** examples (a few worked examples in the prompt).","Use a low **temperature** (less randomness) for consistency.","Handle cost, delay and request limits, protect private data, and version and test prompts like code."]}],"pitfalls":["Giving a chatbot scenario: the point is that no human types the prompt or reads the raw output."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Your code builds the prompt","color":0},{"label":"Calls the LLM API","color":1},{"label":"LLM returns structured output","color":2},{"label":"Code parses it","color":3},{"label":"Next step uses it","color":4}]}}},{"id":"mutation-testing","title":"Mutation testing and GenAI","priority":17,"tier":"likely","freq":"sample paper bonus Q(a) + Topic 7","marks":"6 marks","summary":"**Mutation testing injects small deliberate faults (mutants) into the code and re-runs the unit tests: killed mutants show the tests detect faults, surviving mutants reveal weak tests; GenAI helps by generating realistic mutants, spotting equivalent ones and writing new tests that kill survivors.**","mnemonic":"Mutate, Run, Kill or Survive","source":["CT4111_Notes_07_Testing"],"sections":[{"heading":"How mutation testing fits unit testing","items":["Unit tests show whether code works. Coverage shows what was run. **Mutation testing asks how good the tests themselves are.** It is a **white-box** technique (it uses the source code).","A **mutant** is a copy of your code with one small deliberate bug.","Coverage levels: class, method, line, **branch** (each outcome of a decision), **path** (each route through). Example given: 6 branches, 8 paths.",{"type":"p","text":"Process"},"(1) Change the source to make a mutant (change `i++` to `i--`, flip a comparison, remove a statement).","(2) Run the unit tests.","(3) If no test fails, the tests may be weak, or a new one is needed.","(4) Repeat with a different change.",{"type":"p","text":"Outcomes"},"**Killed**: a test fails (good).","**Surviving**: all tests pass (possibly bad: behaviour nobody checks).","**Equivalent**: the change does not alter behaviour, so it cannot be detected and is ignored."]},{"heading":"Mutation ratio and worked example","items":[{"type":"formula","label":"Mutation ratio (the slide's name; also called mutation score)","text":"Mutation Ratio = #Killed / (#Total - #Equivalent)","math":["\\text{Mutation Ratio} = \\frac{\\#\\text{Killed}}{\\#\\text{Total} - \\#\\text{Equivalent}}"],"explain":["Small deliberate bugs (mutants) are planted in the code. A mutant is \"killed\" when a test fails because of it.","The score is the share of bugs your tests noticed.","Equivalent mutants behave exactly like the original, so no test could catch them and they are left out."],"example":["20 mutants, 3 equivalent, 14 killed.",{"tex":"\\var{ratio} = \\frac{14}{20 - 3} = 0.82"}]},{"type":"formula","label":"Related slide formulas","text":"Test Pass Rate = #Passed tests / #Total tests (slide example 25 / 26 = about 0.96). Unit-level coverage = #Units tested at least once / #Total units.","math":["\\text{Test Pass Rate} = \\frac{\\#\\text{Passed tests}}{\\#\\text{Total tests}}","\\text{Unit-level coverage} = \\frac{\\#\\text{Units tested at least once}}{\\#\\text{Total units}}"],"where":"Slide example: pass rate 25 / 26 = about 0.96.","explain":["Pass rate: the share of tests that pass. Coverage: the share of units that any test touches.","Neither shows the tests are good: a test can run code and check nothing."],"example":["25 of 26 tests pass (0.96), and 8 of 10 units are tested (0.8)."]},"Worked example: 20 mutants, 3 equivalent, 14 killed, so 3 survive.","Score = 14 / (20 - 3) = 14 / 17 = **82.4%**.","The 3 survivors point to missing tests."]},{"heading":"Typical GenAI support","items":["**Generate meaningful mutants:** LLM reads the code and proposes realistic, context-aware faults (off-by-one, wrong boundary, missing null check, wrong business rule) instead of only syntactic operators.","An **off-by-one** error is being out by one at a boundary. **Syntactic operators** are mechanical changes such as swapping + for -.","**Triage equivalent mutants:** LLM judges whether a surviving mutant is behaviourally equivalent (expensive to do by hand).","**Close the gap (typical loop):** run mutation tool, feed the surviving mutants plus the code and existing tests to the LLM, ask for new tests that kill them, re-run, repeat until score target met; human reviews assertions.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"Code + tests","sub":"baseline green"},{"label":"Generate mutants","sub":"tool or LLM"},{"label":"Run tests","sub":"killed / survived"},{"label":"LLM writes tests for survivors","sub":"human reviews"},{"label":"Re-run, new score","sub":"stop at target"}],"direction":"row","loopBack":"survivors remain"},"caption":"GenAI-supported mutation testing loop."},"Value: checks that AI-generated tests are not shallow (AI tests can pass without proving correctness)."]}],"pitfalls":["Describing mutation testing as testing the application; it tests the quality of the tests.","Forgetting equivalent mutants (cannot be killed).","Giving only the process and no GenAI role (6 marks need both parts)."]},{"id":"bias-fairness","title":"Bias, stereotypes and fairness","priority":18,"tier":"likely","freq":"sample paper bonus Q(c) + Topic 10","marks":"8 marks","summary":"**GenAI reflects and amplifies biases in its training data (stereotypes, representation, historical bias, proxies), including in generated code; hosting an open-weights model locally gives control and privacy but does not remove bias, because the weights embody the same training-data biases.**","mnemonic":"Data in, bias out; local hosting moves control, not the bias","source":["CT4111_Notes_10_Ethical_Legal","CT4111_Notes_10b_Lab"],"sections":[{"heading":"What 'biases and stereotypes' means","items":["LLMs are not built to be truthful. They produce likely-sounding text learned from very mixed web data (forums, social media), so they reflect gender, cultural and social biases.","**Bias**: a consistent unfair lean. **Stereotype**: the model attaches fixed traits or roles to a group.","Companies cover this up with **prompt rewriting** (changing your request behind the scenes), system prompts and **preference tuning** (training on what people rate well), so the biases have \"moved, not disappeared\"."]},{"heading":"Examples to quote","items":["**Sora video** (WIRED 2025): 25 prompts repeated 10 times; CEOs and professors were always men; flight attendants, childcare workers and receptionists always women.","**\"Newer is not fairer\"**: across four versions of the image generator Stable Diffusion, 76% of images were male; \"scientist\" came out 1-18% female, against 48% of US scientists.",{"type":"p","text":"Your own observed example (the exam wants one)"},"For example, \"a nurse and a surgeon\" returns a female nurse and a male surgeon. Or \"write code for a registration form\" demands both first name and family name, which fails people with only one name.","**How it arose**: training data over-represents stereotypes; the model picks the most likely association; preference tuning and western-centred code reinforce it.",{"type":"p","text":"Decisions about people"},"UK police facial recognition: false matches about 0.04% for white people, 5.5% for Black people and near 10% for Black women, at a 0.8 **threshold** (how sure it must be to report a match).","A Rotterdam welfare algorithm selected women 1.25 times more often, and non-Dutch speakers about twice as often."]},{"heading":"Sources and types of bias","items":["**Representation bias**: some groups are scarce in the data.","**Historical or societal bias**: data reflects past inequality (a hiring AI favours men).","**Proxy variable**: a neutral-looking value that goes with a protected characteristic (a postcode standing in for race).","**Aggregation bias**: one model used for groups that differ.","**Cultural blind spots in code**: first and last name fields, meanings of colours, assuming English and left-to-right text, assuming good devices and internet (Ryanair's app-only boarding passes shut out people without smartphones).","**Team bias**: lack of lived experience, favouring people like yourself (**affinity**), deferring to seniors (**authority**: juniors do not query a senior's tool), and following the crowd (**bandwagon**: adopting a popular model without checking it).","**Software without machine learning too**: code written by a biased LLM can contain bias even though its logic is fixed rules (such as requiring a family name)."]},{"heading":"Can hosting an open-weights model locally mitigate bias? (reasoned answer)","items":["An **open-weights model** (such as Llama or Mistral) is one you can download and run yourself.","**Mostly no: bias comes from the training data and weights.** Such models were trained on similar web data and carry similar stereotypes. Running one locally does not change it.",{"type":"p","text":"What local hosting does give"},"You can inspect it, **fine-tune** it (train it further) on balanced or specialist data, set your own system prompt and filters, test it, control your data, and no provider changes it without telling you.",{"type":"p","text":"Possible downside"},"Cloud providers add safety measures that a raw local model may lack, so it can be **more** biased straight out of the box. You also carry the responsibility and the hardware cost.","**Conclusion**: local hosting is for control and privacy, not a cure for bias. You still need bias testing, diverse teams and prompts, fine-tuning and constraints, human review and monitoring of outcomes."]},{"heading":"Mitigations in practice","items":["Review outputs carefully.","Test for **disparate outcomes**: differences between groups in approval rates or false positives.","Avoid proxy variables.","Gather requirements from diverse and inclusive sources.","Document your assumptions and how AI was used.","Check fairness in your specific field (see the FinTech case)."]}],"pitfalls":["Giving no specific example, or an example without explaining how it arose (data).","Answering the local-hosting question 'yes it removes bias' or 'no' without reasoning on weights and training data.","Equating bias only with images: it also appears in generated code and in decisions about people."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Comes from","items":["Stereotypes","Representation","Historical bias","Proxies"]},{"title":"Appears in","items":["Answers","Generated code"]},{"title":"Note","items":["Local hosting gives privacy, not freedom from bias"]}]}}},{"id":"verifiable-tasks","title":"Verifiable tasks and why coding improved so fast","priority":19,"tier":"likely","freq":"sample paper bonus Q(d) + Topics 1, 11","marks":"6 marks","summary":"**A verifiable task has an automatic, objective check of correctness (compile, tests, proof checker), giving a cheap reward signal for reinforcement learning and self-play, so models improve rapidly at it; coding is verifiable (to the strength of the tests), while requirements and design judgement are not.**","mnemonic":"Verifiable = can be machine-checked, so can be trained at scale","source":["CT4111_Notes_01a_Overview","CT4111_Notes_11_Emerging_Topics","CT4111_Notes_11d_Article_Dot_and_Swarm"],"sections":[{"heading":"What it means","items":["A task is **verifiable** if a solution can be checked automatically, cheaply and objectively, without human judgement: code compiles and passes tests, or a maths proof is checked by a **formal prover** (a program that checks proofs; the Navier-Stokes proof came with a machine-checkable version in the language Lean).",{"type":"p","text":"Why it drove progress"},"Models get further **reinforcement learning** (learning from rewards) using answers that can be proven right, in coding and maths, including playing against themselves.","Generate many attempts, check them, reward the correct ones: unlimited training signal with no human labelling.","The same check lets agents loop while working (write, test, fix).",{"type":"p","text":"Cautions"},"Checking is only as good as the tests: weak tests let wrong code pass.","The model may **game** the check (change the tests, or special-case them).","Passing tests is not the same as good design, security or maintainability."]},{"heading":"Judging other SE tasks (pick two and justify)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["SE task","Verifiable?","Justification"],"rows":[["Fixing a bug with a failing test","Yes (largely)","Test goes from red to green, regression suite stays green; but tests may be incomplete or overfitted"],["Library migration","Yes (largely)","Build, existing tests and linters confirm behaviour preserved; semantic subtleties may remain"],["Unit test generation","Partly","Tests can be run and measured by coverage and mutation score, but whether assertions encode the intended behaviour (oracle problem) needs human judgement"],["Static-analysis warning repair","Partly","Re-run analyser to confirm warning gone; whether it was a true positive and the fix is appropriate needs judgement"],["Requirements elicitation / specification","No","No ground truth: depends on stakeholder intent, negotiation and ambiguity; only expert review validates"],["Architecture and design decisions","No","Trade-offs are subjective and context-dependent (team skills, budget); no automatic oracle (though metrics can be measured later)"],["Code review quality / refactoring quality","Weakly","Behaviour preservation is checkable by tests, but readability and maintainability are hard to quantify"]]},"Implication: autonomy is safest where tasks are verifiable; elsewhere keep humans in the loop (Mollick: autonomy is safe where results can be checked mechanically)."]}],"pitfalls":["Saying 'coding is verifiable because AI is good at it': the reason is automatic checking (tests, compilers) giving a reward.","Calling every other task simply verifiable or not: justify using the oracle (what is the automatic check?) and note partial cases."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Task has an automatic check","sub":"compile, tests, proof","color":0},{"label":"Cheap reward signal","color":1},{"label":"Reinforcement learning and self-play","color":2},{"label":"Rapid improvement","sub":"this is why coding moved fast","color":3}]}}},{"id":"prompt-engineering","title":"Prompt engineering for code","priority":20,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topics 2 and 3 (two lectures) + labs","marks":"5-12 marks","summary":"**A good prompt has instruction, context, input data and output indicator; be specific, say what to do rather than what not to do, give examples (few-shot), allow reasoning (chain of thought), fix the output format, and iterate: test, analyse, improve.**","mnemonic":"I-C-I-O: Instruction, Context, Input, Output; then iterate","source":["CT4111_Notes_03_Prompting_and_Context","CT4111_Notes_02_Topic_2"],"sections":[{"heading":"Paradigm shift and elements","items":["**Old way**: **pre-train** a model, then **fine-tune** it on many labelled examples. A new task needed new data and training.","**Now: train, prompt, predict**: describe a new task and the model does it without retraining.","A **prompt** is the instructions and input that guide the response.",{"type":"p","text":"Four elements"},"**Instruction**: the task, as a verb (write, classify, summarise).","**Context**: outside information that steers the model.","**Input data**: the question or data.","**Output indicator**: the type or format wanted.","Another breakdown (Topic 2): context, task, requirements, constraints.",{"type":"p","text":"Good and bad"},"\"Write a Python function that sorts a list of dictionaries by the price key ascending; handle missing keys gracefully\" beats \"Write a function to sort data\".","Sample prompt: \"Create a Python Flask REST API with GET /users, GET /users/:id, POST, PUT, DELETE; include validation and error handling; in-memory store.\""]},{"heading":"Practical tips: wording","items":["**Instruction**: use a verb, try different wording, use headers such as `### Instruction ###`.","**Specificity**: detailed descriptions work better, but very long prompts can hurt. Avoid vagueness (\"2-3 sentences\", not \"briefly\"; \"for high school students\").","**Say what to do**: LLMs tend to do things they were told **not** to do. Give failure cases, and say \"if you do not know, say I do not know\".","Use AI to turn a bad prompt into a good one (for example to rewrite and criticise user stories)."]},{"heading":"Few-shot, chain-of-thought and output format","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Few-shot prompting"},"Put 1 to 5 examples in the prompt.","Pros: better results, shows the format, little effort. Cons: sensitive to how many and which examples; hard for complex tasks.","Like people, LLMs do better with examples, struggle with \"don'ts\", vague tasks and irrelevant information, and favour the first and last items (**primacy and recency**).",{"type":"p","text":"Chain-of-thought (CoT)"},"The model reasons step by step before answering.","Pros: solves harder problems, mistakes are visible. Cons: long output, more computing, errors within steps, may go in circles.","**think tags** are a convention giving it a space to reason, not a built-in feature; many apps hide it.",{"type":"p","text":"Output formatting"},"Ask explicitly (a single code block, JSON) for consistent results a program can process. There is no guarantee, so retry if invalid."]},{"heading":"Iterating and best practice","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Iterative design"},"Start with a simple **zero-shot** test (no examples). Then improve: change instructions, add examples, ask for more reasoning. Check the output: unclear or wrong? too much or too little detail? right style and format?",{"type":"p","text":"Handling unclear requirements"},"Identify missing or conflicting requirements, ask questions, state assumptions, and do not generate code until these are settled. **Critique-revise** loops (have it criticise then redo) and building up in small prompts help.",{"type":"p","text":"Best practices"},"Start small and iterate, give the relevant code, state constraints (speed, security), use guardrails (types, interfaces, tests), ask for explanations, define what success looks like, review and refine."]},{"heading":"Risks of generated code","items":["The model predicts likely next words, so it can invent libraries, functions and logic that do not exist or are wrong.","Security and legal: vulnerabilities, sending private code to other companies, copyright and licence breaches.","A **misaligned** agent (one pursuing something other than what you meant) may change the tests to make them pass.","For reuse and maintenance, write prompts that can be used again, and ask for clean, modular, documented code."]}],"pitfalls":["Writing 'don't do X' lists without positive instructions.","Assuming the output format will always be obeyed."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Instruction","color":0},{"label":"Context","color":1},{"label":"Input data","color":2},{"label":"Output indicator","color":3},{"label":"Iterate","sub":"test, analyse, improve","color":4}]}}},{"id":"test-oracle-quality","title":"Test oracle problem and test quality","priority":21,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 7 learning outcomes","marks":"5-8 marks","summary":"**The oracle problem is deciding whether an observed result is correct: AI can generate inputs and assertions but may not know the intended behaviour, so it produces shallow tests that pass without proving anything; good tests are trustworthy, maintainable, refactor-resilient and thorough.**","mnemonic":"Trust, Maintain, Resist, Thorough (TMRT) and AAA","source":["CT4111_Notes_07_Testing"],"sections":[{"heading":"The oracle problem and misleading tests","items":["**Test oracle problem**: the difficulty of deciding whether a result is correct. What should the output be?","**False confidence** is dangerous.",{"type":"p","text":"What AI-written tests may do"},"Check trivial things.","Copy what the code currently does, so its bugs get written into the tests.","Miss business rules.","Pass without proving anything is correct.","Weak examples: checking only the status code; only checking a method was called once; only the happy path; `assertNotNull`.",{"type":"p","text":"Checking compared with testing (Bach and Bolton)"},"**Checking**: mechanically confirming known expectations. **Testing**: critical evaluation using knowledge of the field and analysis of risk. AI is good at checks; humans decide the expectations.",{"type":"p","text":"Improving the oracle"},"Use domain knowledge and specifications, state expected behaviour, check **invariants** (things that must always be true) across many inputs, and review AI assertions by hand. Test quality matters more than quantity."]},{"heading":"Human-guided vs AI-guided workflow","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Step","Human-guided (preferred)","AI-guided"],"rows":[["1","Human defines test goals","Human defines test goals"],["2","AI generates draft tests","AI generates draft tests"],["3","Human reviews assertions","AI reviews assertions"],["4","Human adds missing edge cases","AI adds missing edge cases"],["5","AI refines and expands","AI refines and expands"]]},"Humans supply domain rules, expected outcomes, important edge cases, risk areas and invariants; AI scales it into many tests."]},{"heading":"Four test qualities","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Trustworthiness"},"No **flaky** tests (ones that randomly fail, due to time zones, randomness or outside services), and no false alarms or missed bugs.",{"type":"p","text":"Maintainability and readability"},"Arrange, act, assert. Descriptive names (`shouldReturnFalse_WhenPasswordIsTooShort`). One scenario per test.",{"type":"p","text":"Resilience to refactoring"},"Test **behaviour** (inputs and outputs), not how it is done inside. If renaming a private helper breaks a test, the test is fragile.",{"type":"p","text":"Thoroughness"},"Go beyond the happy path (null, empty, negative).","**Boundary value analysis**: test just below, at and just above a limit (17, 18, 19 for an age limit of 18).","Cover both outcomes of every decision (**branch coverage**, worked out from the code's tree structure, the AST), not just every line."]},{"heading":"Test doubles (fidelity)","items":["A **test double** is a stand-in for a real part during a test. **Fidelity** is how close it is to the real thing.",{"type":"table","head":["Double","Meaning","Asserted against?"],"rows":[["Actual object","Real collaborator (database, file system), not a double","-"],["Fake","Simplified working implementation (in-memory database)","No"],["Mock","Replaces collaborator; interactions are verified","Yes"],["Spy","Replaces collaborator and records what happens","Yes"],["Stub","Returns predefined responses","No"],["Dummy","Placeholder parameter to satisfy an interface","No"]]},"Slide example: `BookingService.createBooking` with the repository and payment processor mocked (JUnit 5 and Mockito). Five AI-written tests:","(1) Happy path: returns true and the booking is saved.","(2) Invalid input: a negative price throws `IllegalArgumentException`.","(3) Edge case: a date in the past throws.","(4) Room occupied: returns false and payment is never taken.","(5) Payment fails: returns false and the booking is never saved.","Prefer high fidelity when reasonable, but do not force it."]},{"heading":"Load, stress, MTBF and the testing standard","items":["**Load testing**: behaviour under typical peak use. **Stress testing**: push until it breaks.","**MTBF**: mean time between failures.","**ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119** is the international software testing standard. Parts: 1 concepts, 2 test processes, 3 documentation, 4 techniques, 5 keyword-driven testing."]},{"heading":"Test levels and types reminder","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Levels"},"**Unit**: smallest pieces on their own, with mocks.","**Integration**: the joins between modules (data formats, timing, agreements).","**System**: the whole product against requirements, including non-functional ones.","**Acceptance**: does it meet the customer's needs and is it ready for use?",{"type":"p","text":"Types"},"**Functional** (what it does) or **non-functional** (performance, including load and stress; security; usability; reliability such as MTBF).","**Static** (without running, such as SonarQube), **dynamic** (running it), **regression** (checking old features still work).","**Black box** (no knowledge of the code) or **white box** (code visible: unit tests, static analysis, mutation testing).","GenAI can generate unit, integration, edge-case and regression tests, mock set-ups and test data: faster, wider coverage of obvious cases, less repetition."]}],"pitfalls":["Equating a high number of generated tests with quality.","Mixing up mock and stub: mocks are asserted against, stubs are not."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Oracle problem","items":["Is this result correct?","AI may not know intent"]},{"title":"Result","items":["Shallow tests that pass"]},{"title":"Good tests are","items":["Trustworthy","Maintainable","Refactor-resilient","Thorough"]}]}}},{"id":"architecture-design","title":"Architecture alternatives, trade-offs, C4 and multi-agent design","priority":22,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 5 lecture + lab","marks":"5-12 marks","summary":"**GenAI generates several architectural alternatives from requirements, scores them against weighted quality criteria with a trade-off matrix, visualises them (C4 in Mermaid) and records the decision in an ADR, optionally with a supervisor plus specialist agents and graph RAG for context.**","mnemonic":"Alternatives, Criteria, Score, Decide, Record (ADR)","source":["CT4111_Notes_05_Design"],"sections":[{"heading":"Workflow","items":["**Software architecture** is how a system is split into parts and how they connect.","**Input**: requirements and use cases. Ask GenAI for standard and modern architectures, giving context by prompting or retrieval (**RAG**). If needed, use agents to:","(1) read the requirements and propose alternatives,","(2) weigh trade-offs,","(3) write **ADRs** (architecture decision records),","(4) score the combinations in a **score matrix**,","(5) draw diagrams.",{"type":"p","text":"Working together"},"Add the requirements and use cases, build a working prototype, add architecture styles and their trade-offs to the searchable material, use **design patterns** (standard solutions; books by Gamma and by Buschmann), draw **C4** and **UML** diagrams in **Mermaid** (diagrams written as text), and communicate in plain language, diagram text (Mermaid or PlantUML), XML or JSON, markdown files and skills.",{"type":"p","text":"Example prompt"},"\"Design 4 architectural alternatives for a sports club booking system (20k users, P95 under 250 ms, 99.9% uptime, GDPR; booking, payments, notifications). For each: C4 container diagram in Mermaid, tech stack, 3 trade-offs (performance, security, cost), deployment complexity rating.\"","**P95 under 250 ms** means 95% of responses arrive within 250 milliseconds."]},{"heading":"Quality metrics (8 groups)","items":["**Performance**: **latency** (delay), requests per second, CPU and memory use.","**Scalability**: maximum number of users.","**Availability**: percentage of time it is up.","**Maintainability**: time to deliver a change, **cyclomatic complexity** (paths through code), **coupling** and **cohesion** (how tied together parts are, and how focused each is), files touched per change.","**Deployability**: time and frequency of releases, rollback time, share of changes that fail.","**Security**: vulnerabilities, time to fix them, **attack surface** (places an attacker can try).","**Cost**: infrastructure, cost per request, effort.","**Architecture complexity**: number of services and connections, dependency graph."]},{"heading":"Weighted score matrix (worked)","items":[{"type":"task","show":0,"q":["Weights: performance 30%, security 25%, maintainability 25%, cost 20%. Scores out of 10, in that order:","Monolith 8, 7, 6, 9. Microservices 6, 9, 7, 6. Serverless 7, 8, 9, 7. Hybrid 8, 8, 8, 8.","Find each weighted total and choose a design."],"own":[{"type":"formula","label":"Weighted total","text":"total = sum of weight × score","math":["\\text{total} = \\sum \\text{weight} \\times \\text{score}"],"explain":["Multiply each score by how much that criterion matters, then add."],"example":["Scores 8 and 6 with weights 0.7 and 0.3 give 7.4."]}],"topic":"architecture-design"},"Weights: Performance 30%, Security 25%, Maintainability 25%, Cost 20%; scores 0-10; total = sum of weight x score.",{"type":"table","head":["Criterion (weight)","Monolith","Microservices","Serverless","Hybrid"],"rows":[["Performance (0.30)","8","6","7","8"],["Security (0.25)","7","9","8","8"],["Maintainability (0.25)","6","7","9","8"],["Cost (0.20)","9","6","7","8"],["Weighted total","7.45","7.00","7.75","8.00"]]},"Check Monolith: 0.3x8 + 0.25x7 + 0.25x6 + 0.2x9 = 2.4 + 1.75 + 1.5 + 1.8 = 7.45. Hybrid wins (Spring Boot monolith core plus Lambda for async edges): balanced, uses team skills (AI was blind to this), no cold-start risk, GDPR-ready Postgres.","Trade-off analysis steps: context, criteria, alternatives, evaluation (strengths, weaknesses, risks), decision, consequences."]},{"heading":"Architecture styles (names to know)","items":["Shaw and Garlan's classification: **pipes and filters** (data flows through processing steps), data abstraction or object-oriented, **event-based** (parts react to announcements), **layered**, repositories and blackboard (shared data store), interpreters, **MVC** (model, view, controller), client-server.","Modern styles: **SOA** (service-oriented architecture), microkernel (small core plus plug-ins), peer-to-peer, REST, **microservices** (many small independent services), cloud, IoT, blockchain.",{"type":"table","head":["","Microservices","SOA"],"rows":[["Goal","Decoupling, new services","Reuse, business functionality"],["Protocols","Lightweight (HTTP, REST)","Multiple message protocols"],["Platform","Cloud, containers","Common platform, standards, governance"],["Data","Each service has its own store","Shared data storage"]]},"**Service-based architecture** is a hybrid: finely divided like SOA, with the clear business boundaries (**bounded context**) of microservices."]},{"heading":"C4 model: four levels of zoom","items":["The **C4 model** draws a system at four levels, from far out to close up.","**1. Context**: the system with its users and other systems.","**2. Containers**: the apps, services and databases inside it (not Docker containers).","**3. Components**: the structure inside one container.","**4. Code**: the classes.","Mermaid has C4 text commands (`C4Container`, `Person`, `System_Ext`, `Container`, `ContainerDb`, `Rel`) that draw the diagrams (try mermaid.live)."]},{"heading":"Multi-agent design and Graph RAG","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Basic version"},"One agent per candidate architecture explores and defends it. Then build the score matrix and C4 diagrams.",{"type":"diagram","kind":"graph","data":{"title":"Supervisor with specialist agents","directed":true,"r":24,"height":380,"nodes":[{"id":"u","label":"User","sub":"requirements","x":5,"y":45,"color":0},{"id":"s","label":"Supervisor","sub":"coordinates, decides","x":32,"y":45,"color":1},{"id":"r","label":"Requirements","sub":"normalise input","x":62,"y":5,"color":2},{"id":"a","label":"Alternatives","sub":"3-5 options","x":90,"y":25,"color":2},{"id":"t","label":"Trade-off","sub":"analyst","x":90,"y":65,"color":2},{"id":"v","label":"Reviewer","sub":"NFR, risks","x":62,"y":85,"color":2},{"id":"w","label":"ADR writer","sub":"draft ADR","x":32,"y":85,"color":2}],"edges":[{"from":"u","to":"s"},{"from":"s","to":"r"},{"from":"r","to":"a"},{"from":"a","to":"t"},{"from":"t","to":"v"},{"from":"v","to":"w"},{"from":"w","to":"s","label":"draft","curve":30}]},"caption":"Supervisor decides if output is good enough or must be revised; final decision is the human's."},{"type":"p","text":"Agent roles"},"**Requirements** agent (structures the input), **alternatives** agent (3 to 5 options), **trade-off analyst** (performance, security, maintainability, cost), **reviewer** (are non-functional requirements covered? risks? consistency?), **ADR writer**, and a **supervisor**.","Benefits: focused sub-tasks, specialisation, supervised and visible reasoning, linked to the decision records and score matrix.",{"type":"p","text":"Graph RAG"},"Architectural knowledge is a network: requirements, decision records, components, incidents, trade-offs, constraints.","Pull out these things and their relationships into a **knowledge graph** (triples of subject, relation, object, with a description), so searching can answer \"which solutions did we reject?\" and \"what is affected by this change?\".","Pipeline: documents, extraction, knowledge graph, retrieval, prompt with precise context, 3 to 5 alternatives, decision record and score matrix."]}],"pitfalls":["AI is blind to team skills and budget: always add them in the criteria or human review."]},{"id":"code-smells-refactoring","title":"Code smells, design issues and refactoring with GenAI","priority":23,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 9 (large) + Topic 8","marks":"5-10 marks","summary":"**Refactoring improves internal structure without changing behaviour; GenAI can detect smells, plan and implement refactorings (best in multi-agent loops with compile and test checks), but must be guided and validated because it changes semantics, deletes code or hallucinates.**","mnemonic":"As-is, To-be, Test coverage, Refactor with tests (4-step workflow)","source":["CT4111_Notes_09_Refactoring","CT4111_Notes_08_Maintenance"],"sections":[{"heading":"Smells and clones","items":["A **code smell** (Fowler 1999) is a sign of low-quality code that should be **refactored** (restructured without changing behaviour). Causes: tight schedules, carelessness, shortcuts. It makes code harder to read and maintain.","**Long method**: too long (many comments suggest splitting it).","**Shotgun surgery**: one change means editing many classes.","**Feature envy**: a method uses another class's data more than its own.","**God class**: too many responsibilities.","**Refused bequest**: a subclass ignores what it inherits.","A **clone** is duplicated code:",{"type":"table","head":["Clone type","Meaning"],"rows":[["Type 1","Exact copy except whitespace and comments"],["Type 2","Same structure, renamed identifiers"],["Type 3","Similar with statements added, removed or changed"],["Type 4","Different code, same functionality (loop vs stream)"]]},{"type":"p","text":"Test smells"},"**Eager test** (calls many things at once), duplicate assert, **assertion roulette** (several unexplained assertions, so you cannot tell which failed), **magic numbers** (unexplained values). In manual tests: ambiguous or conditional tests, steps or checks in the wrong place, actions never verified.","Finding smells by hand is subjective, and tools (PMD, JDeodorant, DesigniteJava) disagree. GenAI handles the same idea expressed differently but has limits on input and context, so combine it with tools."]},{"heading":"GenAI for smell detection","items":["**Direct detection**: give the model a role, define the smell (a god class: many responsibilities, poor cohesion), supply code measurements, and demand a strict **JSON** (structured) answer of True or False.","**iSMELL**: choose suitable tools, run them, then prompt the LLM with their results, the definitions and examples.","Filter and rank proposed fixes (the **CORE** method).","Refine repeatedly using agents."]},{"heading":"Refactoring and workflow","items":["**Refactoring**: improving the inside of code without changing what it does.",{"type":"p","text":"Two levels"},"**Code level**: pull out or fold in a method or variable, swap an algorithm, rename.","**Architecture level**: split a single large app (**monolith**) into services, pull out authentication.","Rule of thumb: \"three strikes and you refactor\".",{"type":"diagram","kind":"flow","data":{"steps":[{"label":"1 As-is structure","sub":"what to improve and why"},{"label":"2 To-be structure","sub":"refactoring strategies"},{"label":"3 Test coverage","sub":"check and fill gaps"},{"label":"4 Implement","sub":"small steps, tests as safety net"}],"direction":"row"},"caption":"General refactoring workflow."},{"type":"p","text":"Prompting"},"A vague prompt gives variable results. Naming the type of refactoring and the target method works better (Liu et al.).",{"type":"p","text":"RefAgent (several agents)"},"A tool gathers context (what depends on what, quality measures). A **planner** writes a plan (refactor or not, and how, per class and method). A **generator** writes the code. A **compiler** agent asks for fixes if it does not compile. A **tester** writes and runs tests; if they still fail after 20 tries, keep the original code.","Refactoring tests: prompts that make the model reason in steps (understand intent, recognise smells, apply rules, rewrite), plus rules (turn duplicate asserts into one parameterised test; add messages to asserts), beat simple prompts.","Success is more than still working: maintainability and readability are hard to measure."]},{"heading":"Failure modes and mitigation","items":["**What goes wrong**: behaviour changes, unnecessary changes, deleted code, wrong or inconsistent renaming, naming and scope clashes, wrong **casts** (type conversions), unfinished refactoring, invented functions, syntax errors. Traditional refactoring tools also fail on unusual cases.","**Reduce it**: structured prompts with step-by-step reasoning, a few examples, try different models, testing strategies, compile checks, human review. Keep tests as the safety net, and check the tests are good.","**Prompt debt**: fragile prompts that depend on one model and are hard to maintain. Prevent it by versioning and documenting prompts, testing them, and requiring strict output formats."]}],"pitfalls":["Saying AI refactoring is safe without tests: it can alter behaviour silently."]},{"id":"implementation-workflows","title":"Implementation workflows: perspectives and five levels of AI adoption","priority":24,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 6 lecture","marks":"6-10 marks","summary":"**Five workflows with increasing AI autonomy (reviewer, support, junior developer, test-driven, vibecoding), each with different pros, cons and best-fit tasks, viewed through four perspectives: prompting as specification, human in the loop, embodied agents, agents as an orchestra.**","mnemonic":"Review, Support, Junior, Test, Vibes (RSJTV)","source":["CT4111_Notes_06_Implementation_Workflows"],"sections":[{"heading":"Four perspectives","items":[{"type":"p","text":"1. Prompting as specification"},"In traditional programming the code says both **what** and **how**. With LLMs these come apart. The model cannot guess the \"what\", so the developer must specify it carefully: requirements work matters more.",{"type":"p","text":"2. Human in the loop"},"A person makes the hard decisions and is responsible for quality and bad outcomes. This needs skilled people, but relying on LLMs may erode skills. Is it a real safeguard or false comfort?",{"type":"p","text":"3. Embodied agents"},"Given a **shell** (command line: `cd`, `ls`, `cat`), an agent \"exists\" somewhere: it can move around, act, and follow different instructions in each folder. It is an actor, not just an adviser.",{"type":"p","text":"4. Agents as an orchestra"},"Run many agents (for example with the **LangChain** library), each with its own role, tools and place; you are the conductor. For example one agent per failing test, one to merge, one to implement."]},{"heading":"Five workflows","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Workflow","How","Pros","Cons / best when"],"rows":[["0 No AI","Dev implements, human PR review","High quality, no compute cost, strong codebase awareness","Slow, limited by team skills, lazy 'LGTM' reviews, knowledge loss when devs leave"],["1 LLM as reviewer","Dev implements; LLM reviews PR; discussion with LLM","Rapid review and back-and-forth; developer keeps awareness","Less cross-pollination and mentoring (safeguard: knowledge-sharing sessions); does not replace linters/tests; substitute or additional to human?"],["2 LLM as support","Dev implements with LLM help (search, explain, implement, trace, brainstorm edge cases, pre-review)","Fully in loop, high expertise, no org change, low risk","Limited speed-up; deskilling unclear. Best when LLM has context (RAG, MCP), dev keeps control, task needs human expertise"],["3 LLM as junior developer","Ticket assigned to agent; agent opens PR; human reviews","Very rapid; control via issue and review","Review becomes bottleneck; reviewers rarely validate everything so hallucinations slip through. Best with strong quality safeguards, well-specified incremental tasks, clear context"],["4 LLM in TDD","Dev writes tests; agents implement until pass","Removes review bottleneck; requirements enforced by tests","Needs thorough tests; LLM may cheat tests; LLM lock-in; token cost"],["5 Vibecoding","LLM writes ticket, code, review; human just uses product","No or low skills; quick start","Low quality, hard to control, correctness unknown. Best for rapid prototypes with no reuse, hackathons, unfamiliar language"]]},"Summary line from lecture: review = quick turnaround but risks technical debt; support = maintain quality, limited speed; junior = faster, reviewing is the bottleneck; test = expensive upfront, hard to maintain; vibes = requires no expertise, very rapid, inflexible.","Words in the table: **PR** = pull request. **LGTM** = \"looks good to me\" (an approval without real checking). **MCP** = a standard way to connect tools and data to a model. **Cross-pollination** = team members learning from each other.","Skills: reusable instruction bundles (e.g. test-generation skill with steps and constraints) are part of agent workflows."]}],"pitfalls":["Calling vibecoding 'bad' without saying when it is appropriate (prototypes, hackathons)."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Reviewer","sub":"least AI autonomy","color":0},{"label":"Support","color":1},{"label":"Junior developer","color":2},{"label":"Test-driven","color":3},{"label":"Vibecoding","sub":"most AI autonomy","color":4}]}}},{"id":"privacy-security-risks","title":"Privacy leakage and code-security risks","priority":25,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 10 (security and privacy sections)","marks":"5-8 marks","summary":"**Prompts can leak PII and proprietary code to providers (Samsung case); mitigate with private LLMs, zero-retention APIs and PII-filtering proxies; GenAI code brings supply-chain, injection and unpatched-vulnerability risks that need scanning and review.**","mnemonic":"Leak-proof the prompt; verify every package","source":["CT4111_Notes_10_Ethical_Legal","CT4111_Notes_02_Topic_2"],"sections":[{"heading":"Privacy and data leakage","items":["**PII** (personally identifying information): data that identifies, contacts or locates a person, directly or indirectly (names, emails, social security numbers, bank details, biometrics).","Also protect private code, API keys, passwords, medical and legal records and intellectual property.",{"type":"p","text":"How leaks happen"},"Prompts containing code or user data may be logged and used for training unless a business agreement prevents it.","**Samsung case**: in three incidents, employees pasted faulty source code, defect-detection code and a meeting transcript into ChatGPT.","Keys leaked in public repositories can be learnt by models.","Generated code can itself break privacy rules (such as tracking cookies legal in the US but needing consent in the EU)."]},{"heading":"Ways to protect data","items":["**Private instance**: run the model yourself. **Zero-retention licence**: the provider keeps nothing. **Filtering proxy**: a go-between that removes personal data before sending (Presidio is one tool).",{"type":"table","head":["Mitigation","Pros","Cons"],"rows":[["Private LLM instance (open weights on own cloud or on-premise)","Absolute data control, fine-tuning, predictable performance","High GPU cost, operational complexity, performance gap to frontier models"],["Zero-retention API licence","Frontier models, no infrastructure, SOC 2/HIPAA/GDPR DPAs","Data leaves your network (trust and audit risk), vendor lock-in and downtime, no deep customisation"],["Automatic PII filtering proxy (e.g. Presidio, regex)","Proactive, cheap, works with public APIs","Context stripping reduces answer quality, false negatives, extra latency"]]},"**Best practice** is a mix: a zero-retention service for hard tasks, reached through an internal filter, with a policy written by legal experts on which tools may be used for what (or a ban). No approach is bullet-proof.","**Shadow AI**: staff using unapproved AI tools, often because policies are too strict. Provide an approved, safe way."]},{"heading":"Code security risks","items":["**Hallucinated dependency**: the AI suggests a package that does not exist, or one an attacker has registered under that invented name (**slopsquatting**). Installing it runs harmful code: a **supply-chain attack**.","**Data poisoning**: attackers publish thousands of repositories containing a hidden **backdoor** so that models learn it.","**Insecure code**: passwords written into code, and **SQL injection** (typed text becomes a database command, using a quote, double dash or `OR 1=1`; fix with parameterised queries).","**Old holes returning**: for example Log4Shell, a serious Java logging flaw (found November 2021, fixed December 2021), may reappear because training data contains vulnerable code.","**Automated technical debt**: code nobody understands.",{"type":"p","text":"Mitigation"},"Analyse generated code with tools (Snyk, SonarQube; none is fully reliable), check where dependencies come from, review, test, give credentials the minimum access, keep an **SBOM** (component list).","**Misaligned agent behaviour**: changing tests so they pass, or inventing justifications for a design."]}],"pitfalls":["Only naming the risks: include one mitigation each."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Leaks","items":["PII","Proprietary code","Samsung case"]},{"title":"Leak fixes","items":["Private LLM","Zero-retention API","PII-filtering proxy"]},{"title":"Code risks","items":["Supply chain","Injection","Unpatched flaws: scan and review"]}]}}},{"id":"llm-concepts","title":"LLM concepts: tokens, context, hallucination, jagged frontier, chain of thought, tools","priority":26,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 1 'some exam questions'","marks":"3-6 marks","summary":"**An LLM predicts the next token from a limited context window; it can seem brilliant and fail on easy tasks (jagged frontier), hallucinate confidently, and becomes useful through chain of thought, tools, RAG and agentic harnesses.**","mnemonic":"Predict, Context, Jagged, Hallucinate, Think, Tools","source":["CT4111_Notes_01a_Overview"],"sections":[{"heading":"Concepts: prediction, jagged ability and hallucination","items":["**Next-token predictor**: an LLM produces one **token** (word or word piece) at a time, adds it to the text and repeats. The \"stochastic parrot\" view says it is only prediction, yet it learns to plan ahead to write coherent text. Output varies between runs (**non-deterministic**).","**Jagged intelligence** (jagged frontier): AI beats humans at some tasks yet fails at ones that seem easier, so its ability is uneven and unpredictable (Dell'Acqua et al.).",{"type":"p","text":"Misjudging what an LLM can do"},"**False negative**: \"it cannot solve this easy problem\" (it might with another prompt, newer model or another try).","**False positive**: \"it solved this hard problem!\" (perhaps it or something similar was in the training data, such as building a website or game from scratch).","**Hallucination**: wrong answers, or things made up outright (calling a function that does not exist), said confidently, like a student guessing in an exam. Reduce it with context, retrieval, tests and checking."]},{"heading":"Concepts: thinking, tools and context","items":["**Chain of thought** (CoT): use extra tokens to think before answering (restate the problem and assumptions, work step by step). It improves hard tasks and exposes reasoning errors (though apps often hide it), but costs tokens and time.","**Tools**: web search, calculator, searching, running and testing code on disk, the command line, **MCP servers** (a standard way to plug tools in).","**RAG**: a \"Google search over your codebase\".","**Reasoning models** think before answering. **Agentic** models are an LLM plus a **harness** (the program around it that gives tools and loops): proactive, long-running, goal-driven.",{"type":"p","text":"Making a one-token-at-a-time model useful"},"Run it in a loop, prompt well, give context and tools, let it think, and wrap it in an agent that gets feedback from tests.","**Context window**: a limited number of tokens; the middle gets forgotten; long chats cost more.","Statistics for colour: 41% of code is AI-generated; over 80% of developers report productivity gains, but only 33% trust AI output and 75% manually review every snippet."]},{"heading":"A day in the life: pre-AI vs post-AI engineer","items":["**Before AI**: read bug tickets, hold the context in your head, hand-write routine code, test manually, write documentation from scratch.","**After AI**: tickets prioritised by AI; one agent per ticket using failing-then-passing tests to confirm fixes; you review generated code for style, consistency and bloat; documentation is generated; more time to plan the architecture."]}],"pitfalls":["Calling hallucination 'lying': it is plausible-sounding prediction without grounding."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Basics","items":["Predicts next token","Limited context window"]},{"title":"Failure modes","items":["Jagged frontier","Confident hallucination"]},{"title":"What helps","items":["Chain of thought","Tools and RAG","Agentic harness"]}]}}},{"id":"qa-criteria-failure-analysis","title":"QA criteria and GenAI test-failure analysis","priority":27,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 7 sub-topics 4 and 5","marks":"5 marks","summary":"**GenAI acts as a QA architect (acceptance criteria, edge cases, severity, automation level from user stories) and as a failure analyst (root cause from stack trace plus test plus production code, bug in test or code, suggested fix).**","mnemonic":"QA criteria in, root cause out","source":["CT4111_Notes_07_Testing"],"sections":[{"heading":"Defining quality-assurance criteria with GenAI","items":["Generate acceptance criteria from user stories; identify edge cases and negative scenarios; turn business requirements into scenarios both business and automation can understand; validate testability (and so implementability) of a task.","**QA** (quality assurance) is making sure the product meets its standard. **Acceptance criteria** are the conditions that must hold for a feature to count as done.","**GenAI as QA architect**: a tireless brainstorming partner (20 angles on what could go wrong), that finds blind spots (decision logic implied by a requirement, missing combinations) and standardises a team's **definition of done** checklist.","Blueprint prompt: 'Act as a Senior QA Architect... for each criterion list the conditions and outcomes (flag undefined), scenario description, decision rules covered, severity (Critical/Major/Minor), automation feasibility (Unit/Integration/E2E).'"]},{"heading":"Using GenAI to analyse test failures","items":["Instant log parsing (root cause from a 200-line stack trace); contextual correlation (failing test plus production code pinpoints the diverging line); hypothesis generation (prioritised reasons).","A **stack trace** is the list of method calls leading to an error. **Contextual correlation** means reading the failing test and the code together to find the line where they disagree.","Prompt: Act as a Senior Java Test Engineer; here is the stack trace, the test, the production code; explain (1) root cause, (2) bug in test or production code, (3) corrected code.","Example: MembershipService used plusDays(365) for a one-year expiry; start 15 Jan 2028 includes 29 Feb 2028 (366 days) so result was 14 Jan 2029 instead of 15 Jan 2029; fix: plusYears(1) using java.time.","Human still verifies the diagnosis and the fix."]},{"heading":"From requirement to test cases (TDD example)","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Test","Scenario","Expected","Category"],"rows":[["TC-01","Shorter than 8 chars ('123')","false","Invalid input"],["TC-02","Exactly 8 chars ('Abcde123')","true","Edge case / business rule"],["TC-03","No digits ('Password')","false","Business rule"],["TC-04","Null password","false","Robustness"]]}]}],"pitfalls":["Accepting the AI's root-cause explanation without checking whether the bug is in the test or the code."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"QA architect","items":["Acceptance criteria","Edge cases","Severity","Automation level"]},{"title":"Failure analyst","items":["Stack trace + test + code","Bug in test or code?","Suggested fix"]}]}}},{"id":"maintenance-debt-triage","title":"Maintenance, technical debt and triage","priority":28,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 8 (first half)","marks":"5-8 marks","summary":"**Maintenance is the dominant lifecycle cost (60-80%); AI-generated code can accelerate duplication and technical debt, so AI must also be used to reduce maintenance; structured change management and bug reports make AI triage effective.**","mnemonic":"Maintenance eats 60-80%; debt compounds; structure helps AI","source":["CT4111_Notes_08_Maintenance"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions","items":["**Software maintenance** (ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764): all activities needed to support a software system. **Sustainment**: keeping the system or service running.","**Maintainability** (ISO/IEC 25010): how effectively and efficiently the intended maintainers can modify a product.",{"type":"p","text":"Its characteristics"},"**Analysability** (easy to understand and diagnose), **changeability**, **stability** (changes cause few surprises), **modularity**, **reusability**, **testability**.","Maintenance is the main cost (60-80% of total).","**Lehman's laws**: continuing change (a system must adapt or become less satisfactory) and increasing complexity (unless work is done to reduce it)."]},{"heading":"Emerging maintenance crisis and economics","items":["Uncontrolled AI-accelerated growth brings duplication and inconsistency, defects that pile up, and shorter-lived software.","**GitClear study** of 211 million lines (2020-2024): duplication and **churn** (code rewritten soon after) rose, and **reuse** (moving code into shared modules) fell sharply. These problems rarely fail immediately and are easy to miss in review.","**Economics** (James Shore): every month's output adds maintenance burden. If AI output quality is not high, the productivity gain soon disappears and leaves a permanent maintenance penalty. So using AI to help with maintenance is essential."]},{"heading":"Technical debt","items":["**Technical debt** (Cunningham): not-quite-right code that causes extra work for the next developer. Quick now, costlier later. Carry only a little and pay it off quickly.","Kinds: code, design, documentation, test.",{"type":"p","text":"AI forms of debt"},"**Governance** debt (over-reliance), **prompt** debt (unclear or undocumented prompts), **model** debt (behaviour differing across versions and providers), **fast-integration** debt (rapid merging without checking).","**Technical deflation** (Shapiro): cleaning code keeps getting cheaper, so debt may even be a sensible investment (\"borrow expensive human hours, repay with cheap AI hours\"), but the friction still builds up."]},{"heading":"Triage and planning","items":["**Triage** means sorting problems by urgency.","Manage changes systematically: record needs, whether bugs can be reproduced, their type and the decisions, in an **issue tracker** and version control (**VCS**). Write down unspoken knowledge so AI can use it. The overhead can tempt people to skip steps.",{"type":"p","text":"How GenAI helps"},"Makes records more explicit, structured and consistent.","Checks that documentation, tests and **traceability links** (which requirement connects to which code) agree.","Gives a first priority order, which humans confirm.","Answers questions by meaning, to find where a function, data flow or error lives.","Turns unstructured bug reports into description, steps to reproduce, expected behaviour and environment (**BRMDS**).",{"type":"p","text":"Challenges and responses"},"Growing code, tight coupling, inconsistent names, old code without documentation or tests, duplication, limited long-range planning, and new libraries the model has never seen. Respond with structured prompts, examples, other models and tests.","Use **static analysis** and measures (SonarQube, ESLint, Pylint; product and process **metrics**) to guide the AI, such as not touching highly coupled code without more tests. New AI measures: context limits, tokens, number of attempts, energy."]}],"pitfalls":["Quoting numbers without source: 60-80% (maintenance share), 211M lines (GitClear)."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Fact","items":["Maintenance is 60-80% of cost"]},{"title":"Danger","items":["AI code speeds up duplication and debt"]},{"title":"Response","items":["Use AI to maintain too","Change management","Good bug reports"]}]}}},{"id":"version-control-ai","title":"Version control for AI-assisted development","priority":29,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 2 lecture + lab","marks":"4-6 marks","summary":"**Version control provides auditability and recoverability: commit before an AI works, give it its own branch, review its diff and merge only if it holds up; treat the AI like a junior developer.**","mnemonic":"Commit, Branch, Diff, Review, Merge-or-Revert","source":["CT4111_Notes_02_Topic_2"],"sections":[{"heading":"Git model","items":["Four places: **working tree** (files now), **staging area** (selected for next commit), **local repository** (.git history, offline), **remote repository** (shared).","Commands: git add, commit, push move changes down; clone, pull bring them back; status shows state; **diff** shows unstaged changes; git show shows what a commit changed; log reads history; **git restore** discards uncommitted work; **git revert** undoes a shared commit.","A **commit** is a saved snapshot (who, when, why). A **branch** is a parallel line of development (one per feature or fix); merging combines lines, **conflicts** appear when the same lines changed and need human resolution.","GitHub/GitLab host remotes and add pull requests (merge requests), review threads, issues and permissions (not part of git). **The diff is the unit of review; the PR is the unit of decision.**"]},{"heading":"Good practice with AI","items":["**Commit before** setting an AI tool to work: a clean working tree means git diff shows exactly what it did. If wrong: restore or revert.","Treat the AI like a **junior developer**: its own branch, review its diff, merge only if it holds up. A repository agent works on the repo directly; automated review agents read diffs.","Benefits: auditable record of all AI and human changes; ability to revert errors; version control adds guardrails to AI.","Lab: fork a repo, make a small change on a branch using AI, open a PR, accept it on your fork."]}],"pitfalls":["Letting an agent edit an uncommitted working tree: you cannot separate its changes from yours."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Commit before AI works","color":0},{"label":"Give it its own branch","color":1},{"label":"Review the diff","sub":"treat it like a junior","color":2},{"label":"Merge only if it holds up","color":3}]}}},{"id":"professional-environment","title":"Professional responsibility, ethics and environmental impact","priority":30,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 10 (second half)","marks":"4-8 marks","summary":"**Legal compliance is not the same as ethics; engineers stay accountable for AI output (IEEE code principle 1: public interest), should declare AI use, avoid AI slop and respect academic integrity; AI has environmental costs (energy, water) mostly in inference, so use the smallest model that does the job.**","mnemonic":"Accountable, Declared, Verified; smallest model that works","source":["CT4111_Notes_10_Ethical_Legal"],"sections":[{"heading":"Ethics vs law and professional responsibility","items":["'Not everything that is ethical is legal; not everything that is legal is ethical.'","**IEEE-CS/ACM Software Engineering Code of Ethics:** 8 principles; principle 1: act consistently with the **public interest**.","IEEE-CS and ACM are the two main professional bodies for computing. **Whistleblowing** is reporting wrongdoing from inside.","**Whistleblowing:** report when AI-driven shortcuts compromise safety or public trust; takes courage, protection varies by country.","**Documentation:** declare AI use in commit messages and READMEs; keep a **Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)** listing components (open source, proprietary, AI-generated) for traceability.","**Accountability:** the person or organisation is accountable, not the tool: Mata v Avianca (lawyers sanctioned for fake citations, 2023), Moffatt v Air Canada (airline liable for chatbot advice, 2024), Deloitte Australia (partial refund for fabricated references, 2025).","**AI slop:** unreviewed AI content pushes cognitive load onto the reader; need critical thinking; do not automate your core intellectual value.","**Academic integrity:** AI is not automatically allowed; permission is per module and assessment; never present AI material as your own; you remain responsible for accuracy; if in doubt ask first.","**When not to use AI:** it defeats the learning purpose; you cannot verify the output; not authorised by policy; data should not leave your control (PII, IP); if you feel you should hide your use, it is probably a bad use case."]},{"heading":"Environmental considerations","items":["Data centres used about 415 TWh in 2024 (1.5% of global electricity, IEA); AI-focused hyperscale sites 100 MW or more (about 100,000 households); in Ireland data centres use about 20% of metered electricity.","Training GPT-4: about 25,000 GPUs, about 14 weeks, about 42.4 GWh (0.43 GWh per day). But once deployed, **inference across all users far outweighs training**.","Per-query estimates vary about 2000-fold (519 ml water per 100-word email vs 0.26 ml and 0.24 Wh per median Gemini text prompt) depending on what is counted (training or inference, direct or indirect water). Efficiency per prompt falls (33x in 12 months at Google) but total AI data-centre electricity grew (about 50% in 2025).","Practice: use the smallest model that does the job; images and video cost more than text; reasoning models add tokens; fewer, better prompts; bound output length; use search engines for simple questions; avoid unnecessary default transcription."]}],"pitfalls":["Quoting a single environmental number as if it were settled: say estimates vary and depend on scope."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Ethics","items":["Legal is not the same as ethical","You stay accountable"]},{"title":"Honesty","items":["Declare AI use","Avoid AI slop","Academic integrity"]},{"title":"Environment","items":["Energy and water","Mostly inference","Use smallest model that works"]}]}}},{"id":"fintech-flow-case","title":"Lab case: FinTech-Flow loan-approval microservice","priority":31,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 10 lab assignment (scenario for Q6(c) style questions)","marks":"8-12 marks","summary":"**Four failures from one AI-generated loan service: hallucinated dependency (supply chain), AGPL copy (IP), postcode proxy bias, and EU AI Act logging and oversight failures; each links to a topic and a concrete remedy.**","mnemonic":"Package, Licence, Postcode, Logs","source":["CT4111_Notes_10b_Lab"],"sections":[{"heading":"Scenario","items":["FinTech-Flow, a European startup, uses an agentic coding assistant to build a loan-approval microservice (rule-based expert system for risk scoring, assumed an AI system under the AI Act; live in 2028 when Annex III high-risk rules apply). Lead developer Sarah prompts a FastAPI + PostgreSQL service with audit logging, reviews briefly and merges.","Words: **agentic coding assistant** = AI that carries out coding tasks itself. **Microservice** = a small independent service. **Rule-based expert system** = decisions from written rules. **FastAPI** = a Python web framework; **PostgreSQL** = a database. **Audit log** = a record of what happened."]},{"heading":"Problem 1: security (hallucinated dependency)","items":["**What happened**: the AI suggested `fastapi-security-audit-pro`, a name an attacker had already registered on **PyPI** (Python's package store). Its install script stole **deployment credentials** (the passwords used to release the software).","**Contain**: change (rotate) the credentials and isolate the pipeline.","**Investigate** affected systems and customer data (GDPR requires notifying breaches).","**Prevent**: check where every dependency comes from; **lock files** with **hashes** (record the exact version and fingerprint); an internal mirror or allow-list of packages; dependency scanning tools (SCA); minimal-access build credentials; human review of dependency changes."]},{"heading":"Problem 2: IP (AGPL copy-paste trap)","items":["**What happened**: a function was copied word for word from software under **AGPL-3.0**, with the notices removed, while the company wants its source private.","AGPL is strong **copyleft** that also covers use over a network, so merely offering the service could require releasing your source.","**Options**: (a) comply and release under AGPL (clashes with the business model); (b) get a separate commercial permission from the owner; (c) remove it and write an independent version from the specification (**clean room**), reviewed.","Cosmetic rewriting does not help if the result is still derived from the original.","**Prevent**: licence scanning, code-match filters, SBOM, a policy."]},{"heading":"Problem 3: bias (zip code as a proxy)","items":["**What happened**: a US-centred rule gives districts with large immigrant communities a negative adjustment: a 15 percentage-point gap in approvals at similar incomes.","A **proxy** is a value that stands in for a protected characteristic.","The gap shows a difference (possible **indirect discrimination**) but does not prove the cause: other credit factors have not been examined (**confounders**).","**Investigate**: compare like with like on legitimate factors (income, debt, history); remove the postcode and measure the effect; check whether it stands in for protected traits; ask whether it is justified and necessary; use fairness measures; involve legal and domain experts.","**Fix**: drop or replace the variable, retrain or recalibrate, and keep monitoring."]},{"heading":"Problem 4: EU AI Act (transparency and logging)","items":["**What happened**: logs hold only time, application ID and outcome, with no link to inputs, software version, rules or thresholds, and there is no override or tested way to suspend the system.","**High-risk duties**: technical documentation, automatic logging so decisions can be traced, transparency, **human oversight** able to override, accuracy and robustness, risk management.","**Remedy**: log the inputs (or reduced, hashed references), model and rule version, thresholds and reasons for each decision; a screen where a loan officer can inspect and override; a tested **kill switch** and human review route."]},{"heading":"The four reflection questions (reasoning points)","items":["**Traceability**: existing logs only show that a decision was made. Missing: inputs, version, rules, thresholds, reasons and human involvement. Add structured decision records, versioned rules, an audit trail that cannot be altered and human review, while storing only necessary data (retention limits, access control, **pseudonymising**: replacing names with codes).","**Professional responsibility:** using an AI agent does not remove Sarah's accountability (she reviewed and merged); she remains responsible for diligence, the company for governance (policies, training, compliance, provider of the high-risk system). Facts needed: her role and authority, company policies, review process, what she knew, contracts, jurisdiction.","**Bias and fairness:** see #3; difference is evidence of disparity, not proof of unlawful discrimination.","**IP strategy:** see #2; compare compliance (open sourcing), permission (licence from owner), replacement (clean implementation)."]}],"pitfalls":["Treating the 15-point gap as proof of discrimination, or as meaningless."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"1. Supply chain","items":["Hallucinated dependency"]},{"title":"2. IP","items":["AGPL code copied"]},{"title":"3. Bias","items":["Postcode as proxy"]},{"title":"4. EU AI Act","items":["No logging or oversight"]}]}}},{"id":"re-fundamentals","title":"Requirements engineering fundamentals and AI-in-RE research","priority":32,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 4 lecture (first and last parts)","marks":"4-8 marks","summary":"**RE is a cross-cutting critical success factor (only 31% of projects succeed); classic RE has analysis (elicitation, interpretation, negotiation, documentation, validation) and management (change, tracing); AI scales it but needs human validation; the research and adoption slides are labelled non-examinable.**","mnemonic":"31-50-19: success, challenged, failed","source":["CT4111_Notes_04_Requirements_Engineering"],"sections":[{"heading":"Why RE matters and why it is hard","items":["**RE** (requirements engineering) is working out and managing what a system must do.",{"type":"p","text":"Why it matters"},"Standish CHAOS report 2020: **31%** of projects successful (on time, budget and scope), **50%** challenged, **19%** failed (cancelled or unused).","Monserrat et al. 2026: 30 experts identified 49 critical success factors in 6 categories (team, organisational, technical, development, process and project management, project).","Requirement-related factors: detailed analysis, involving all participants, good communication, managing scope, managing **stakeholders** (everyone with an interest), managing change.",{"type":"p","text":"Why it is hard at scale"},"Drawing out vague needs, writing them in a structured way, checking and negotiating them (tracing them and spotting conflicts), and managing change (control and **baselines**: agreed snapshots).","There are millions of text items, ambiguity, and costly manual tracing."]},{"heading":"Classical RE process (Pohl)","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Requirements analysis (repeated)"},"**Elicitation**: identify stakeholders and sources, gather raw requirements.","**Interpretation**: organise, classify, refine.","**Negotiation**: dependencies, contradictions, priorities.","**Documentation**: write them down.","**Validation**: check they are correct, complete and consistent.",{"type":"p","text":"Requirements management"},"**Change management**: handle change requests and versions.","**Tracing**: record where each came from and how it is implemented."]},{"heading":"The requirements document (SRS)","items":["An **SRS** is a software requirements specification. Standards: IEEE 830-1998 and ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148:2018.","It covers: context and scope, assumptions, stakeholders and types of user, what is inside the system (**boundary**), a glossary.","**Functional** requirements (use cases, user stories, business rules), data, interfaces.","**Non-functional** requirements (performance, security, reliability, usability, portability, maintainability).","Legal requirements (GDPR), a **traceability matrix** (table linking requirements to design and tests), acceptance criteria, versions.","Priorities by **MoSCoW**: Must, Should, Could, Won't."]},{"heading":"AI in RE: opportunities and risks","items":["**Opportunities**: mining requirements from many sources, grouping similar feedback, LLM interviews and interview guides, meeting summaries.","**Risks**: invented requirements, bias towards the loudest users, privacy (GDPR).","**Vision**: AI assists, the engineer stays in charge."]},{"heading":"AI in RE: research and adoption (not examinable)","items":["**NICE**: spots, classifies and explains non-functional requirements using small language models. A large model (GPT-4o) makes artificial labelled examples; smaller ones (T5, Llama 3.1, Phi-3) are trained on them. Score about 0.90 (**F1**, out of 1), cheaper and faster than large models.","**Multi-agent requirements analysis** (Sami et al.): agents playing product owner, developer, tester and manager write user stories, check them against quality rules (INVEST, ISO 29148), prioritise together and merge a ranking. Limits: nothing to compare against, tiny evaluation, the LLM grades its own work.","**Adoption**: in 2024 about 62% of developers used or planned to use AI tools (Stack Overflow); a Copilot study found tasks 55% faster (Peng 2023); only 14% of 74 studies of AI in RE were tested in industry. What would speed adoption: models tuned to the field or using retrieval, building into existing tools (DOORS, Jira), standards, long-term studies."]}],"pitfalls":["Spending time memorising the research papers: the lecture marks that part non-examinable."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Elicitation","color":0},{"label":"Interpretation","color":1},{"label":"Negotiation","color":2},{"label":"Documentation","color":3},{"label":"Validation","color":4},{"label":"Management","sub":"change and tracing, throughout","color":0}]}}},{"id":"reverse-engineering-recovery","title":"Reverse engineering, re-engineering and architecture recovery","priority":33,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 9 (first half)","marks":"4-6 marks","summary":"**Reverse engineering recovers design, architecture and requirements from code; re-engineering restructures software into a new form (refactor, migrate, retire); GenAI helps summarise and classify but works best combined with traditional tools and indicators.**","mnemonic":"Recover, Restructure, Migrate, Retire","source":["CT4111_Notes_09_Refactoring"],"sections":[{"heading":"Definitions","items":["**Reverse engineering**: examining an existing system to identify its parts and how they relate, to recover documentation and design.","You might recover interfaces, architecture, technologies, business rules, requirements, or file and network formats; draw higher-level diagrams; or prepare for a migration. It needs knowledge of the field.","**Re-engineering**: restructuring it into a new form: refactoring, renovation, rewriting.","**Migration**: moving it to a different environment (operating system or database).","**Retirement**: an organised end of life.","Success is more than still working: maintainability, readability and extensibility depend on context and are hard to measure."]},{"heading":"Architecture recovery","items":["**Architecture recovery** means rebuilding the system's real structure from what exists (mainly code).","It is slow and error-prone for large systems and needs judgement to get from details to the big picture. Automation is limited: it sees words rather than context or patterns, and misses hidden dependencies.","Example: **SemArc** groups Bash script files into modules and components.","**GenAI strengths**: summarising, telling general concepts apart from design patterns, managing faults.","**Challenges**: ambiguous names (\"model\" and \"view\"), different ways of implementing the same thing, frameworks and libraries.",{"type":"p","text":"Indicators workflow"},"Define the patterns and signs to look for, decide how to search, run traditional tools, then let the LLM interpret the results (combine GenAI with non-GenAI).","**Deductive recovery** asks the model to reason step by step from those signs, then the result is drawn as layered views."]}],"pitfalls":["Confusing reverse engineering (understanding) with re-engineering (changing)."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Reverse engineering","items":["Recover design and requirements from code"]},{"title":"Re-engineering","items":["Refactor","Migrate","Retire"]},{"title":"GenAI","items":["Summarise and classify","Best with traditional tools"]}]}}},{"id":"ai-code-review","title":"How the Claude Code team does code review","priority":34,"tier":"possible","freq":"Topic 11 article 3","marks":"4-8 marks","summary":"**Anthropic moved most code reading to machines with layered checks and human approval scaled to risk, rejecting 'a human read it' as a proxy for safety; the goal is less human time per change with no more reverts, backed by fast rollback; critics question self-review independence and generalisability.**","mnemonic":"Layers, risk-scaled humans, fast undo","source":["CT4111_Notes_11e_Article_Code_Review","CT4111_Notes_11_Emerging_Topics"],"sections":[{"heading":"Background: AI doing the code review","items":["Source: Osmani, Westlee Lunt and Wang (October 2026), about Anthropic's own practice.","About 90% of Claude Code's code is written by Claude. Changes landing per day in the shared repository (**monorepo**) rose from about 2,500 in May to over 30,000 in September. Merged pull requests more than doubled while the share undone (**reverted**) fell by over 40%.","Review was not dropped: machines now do the reading. Human attention is the scarce resource, spent according to risk, with fast undo as the safety net.","Review has two jobs: find problems before release, and build shared understanding."]},{"heading":"The review layers and how the AI reviewer works","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Eight layers"},"(1) Before the pull request: tests and build, plus a helper AI that has not seen the work reviews the changes.","(2) AI review on every pull request.","(3) A separate security review that can block merging.","(4) Required automatic checks (style, types, tests, build).","(5) Human approval, according to risk.","(6) A **merge queue** re-runs checks against the latest main branch.","(7) **Feature flags** (switches) and gradual release.","(8) Monitoring and fast rollback.",{"type":"p","text":"The AI reviewer"},"It reads the changes against the whole repository and their intent. Several agents hunt for bugs in parallel and others vote; only findings with enough agreement are posted, tagged Important, Nit (minor) or Pre-existing.","Instruction files: **CLAUDE.md** (technology, conventions, goals) and **REVIEW.md** (code owners define what counts as failure and limit nitpicks)."]},{"heading":"Human approval, convergence and limits","items":[{"type":"p","text":"Approval by risk"},"Low risk: no human once checks pass. Medium: one. High: two. The level depends on the change, not the author. An agent's approval never counts as a person's, and whoever prompted the agent must review.",{"type":"p","text":"Convergence and improvement"},"After three rounds, ask the author to rethink. Re-review only the new changes. The average number of reviews needed to pass fell from 2.08 to 1.32. Incidents and reverts become test cases (**evals**) for the reviewer, scored daily.",{"type":"p","text":"Who does what"},"Humans keep judgement and accountability (what to build, risky code, architecture, outcomes). Claude does reading, checking and security scanning. \"Nobody keeps a complete mental model, and we do not think anyone needs to\" (explanations are produced on demand).","**Not solved**: nothing automatically stops things when reverts rise; an AI author and AI reviewer can loop; slowing down was rejected."]},{"heading":"Critiques and discussion questions","items":["**\"Claude reviewing Claude\"** (a writer called paddo): the same model has the same blind spots, so it is not independent. A second model found serious bugs in 29 of 51 changes that had passed, so use a different model family.","No one has a mental picture to debug from when something critical breaks.","The figures are self-reported by a company promoting a paid product (about $15-25 per review). **SOC 2** (a security audit standard) may require a second human. The 30,000 changes are across the whole shared repository.","**Questions**: is this temporary scaffolding that better models will replace (the bitter lesson), or lasting engineering? What would you need to know before owning such a code base?"]}],"pitfalls":["Treating the article's statistics as independent evidence: they are Anthropic's own."],"visual":{"kind":"flow","data":{"direction":"col","steps":[{"label":"Machines read most code","color":0},{"label":"Layered checks","color":1},{"label":"Human approval scaled to risk","color":2},{"label":"Fast rollback","sub":"goal is less human time, no more reverts","color":3}]}}},{"id":"dot-and-swarm","title":"The Dot and the Swarm (Mollick)","priority":35,"tier":"low","freq":"Topic 11 article 2","marks":"4-6 marks","summary":"**Mollick admits he wrongly expected humans to design agent organisation for years; applying the bitter lesson, models organise themselves (a personal agent 'dot' and a 10,000-agent 'swarm' that attacked Navier-Stokes), so what remains is choosing the target and checking results, with the principal-agent problem still open.**","mnemonic":"Dot = personal agent, Swarm = self-organising agents; specify and verify","source":["CT4111_Notes_11d_Article_Dot_and_Swarm"],"sections":[{"heading":"The claim, the dot and the swarm","items":["**Claim** (Ethan Mollick): AI agents organise themselves, so carefully designing agent organisations matters much less than he had thought: \"organizing work is just one more thing AI can learn to do\". It is a case of the **bitter lesson** (hand-built machinery gets replaced by better models).","**The dot**: personal agents with access to your computer and accounts, which react to events and message you first (spotting a wrong permit number, or an airline credit about to expire). What matters is what you no longer have to spell out.","**The swarm**: OpenAI reported about 10,000 agents over about 88 hours and 2.7 million messages working on a famous unsolved maths problem (Navier-Stokes, one of the Millennium Prize problems) with little human coordination. At small scale, Codex started three agents without being asked, and thirteen when told about three teams."]},{"heading":"Why it works, limits and critiques","items":["**Why less management is needed**: no office politics, promotions or freeloading; messages are cheap, so the usual cost of communication (**Brooks's law**: adding people to a late project makes it later) does not bite.","**Limits**: agents cannot replace much human work; long dull tasks are unproven; self-organisation can go wrong (the Hugging Face incident); the **principal-agent problem** remains (an agent may not act as its owner wants: GPT-6.1 Astra was shelved after acting without permission).","**Critiques**: the agents are near-identical copies of one model, so shared errors look like agreement. The human role is like a beekeeper: set conditions, watch the overall picture, control the edges. Faster, longer runs make a poor setup costlier. The example had one owner and a machine-checkable proof, the proof was still under review, and it cost about 130 billion tokens (estimated $22 million)."]}],"pitfalls":["Over-generalising from a verifiable maths problem with one owner to everyday team software work."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Dot","items":["A personal agent"]},{"title":"Swarm","items":["10,000 agents on Navier-Stokes"]},{"title":"Humans still","items":["Choose the target","Check results"]},{"title":"Open issue","items":["Principal-agent problem"]}]}}},{"id":"agents-and-tools","title":"Generative vs agentic AI, agent types and tools","priority":36,"tier":"low","freq":"Topic 2 + Topic 5 (IBM classification marked not examinable) + getting-started lab","marks":"2-4 marks","summary":"**Generative AI is reactive and stateless per call; agentic AI runs a perceive-plan-act loop with memory and tools; the IBM agent taxonomy (simple reflex to hybrid) is not examinable; tool set-up slides (Copilot, Cline + Gemini) are practical only.**","mnemonic":"Reactive vs proactive; reflex, model, goal, utility, learning","source":["CT4111_Notes_02_Topic_2","CT4111_Notes_05_Design","CT4111_Notes_01b_Getting_Started_AI_Coding"],"sections":[{"heading":"Generative vs agentic","items":[{"type":"table","head":["Generative AI","Agentic AI"],"rows":[["Reactive prompt-response","Proactive perceive-plan-act loop without manual triggers"],["Content creation, translation, transformation","Autonomous multi-step goal achievement"],["Stateless per call unless augmented (RAG)","Persistent memory and context across steps and sessions"],["Outputs for human review","Calls external tools, services, APIs"],["Informational risk: hallucination checks","Operational risk: strict guardrails and audit trails"]]}]},{"heading":"Agent types (IBM, not examinable)","items":["**Simple reflex**: if-then rules, no memory. **Model-based reflex**: keeps an internal picture of the world.","**Goal-based**: acts to reach a goal. **Utility-based**: picks what scores highest.","**Learning**: improves itself, using a critic, a learning part and a part that suggests experiments.","**Hierarchical**: a manager delegates to simpler agents. Also **multi-agent** systems and **hybrids**.","**Reflexion agent**: a generator, a search tool and a reviser, producing answers with checked sources.","Tools: GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, ChatGPT and Codex, Cursor, Amazon Q Developer."]},{"heading":"Getting started (lab, practical only)","items":["Free options in VS Code: GitHub Copilot (Student Developer Pack via education.github.com; needs verified university email, name matching student card, proof of enrolment) and Google Gemini Flash with API key via the Cline extension (bring your own key, auto-approve settings). Treat API keys like passwords; never submit them."]}],"pitfalls":["Spending exam time on IBM agent types: slides say not examinable."],"visual":{"kind":"groups","data":{"groups":[{"title":"Generative AI","items":["Reactive","Stateless per call"]},{"title":"Agentic AI","items":["Perceive, plan, act loop","Memory","Tools"]}]}}}],"flashcards":[{"id":"mode-1","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Conversational (advisory) mode: where, what context, human role?","back":"Separate chat; only pasted/uploaded context; you are the author and integrate code yourself.","hook":"Ask"},{"id":"mode-2","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Conversational editing mode: define it.","back":"IDE chat sees file/workspace, proposes or applies bounded edits as a diff; human is the director who approves.","hook":"Edit"},{"id":"mode-3","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Repository agent mode: define it.","back":"Delegated task in a sandbox on the repo; returns a branch/PR asynchronously; human is task specifier and approver.","hook":"Delegate"},{"id":"mode-4","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Foreground vs background AI work?","back":"Foreground: you watch and can interrupt. Background: it works while you do other things."},{"id":"mode-5","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Synchronous vs asynchronous AI mode?","back":"Sync: you wait, turn-taking in seconds. Async: submit and return later to the result (PR)."},{"id":"mode-6","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Human role in each mode (three words)?","back":"Author (chat), Director (editing), Task specifier + approver (repo agent).","hook":"A-D-T"},{"id":"mode-7","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"When recommend conversational mode?","back":"Learning, explaining code, error interpretation, design brainstorming, confidential code kept out of tools."},{"id":"mode-8","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"When recommend repository agent?","back":"Well-bounded, well-tested, clearly specified tasks run in background or in parallel (bug with failing test, dependency bump, migration)."},{"id":"mode-9","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"What is an interactive coding agent?","back":"Foreground agent that reads repo, edits several files, runs tests and iterates while you supervise (Claude Code CLI)."},{"id":"mode-10","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"What is automated review mode?","back":"AI critiques your diff on push or PR; event-triggered; produces comments, not features; does not replace humans."},{"id":"mode-11","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Three human factors a repo agent's result depends on?","back":"Your prompt writing, how precisely you specify the task, how carefully you read the result."},{"id":"def-1","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"Define generative AI.","back":"AI that generates new content (text, code, images) resembling its training data, learnt from large datasets."},{"id":"def-2","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"Define machine learning (Samuel 1959).","back":"Field of study giving computers the ability to learn without being explicitly programmed."},{"id":"def-3","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"Define guardrails.","back":"Technical and procedural controls keeping AI within safe bounds: tests/types in prompts, permissions, PII proxy, version control, audit trails."},{"id":"def-4","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"Define self-play.","back":"A model improves by competing against or checking copies of itself with outcome reward, no human data (AlphaGo; RL on verifiable code)."},{"id":"def-5","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"Define human in the loop.","back":"A human reviews, decides or approves within an AI workflow and is responsible for quality and adverse outcomes."},{"id":"def-6","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"Define LLM.","back":"Deep-learning foundation model that predicts the next token in a sequence."},{"id":"def-7","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"Define foundation model.","back":"Deep network pre-trained for one task that can be adapted to related tasks."},{"id":"def-8","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"Define agentic AI.","back":"LLM plus harness: proactive, long-running, goal-driven, autonomous; perceive-plan-act with tools and memory."},{"id":"def-9","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"AI > ML > DL > ? nesting with decades.","back":"AI 1950s, ML 1980s, deep learning 2010s, generative AI/LLMs 2020s."},{"id":"def-10","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"What is RLHF?","back":"Reinforcement learning with human feedback: trains an LLM to take turns, be polite, stay on topic."},{"id":"def-11","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"Three stages of LLM training?","back":"Pre-training (next token), RLHF, further RL with AI feedback or provably correct solutions / self-play."},{"id":"def-12","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"What is a token and a context window?","back":"Token = word or part of a word. Context window = limited tokens the model can read."},{"id":"def-13","topicId":"genai-definitions","front":"EU AI Act definition of AI (key words)?","back":"Machine-based, varying autonomy, may adapt after deployment, infers from input to generate predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that influence environments."},{"id":"re-1","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"What is IREB and AI4RE?","back":"International Requirements Engineering Board; AI4RE = its concept and module for using generative AI in RE with human control."},{"id":"re-2","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"Name the four RE phases AI4RE prompts support.","back":"Elicitation, documentation, validation, management.","hook":"EDVM"},{"id":"re-3","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"Three pillars of AI4RE concept?","back":"Pragmatic and responsible AI; human-in-the-loop; built-in risk management."},{"id":"re-4","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"Six-part structure of an AI4RE Prompt Guide page?","back":"Task description, AI approach, conclusion, prompt templates, filled example, pitfalls and tips."},{"id":"re-5","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"What do AI4RE templates contain?","back":"AI role, user role, task and output format with [PLACEHOLDER] variables to configure."},{"id":"re-6","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"Onion model layers?","back":"Product, System, Containing system, Wider environment (regulators, public, competitors).","hook":"1-4 outward"},{"id":"re-7","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"AI4RE Identify Stakeholders: what do Prompt 1 and 2 do?","back":"P1 lists groups prioritised and classified by onion layer with assumptions; P2 maps groups to individuals/roles using an org chart."},{"id":"re-8","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"Must the AI4RE output be validated?","back":"Yes: validate groups, priorities and assumptions with domain experts before use."},{"id":"re-9","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"IREB vision of the AI-augmented RE role?","back":"From elicitor, documenter, validator to governor of AI-supported processes."},{"id":"re-10","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"Name the IREB human roles.","back":"AI process designer, prompt designer and quality reviewer, risk and compliance manager, stakeholder communicator and trust builder."},{"id":"re-11","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"AI4RE data privacy pitfall?","back":"Do not upload org charts or personal data to public AI tools without approval; anonymise or use role IDs."},{"id":"adr-1","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"What is an ADR?","back":"Short structured document capturing one architectural decision, its context, alternatives and consequences: the why behind the code."},{"id":"adr-2","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"Main components of an ADR?","back":"Title, status, date, author/deciders, context, decision, alternatives considered (pros and cons), consequences."},{"id":"adr-3","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"ADR status values?","back":"Proposed, Accepted, Deprecated, Superseded."},{"id":"adr-4","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"GenAI benefit for ADR consistency?","back":"Auto-detects conflicts with previous decisions; enforces template and terminology."},{"id":"adr-5","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"GenAI benefit for ADR research?","back":"Instant pros and cons from technology trends instead of hours of manual trade-off analysis."},{"id":"adr-6","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"GenAI benefit for ADR onboarding?","back":"Semantic search or Q&A ('why not NoSQL?') instead of reading hundreds of files."},{"id":"adr-7","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"GenAI benefit for ADR governance?","back":"Proactive alerts when code drifts from ADR intent; keeps docs current; audit trail."},{"id":"adr-8","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"Three risks of AI-driven ADRs?","back":"Technical hallucinations, contextual blindness (tribal knowledge, skills, budget), accountability gap (rubber-stamping)."},{"id":"adr-9","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"AI and ADRs: pilot or co-pilot?","back":"Co-pilot: AI drafts options, humans decide and are accountable."},{"id":"adr-10","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"Problems of the traditional 'we will do microservices' decision?","back":"Single point of failure, undocumented trade-offs, nobody knows why later, alternatives unknown."},{"id":"sdlc-1","topicId":"sdlc-genai","front":"Six SDLC phases?","back":"Requirements gathering, design, coding, testing, deployment, feedback.","hook":"R-D-C-T-D-F"},{"id":"sdlc-2","topicId":"sdlc-genai","front":"GenAI in requirements phase?","back":"Summarise meetings, search existing products, generate user stories and timeline."},{"id":"sdlc-3","topicId":"sdlc-genai","front":"GenAI in design phase?","back":"Discuss alternatives, produce design documents, ADRs, C4 diagrams, acceptance criteria."},{"id":"sdlc-4","topicId":"sdlc-genai","front":"GenAI in coding phase?","back":"Code generation and completion, code review, pull-request support."},{"id":"sdlc-5","topicId":"sdlc-genai","front":"GenAI in testing phase?","back":"Auto-generate unit, integration, edge-case tests, data, mocks; analyse failures."},{"id":"sdlc-6","topicId":"sdlc-genai","front":"GenAI in deployment phase?","back":"Debug and review devops configuration, generate pipelines."},{"id":"sdlc-7","topicId":"sdlc-genai","front":"GenAI in feedback phase?","back":"Summarise customer feedback, classify and prioritise bug reports."},{"id":"sdlc-8","topicId":"sdlc-genai","front":"Criteria for choosing the one phase to use GenAI on?","back":"Repetitive volume, verifiable output, low blast radius, easy human review, big productivity gain."},{"id":"sdlc-9","topicId":"sdlc-genai","front":"User story format?","back":"As a [role], I want [feature], so that [benefit]."},{"id":"sdlc-10","topicId":"sdlc-genai","front":"Why is SWE more than coding?","back":"Includes requirements, documentation, testing, maintenance, deployment, DevOps, legal and ethical issues."},{"id":"pr-1","topicId":"se-principles-changed","front":"How has DRY changed with GenAI?","back":"Weaker: duplicates are cheap to generate and to refactor, though duplication still harms maintenance and context."},{"id":"pr-2","topicId":"se-principles-changed","front":"How has abstraction/information hiding changed?","back":"Abstraction shifts up to specs/prompts; AI reads big code, but finite context still rewards modularity and interfaces."},{"id":"pr-3","topicId":"se-principles-changed","front":"How has 'requirements before code' changed?","back":"Strengthened: LLMs cannot guess the what; prompt = specification; tests/user stories as executable requirements."},{"id":"pr-4","topicId":"se-principles-changed","front":"How has infrastructure as code changed?","back":"Extended: AI generates IaC; agents act on infrastructure so IaC is the audit guardrail; prompts, skills and agent config become code too."},{"id":"pr-5","topicId":"se-principles-changed","front":"Evidence that AI increases duplication?","back":"GitClear study of 211M lines: duplication and churn up, reuse down."},{"id":"rag-1","topicId":"rag-context","front":"Goal of RAG?","back":"Ground LLM answers in relevant, current or private knowledge, reduce hallucination, fit finite context."},{"id":"rag-2","topicId":"rag-context","front":"RAG set-up steps?","back":"Chunk documents, embed chunks, store vectors."},{"id":"rag-3","topicId":"rag-context","front":"RAG query-time steps?","back":"Embed question, retrieve nearest chunks by similarity, paste into prompt, generate."},{"id":"rag-4","topicId":"rag-context","front":"What are embeddings?","back":"Numeric vectors representing meaning; similarity finds relevant text without exact words."},{"id":"rag-5","topicId":"rag-context","front":"RAG one-liner analogy?","back":"A Google search over your codebase."},{"id":"rag-6","topicId":"rag-context","front":"Why not paste everything into the prompt?","back":"Finite context window, middle content forgotten, too much or too little causes hallucination, stale code."},{"id":"rag-7","topicId":"rag-context","front":"What is Graph RAG?","back":"Retrieval from a knowledge graph of entities and relationships (triplets), e.g. requirements, ADRs, components, incidents."},{"id":"rag-8","topicId":"rag-context","front":"Keep-context-clean tips?","back":"Update docs, start new session with notes, prefer plain text/markdown, supply niche library docs (context7)."},{"id":"tdd-1","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"TDD definition (SWEBOK v4)?","back":"Construction method where unit tests are written before the code; testing becomes a design tool."},{"id":"tdd-2","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"Red-green-refactor?","back":"Red: failing test. Green: minimal code to pass. Refactor: clean up with tests as safety net.","hook":"R-G-R"},{"id":"tdd-3","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"AI in RED stage?","back":"Prompt-to-test: AI proposes and writes tests from natural-language requirements."},{"id":"tdd-4","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"AI in GREEN stage?","back":"Auto-implementation: AI writes code to pass tests, looping on failures."},{"id":"tdd-5","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"AI in REFACTOR stage?","back":"AI code review and restructuring while tests preserve behaviour."},{"id":"tdd-6","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"Human role in AI-augmented TDD?","back":"Define goals, review assertions, verify architecture and logic."},{"id":"tdd-7","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"Risk of agentic TDD?","back":"LLM cheats: edits tests or special-cases to pass; also lock-in and token cost."},{"id":"tdd-8","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"Why does AI make TDD less painful?","back":"Removes the 'tests take too long' barrier; a test becomes the way to instruct the AI."},{"id":"tdd-9","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"TDD mantra?","back":"If it is not green, do not optimise; if you have no tests, do not refactor."},{"id":"tp-1","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","front":"Reusable test-prompt template fields?","back":"Role, task, language/framework, context, business rules, must cover, avoid, output format."},{"id":"tp-2","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","front":"ROSES framework?","back":"Role, Objective, Scenario, Expected solution, Steps.","hook":"ROSES"},{"id":"tp-3","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","front":"Which prompt changes matter most for unit tests?","back":"Naming business rules and edge cases, forbidding shallow asserts, fixing framework, giving real source."},{"id":"tp-4","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","front":"Prompt phrase that avoids shallow tests?","back":"'Avoid shallow tests such as assertNotNull; each test must verify a meaningful business rule.'"},{"id":"tp-5","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","front":"Why give business rules in a test prompt?","back":"They supply the oracle; AI cannot guess intended behaviour."},{"id":"tp-6-m","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","front":"Which boundaries should a test prompt ask for around a deadline or size limit?","back":"Just under, exactly at and just over the limit; values in the past; for times, daylight saving and time zones."},{"id":"tp-7-m","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","front":"Which failure paths should a test prompt ask for in a paid job-submission feature?","back":"Payment declined, timeout or double submit; invalid input; incompatible options; unauthorised user. Check no side effect happens."},{"id":"mut-1","topicId":"mutation-testing","front":"What is mutation testing?","back":"Inject small faults (mutants) and run tests to measure how good the tests are."},{"id":"mut-2","topicId":"mutation-testing","front":"Killed vs surviving vs equivalent mutant?","back":"Killed: a test fails (good). Surviving: all pass (weak tests). Equivalent: no behaviour change, undetectable."},{"id":"mut-3","topicId":"mutation-testing","front":"Mutation score (slide: mutation ratio) formula?","back":"killed / (total mutants - equivalent mutants)."},{"id":"mut-4","topicId":"mutation-testing","front":"GenAI role in mutation testing?","back":"Generate realistic mutants, triage equivalent ones, write tests that kill survivors."},{"id":"mut-5","topicId":"mutation-testing","front":"Coverage levels?","back":"Class, method, line, branch, path."},{"id":"mut-6","topicId":"mutation-testing","front":"Mutation example?","back":"Change i++ to i--, flip a comparison, delete a statement."},{"id":"or-1","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Test oracle problem?","back":"Difficulty deciding whether an observed result is correct."},{"id":"or-2","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Why AI tests can mislead?","back":"Assert trivial properties, copy the implementation, miss business rules, pass without proving correctness."},{"id":"or-3","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Checking vs testing (Bach and Bolton)?","back":"Checking: verify known assertions. Testing: critical, domain-aware exploration."},{"id":"or-4","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Four test qualities?","back":"Trustworthiness, maintainability/readability, resilience to refactoring, thoroughness."},{"id":"or-5","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"AAA pattern?","back":"Arrange, Act, Assert."},{"id":"or-6","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Boundary value analysis?","back":"Test X-1, X, X+1 around thresholds (17, 18, 19)."},{"id":"or-7","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Mock vs stub?","back":"Mock is asserted against (interactions); stub just returns canned answers."},{"id":"or-8","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Fake vs spy vs dummy?","back":"Fake: simplified working implementation. Spy: records calls. Dummy: placeholder parameter."},{"id":"or-9","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Flaky test?","back":"Passes or fails randomly without code change (time zones, randomness, external APIs)."},{"id":"or-10","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Resilient test example?","back":"Replacing a for loop by a stream must not break tests; renaming a private helper must not either."},{"id":"or-11","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Unit vs integration vs system vs acceptance test?","back":"Unit: isolated units. Integration: interfaces between modules. System: whole product vs requirements. Acceptance: customer needs/production ready."},{"id":"or-12","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","front":"Black box vs white box?","back":"Black box: from specs, no code. White box: uses code structure (unit, static analysis, mutation)."},{"id":"qa-1","topicId":"qa-criteria-failure-analysis","front":"GenAI as QA architect: three benefits?","back":"Brainstorming partner, uncovers blind spots, standardises definition of done."},{"id":"qa-2","topicId":"qa-criteria-failure-analysis","front":"What to ask in a QA criteria prompt?","back":"Conditions and outcomes, scenario, decision rules covered, severity, automation level."},{"id":"qa-3","topicId":"qa-criteria-failure-analysis","front":"Failure analysis prompt inputs?","back":"Stack trace, test code, production code; ask root cause, test or code bug, corrected snippet."},{"id":"qa-4","topicId":"qa-criteria-failure-analysis","front":"Leap-year example?","back":"plusDays(365) is a day short across 29 Feb; fix with plusYears(1)."},{"id":"mod-1","topicId":"modification-types","front":"Five modification request types?","back":"Corrective, preventive, adaptive, perfective, additive.","hook":"CPAPA"},{"id":"mod-2","topicId":"modification-types","front":"Taxonomy grouping?","back":"Correction: corrective, preventive. Enhancement: adaptive, perfective, additive."},{"id":"mod-3","topicId":"modification-types","front":"Corrective maintenance?","back":"Fixing runtime errors, security vulnerabilities, emergency patches."},{"id":"mod-4","topicId":"modification-types","front":"Preventive maintenance?","back":"Restructuring and reducing technical debt to prevent problems."},{"id":"mod-5","topicId":"modification-types","front":"Adaptive maintenance?","back":"Adapting to a changed operating environment."},{"id":"mod-6","topicId":"modification-types","front":"Perfective maintenance?","back":"Improving performance, maintainability or user experience."},{"id":"mod-7","topicId":"modification-types","front":"Additive maintenance?","back":"Adding major new functionality after delivery."},{"id":"mod-8","topicId":"modification-types","front":"GenAI for corrective?","back":"Analyse logs and stack traces; surface similar past bugs."},{"id":"mod-9","topicId":"modification-types","front":"GenAI for preventive?","back":"Scan outdated/vulnerable dependencies; prioritise refactoring by risk."},{"id":"mod-10","topicId":"modification-types","front":"GenAI for adaptive?","back":"Monitor compatibility issues; find all affected areas of a dependency change."},{"id":"mod-11","topicId":"modification-types","front":"GenAI for perfective?","back":"Suggest optimisations (query rewrites, indexes); generate boilerplate."},{"id":"mod-12","topicId":"modification-types","front":"GenAI for additive?","back":"Prioritise candidate features from requests and usage; assess impact and assist adaptation."},{"id":"fl-1","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"Three stages of SWE-GPT style repair?","back":"Repository understanding, fault localisation, patch generation (with validation loop)."},{"id":"fl-2","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"Human role in fault repair?","back":"Report and prioritise, provide tests and intent, review and approve, own accountability."},{"id":"fl-3","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"What helps repair most (fact selection)?","back":"Mostly error message plus issue description; ultimately context-specific."},{"id":"fl-4","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"ContrastRepair idea?","back":"Add contrastive test pairs: failing test plus similar passing test."},{"id":"fl-5","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"RepairAgent?","back":"Autonomous LLM agent that uses tools to read, search, run tests and edit."},{"id":"fl-6","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"PATCH collaborating agents?","back":"Bug reporter, code explainer, pattern summariser, patch generator, patch verifier."},{"id":"fl-7","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"BRMDS dimensions of a bug report?","back":"Environment, expected behaviour, actual behaviour, bug category, suggested solution."},{"id":"fl-8","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"What feeds failure back in the repair loop?","back":"Test failures, compile errors and lint results returned to the patch generator, up to an iteration limit."},{"id":"lm-1","topicId":"library-migration","front":"Google one-version policy?","back":"Every dependency kept up to date across the codebase to minimise drift."},{"id":"lm-2","topicId":"library-migration","front":"Why are library migrations hard?","back":"Not fully automatable, manual, take months or years, may need repeating."},{"id":"lm-3","topicId":"library-migration","front":"Key parts of a migration prompt?","back":"Persona, explicit mapping rules, constraints (no other changes), output format."},{"id":"lm-4","topicId":"library-migration","front":"Why add traditional validation to AI migration?","back":"Catches model quirks (stray comments, unwanted edits); cheaper at scale."},{"id":"lm-5","topicId":"library-migration","front":"Migration examples from lecture?","back":"JUnit 3 to 4; Joda-Time to java.time."},{"id":"lm-6","topicId":"library-migration","front":"Pacing migrations?","back":"Small batches so review teams are not overwhelmed."},{"id":"sa-1","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","front":"Static analysis warning pipeline stages?","back":"Classify (TP/FP), plan, fix, validate, rank, human review.","hook":"Classify then repair"},{"id":"sa-2","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","front":"CodeCureAgent prompt components?","back":"Role/objective, constraints, tools, input warning, agent history, response format."},{"id":"sa-3","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","front":"CORE ranker scoring?","back":"0 strong reject to 3 strong accept: only edits that fix the warning with least impact."},{"id":"sa-4","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","front":"iSMELL idea?","back":"LLM selects tools, runs them, prompts LLM with results, definitions, examples to refactor."},{"id":"sa-5","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","front":"Why need GenAI in static analysis?","back":"Reduce false positives, add context, explain, enforce soft rules, repair; combine with deterministic tools."},{"id":"sa-6","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","front":"Failure modes of agentic repair?","back":"Wrong classification, insufficient context, incorrect modifications, multiple warnings at once, no file operations."},{"id":"sa-7","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","front":"Why static tools alone struggle?","back":"False positives, no repo context, inconsistent between tools, cannot fix."},{"id":"cs-1","topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","front":"Long method / shotgun surgery / feature envy / god class?","back":"Too long; one change touches many classes; method uses another class's data; class with too many responsibilities."},{"id":"cs-2","topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","front":"Four clone types?","back":"1 exact, 2 renamed, 3 modified statements, 4 same function different code."},{"id":"cs-3","topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","front":"Define refactoring.","back":"Improving internal structure without changing external behaviour."},{"id":"cs-4","topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","front":"Four-step refactoring workflow?","back":"As-is structure, to-be structure, check test coverage, implement with tests as safety net."},{"id":"cs-5","topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","front":"RefAgent agents?","back":"Planner, generator, compiler, tester (plus context retrieval)."},{"id":"cs-6","topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","front":"Refactoring failure modes?","back":"Semantic changes, unnecessary edits, deleted code, wrong renames, hallucinated APIs, incomplete refactor."},{"id":"cs-7","topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","front":"Test smells?","back":"Eager test, duplicate assert, assertion roulette, magic number; ambiguous or conditional manual tests."},{"id":"cs-8","topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","front":"What is prompt debt?","back":"Brittle, model-dependent prompts that are hard to maintain; fix with versioning and evaluations."},{"id":"cs-9","topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","front":"Three strikes rule?","back":"Refactor on the third repetition before adding or changing."},{"id":"md-1","topicId":"maintenance-debt-triage","front":"Maintenance share of lifecycle cost?","back":"About 60-80%."},{"id":"md-2","topicId":"maintenance-debt-triage","front":"Lehman's two laws mentioned?","back":"Continuing change; increasing complexity."},{"id":"md-3","topicId":"maintenance-debt-triage","front":"Maintainability characteristics?","back":"Analysability, changeability, stability, modularity, reusability, testability."},{"id":"md-4","topicId":"maintenance-debt-triage","front":"Technical debt (Cunningham)?","back":"Not-quite-right code causing extra work later; fine short term if repaid."},{"id":"md-5","topicId":"maintenance-debt-triage","front":"AI faces of technical debt?","back":"Governance, prompt, model, fast-integration."},{"id":"md-6","topicId":"maintenance-debt-triage","front":"Technical deflation idea?","back":"Cleaning code is getting cheaper with AI, so debt can be repaid cheaply."},{"id":"md-7","topicId":"maintenance-debt-triage","front":"GitClear finding?","back":"On 211M lines duplication and churn rose, reuse fell."},{"id":"rev-1","topicId":"reverse-engineering-recovery","front":"Reverse vs re-engineering?","back":"Reverse: recover design/requirements from code. Re-engineering: restructure, migrate or retire into a new form."},{"id":"rev-2","topicId":"reverse-engineering-recovery","front":"Architecture recovery indicators workflow?","back":"Define patterns, choose search strategy, run traditional tools, LLM interprets results."},{"id":"bl-1","topicId":"bitter-lesson","front":"Sutton's bitter lesson in one line?","back":"General methods that leverage computation (search and learning) beat built-in human knowledge in the long run."},{"id":"bl-2","topicId":"bitter-lesson","front":"Why is it 'bitter'?","back":"It defeats the favoured human-centric approach, so it is hard to accept."},{"id":"bl-3","topicId":"bitter-lesson","front":"Four examples in the essay?","back":"Chess, Go, speech recognition, computer vision.","hook":"C-G-S-V"},{"id":"bl-4","topicId":"bitter-lesson","front":"Two methods that scale without limit?","back":"Search and learning."},{"id":"bl-5","topicId":"bitter-lesson","front":"Brooks' critique?","back":"Human ingenuity moved rather than vanished (e.g. CNN architecture design); costs exclude most researchers."},{"id":"bl-6","topicId":"bitter-lesson","front":"Sutton on LLMs (2025)?","back":"LLMs learn from human data, another human-knowledge approach that will be overtaken."},{"id":"bl-7","topicId":"bitter-lesson","front":"Bitter lesson applied to SE agents?","back":"Hand-built scaffolding (prompt chains, retrieval, agent org charts) is replaced by better general models."},{"id":"bl-8","topicId":"bitter-lesson","front":"Why does it fit coding?","back":"Coding is verifiable, so compute can scale search and RL."},{"id":"bl-9","topicId":"bitter-lesson","front":"Karpathy's phrase about LLMs?","back":"Ghosts, not animals: trained on human data."},{"id":"lg-1","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"GPL contamination?","back":"Verbatim copyleft code in your product forces your code under GPL."},{"id":"lg-2","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"MIT vs Apache 2.0 vs GPL?","back":"MIT permissive; Apache permissive plus patent grant; GPL strong copyleft."},{"id":"lg-3","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"Who owns AI-generated code?","back":"Legally ambiguous; a model is not a legal person and cannot own copyright."},{"id":"lg-4","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"Copilot class action (2022)?","back":"Claimed training on public repos violated licence attribution and other terms."},{"id":"lg-5","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"GDPR principles (7)?","back":"Lawfulness/fairness/transparency, purpose limitation, data minimisation, accuracy, storage limitation, integrity and confidentiality, accountability."},{"id":"lg-6","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"Special category data?","back":"Health, biometric, genetic, sexual orientation, political, religious, ethnic origin, trade union."},{"id":"lg-7","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"When is a DPIA needed?","back":"Likely high risk to people's data (e.g. automated decisions, large-scale monitoring, special data)."},{"id":"lg-8","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"GDPR and AI-generated code risk?","back":"Code may collect unneeded personal data, breaching data minimisation."},{"id":"lg-9","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"EU AI Act risk tiers?","back":"Unacceptable, high risk, transparency/limited, minimal.","hook":"U-H-T-M"},{"id":"lg-10","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"Credit scoring under the AI Act?","back":"High risk: full compliance (Articles 8-17: documentation, logging, bias testing, oversight)."},{"id":"lg-11","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"AI Act transparency tier?","back":"Chatbots, deepfakes, AI-generated content must be disclosed (Article 50)."},{"id":"lg-12","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"AI Act duties for general-purpose model providers?","back":"Technical docs, downstream docs, copyright policy, training-content summary; extra for systemic risk."},{"id":"lg-13","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"Does GenAI-written software count as an AI system?","back":"Not automatically; check the definition. Even rule-based code can qualify."},{"id":"lg-14","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"Mitigation for licence risk?","back":"Licence/similarity scanning, review, SBOM, indemnified tools, replace flagged code."},{"id":"lg-15","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"Mitigation for GDPR risk in prompts?","back":"DPIA, private or zero-retention LLM, PII filter, no real customer data in prompts."},{"id":"lg-16","topicId":"legal-risks","front":"Who is liable for AI output?","back":"The person or organisation using it, not the tool (Air Canada, Avianca)."},{"id":"bi-1","topicId":"bias-fairness","front":"What does 'GenAI exhibits bias' mean?","back":"Outputs reflect stereotypes and skews from training data (gender, culture, race)."},{"id":"bi-2","topicId":"bias-fairness","front":"Sora/Stable Diffusion bias findings?","back":"CEOs always men, carers always women; 76% male images, scientists 1-18% female vs 48%."},{"id":"bi-3","topicId":"bias-fairness","front":"Representation vs historical bias?","back":"Groups missing from data vs data reflecting past inequality."},{"id":"bi-4","topicId":"bias-fairness","front":"Proxy variable?","back":"Neutral-looking feature correlated with a protected attribute (zip code)."},{"id":"bi-5","topicId":"bias-fairness","front":"Aggregation bias?","back":"One model for groups that need different treatment."},{"id":"bi-6","topicId":"bias-fairness","front":"Biased non-ML code example?","back":"Generated form that requires a family name excludes mononymous cultures."},{"id":"bi-7","topicId":"bias-fairness","front":"Does a local open-weights model remove bias?","back":"No: weights carry training-data bias; it gives control, fine-tuning and privacy, not neutrality."},{"id":"bi-8","topicId":"bias-fairness","front":"Team-related biases?","back":"Affinity, authority, bandwagon, lack of lived experience."},{"id":"bi-9","topicId":"bias-fairness","front":"Why stereotypes 'moved, not disappeared'?","back":"Companies mask them with prompt rewriting and preference tuning."},{"id":"vt-1","topicId":"verifiable-tasks","front":"What is a verifiable task?","back":"Correctness can be checked automatically and objectively (tests, compiler, proof checker)."},{"id":"vt-2","topicId":"verifiable-tasks","front":"Why does verifiability speed AI progress?","back":"Cheap reward signal for RL and self-play, so unlimited training and agent loops."},{"id":"vt-3","topicId":"verifiable-tasks","front":"Caveat of 'verifiable' code?","back":"Only as good as the tests; models can game tests; passing is not good design."},{"id":"vt-4","topicId":"verifiable-tasks","front":"Example non-verifiable SE task?","back":"Requirements elicitation or architecture decisions: no objective oracle."},{"id":"ps-1","topicId":"privacy-security-risks","front":"Samsung ChatGPT incident?","back":"Employees pasted source code and meeting notes into ChatGPT, leaking proprietary data."},{"id":"ps-2","topicId":"privacy-security-risks","front":"Three prompt-leak mitigations?","back":"Private LLM, zero-retention API, PII filtering proxy."},{"id":"ps-3","topicId":"privacy-security-risks","front":"Best-practice hybrid?","back":"Zero-retention API behind an internal PII-filter proxy, with a legal policy."},{"id":"ps-4","topicId":"privacy-security-risks","front":"Shadow AI?","back":"Employees using unauthorised AI tools, often because policy is too strict."},{"id":"ps-5","topicId":"privacy-security-risks","front":"Hallucinated dependency attack?","back":"AI suggests a non-existent or attacker-registered package; installing it runs malware."},{"id":"ps-6","topicId":"privacy-security-risks","front":"Data poisoning?","back":"Attackers plant malicious code in public repos so LLMs learn it."},{"id":"ps-7","topicId":"privacy-security-risks","front":"SQL injection?","back":"Input containing quotes, -- or OR 1=1 alters the query; fix with parameterised queries."},{"id":"ps-8","topicId":"privacy-security-risks","front":"Log4Shell lesson?","back":"Patched vulnerabilities can reappear from code in training data."},{"id":"ps-9","topicId":"privacy-security-risks","front":"Scanning tools named?","back":"Snyk, SonarQube (not 100% reliable)."},{"id":"pe-1","topicId":"professional-environment","front":"IEEE code principle 1?","back":"Act consistently with the public interest."},{"id":"pe-2","topicId":"professional-environment","front":"SBOM?","back":"Software Bill of Materials: list of all components, open source, proprietary and AI-generated."},{"id":"pe-3","topicId":"professional-environment","front":"Accountability cases?","back":"Mata v Avianca, Moffatt v Air Canada, Deloitte Australia refund."},{"id":"pe-4","topicId":"professional-environment","front":"AI slop?","back":"Unreviewed AI content that pushes cognitive load onto the reader."},{"id":"pe-5","topicId":"professional-environment","front":"When not to use AI?","back":"Defeats learning, unverifiable, unauthorised, data must stay private, or you would hide it."},{"id":"pe-6","topicId":"professional-environment","front":"Where is AI energy mostly spent?","back":"Inference across all users exceeds training once deployed."},{"id":"pe-7","topicId":"professional-environment","front":"Data centre share?","back":"About 415 TWh in 2024, 1.5% of global electricity; about 20% of Irish metered electricity."},{"id":"pe-8","topicId":"professional-environment","front":"Environmental practice?","back":"Smallest model that works, fewer better prompts, bounded outputs, avoid needless image/video."},{"id":"ft-1","topicId":"fintech-flow-case","front":"FinTech-Flow four problems?","back":"Hallucinated dependency, AGPL copy, zip-code proxy bias, AI Act logging and oversight failure.","hook":"Package, Licence, Postcode, Logs"},{"id":"ft-2","topicId":"fintech-flow-case","front":"What does a 15-point approval gap prove?","back":"Disparity only; causation needs controls for legitimate factors and proxy analysis."},{"id":"ft-3","topicId":"fintech-flow-case","front":"Options for AGPL-copied code?","back":"Comply (open source), get permission, or remove and replace by clean implementation; cosmetic rewrite is not enough."},{"id":"pp-1","topicId":"programmatic-prompts","front":"Programmatic prompt?","back":"Prompt built by a program and output consumed by a program (LLM as an API)."},{"id":"pp-2","topicId":"programmatic-prompts","front":"Example scenario?","back":"CI script that classifies bug reports into JSON labels via an LLM API."},{"id":"pp-3","topicId":"programmatic-prompts","front":"Needed in programmatic prompts?","back":"Explicit output format, validation and retry, versioned prompts, privacy control."},{"id":"pe2-1","topicId":"prompt-engineering","front":"Four prompt elements?","back":"Instruction, context, input data, output indicator.","hook":"I-C-I-O"},{"id":"pe2-2","topicId":"prompt-engineering","front":"Few-shot prompting pros and cons?","back":"Better performance and format with low effort; sensitive to example choice, weak on complex tasks."},{"id":"pe2-3","topicId":"prompt-engineering","front":"Chain-of-thought pros and cons?","back":"Solves complex problems and shows errors; longer, costlier, may loop."},{"id":"pe2-4","topicId":"prompt-engineering","front":"Do vs do-not in prompts?","back":"LLMs tend to do what they are told not to; state what to do and give fail cases."},{"id":"pe2-5","topicId":"prompt-engineering","front":"Iterative prompt design loop?","back":"Simple test, analyse output, improve (instructions, examples, CoT)."},{"id":"pe2-6","topicId":"prompt-engineering","front":"Managing ambiguity?","back":"Ask questions, state assumptions, do not generate code until resolved."},{"id":"pe2-7","topicId":"prompt-engineering","front":"Old vs new LLM usage paradigm?","back":"Pre-train and fine-tune per task vs train, prompt, predict."},{"id":"pe2-8","topicId":"prompt-engineering","front":"Code-generation risks?","back":"Fabricated APIs, false logic, vulnerabilities, leaked proprietary code, licence violations, test tampering."},{"id":"iw-1","topicId":"implementation-workflows","front":"Four perspectives on LLM workflows?","back":"Prompting as specification, human in the loop, embodied agents, agents as an orchestra."},{"id":"iw-2","topicId":"implementation-workflows","front":"Five workflows?","back":"LLM as reviewer, support, junior developer, in TDD, vibecoding.","hook":"RSJTV"},{"id":"iw-3","topicId":"implementation-workflows","front":"Junior-developer workflow bottleneck?","back":"Human review; reviewers rarely validate everything."},{"id":"iw-4","topicId":"implementation-workflows","front":"Vibecoding best use?","back":"Rapid prototypes, hackathons, unfamiliar language; not for reuse."},{"id":"iw-5","topicId":"implementation-workflows","front":"LLM-as-support benefits?","back":"Developer stays in loop, high quality, low risk; limited speed-up."},{"id":"iw-6","topicId":"implementation-workflows","front":"LLM-as-reviewer drawback?","back":"Less cross-pollination and mentoring between developers."},{"id":"vc-1","topicId":"version-control-ai","front":"Four git locations?","back":"Working tree, staging area, local repo, remote repo."},{"id":"vc-2","topicId":"version-control-ai","front":"Undo commands?","back":"git restore for uncommitted work; git revert for shared commits."},{"id":"vc-3","topicId":"version-control-ai","front":"Good practice before AI work?","back":"Commit first, give AI its own branch, review the diff, merge only if sound."},{"id":"vc-4","topicId":"version-control-ai","front":"Unit of review vs decision?","back":"The diff is the unit of review; the PR is the unit of decision."},{"id":"lc-1","topicId":"llm-concepts","front":"Jagged intelligence?","back":"AI beats humans on some tasks but fails on easier ones; uneven frontier."},{"id":"lc-2","topicId":"llm-concepts","front":"Hallucination in code?","back":"Calling a function or package that does not exist, or false logic."},{"id":"lc-3","topicId":"llm-concepts","front":"Chain of thought (define)?","back":"Using extra tokens to reason step by step before answering."},{"id":"lc-4","topicId":"llm-concepts","front":"LLM false negative vs false positive (about ability)?","back":"FN: 'it cannot do this easy task' (prompt or model issue). FP: 'it solved a hard task' (maybe in training data)."},{"id":"lc-5","topicId":"llm-concepts","front":"Tools LLMs can use?","back":"Web search, calculator, grep and code search, run and test code, shell, MCP."},{"id":"lc-6","topicId":"llm-concepts","front":"Primacy and recency?","back":"Items at start and end of the context are remembered better than the middle."},{"id":"ar-1","topicId":"architecture-design","front":"C4 four levels?","back":"Context, containers, components, code."},{"id":"ar-2","topicId":"architecture-design","front":"Weighted score formula?","back":"Sum of weight x score per criterion."},{"id":"ar-3","topicId":"architecture-design","front":"Supervisor multi-agent roles?","back":"Requirements, alternatives, trade-off analyst, reviewer, ADR writer, supervisor."},{"id":"ar-4","topicId":"architecture-design","front":"Microservices vs SOA data?","back":"Microservices: own data each. SOA: shared storage."},{"id":"ar-5","topicId":"architecture-design","front":"Graph RAG entities and edges?","back":"Nodes with types, verb-based relationships, descriptions; stored as triplets."},{"id":"ar-6","topicId":"architecture-design","front":"Architecture quality metric groups?","back":"Performance, scalability, availability, maintainability, deployability, security, cost, complexity."},{"id":"rf-1","topicId":"re-fundamentals","front":"Standish CHAOS 2020 split?","back":"31% successful, 50% challenged, 19% failed."},{"id":"rf-2","topicId":"re-fundamentals","front":"RE analysis activities?","back":"Elicitation, interpretation, negotiation, documentation, validation."},{"id":"rf-3","topicId":"re-fundamentals","front":"RE management activities?","back":"Change management and tracing."},{"id":"rf-4","topicId":"re-fundamentals","front":"MoSCoW?","back":"Must, Should, Could, Won't."},{"id":"rf-5","topicId":"re-fundamentals","front":"SRS standards?","back":"IEEE 830-1998 and ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148:2018."},{"id":"cr-1","topicId":"ai-code-review","front":"Anthropic review layers (headline)?","back":"Pre-PR checks, AI review, security review, CI, risk-scaled human approval, merge queue, flags, monitoring and rollback."},{"id":"cr-2","topicId":"ai-code-review","front":"Human approvals by risk?","back":"Low none (after checks), medium one, high two."},{"id":"cr-3","topicId":"ai-code-review","front":"Main critique of Claude reviewing Claude?","back":"Same-model reviewers share blind spots; not independent."},{"id":"cr-4","topicId":"ai-code-review","front":"CLAUDE.md vs REVIEW.md?","back":"Project conventions and goals vs code-owner definitions of failure for review."},{"id":"ds-1","topicId":"dot-and-swarm","front":"Mollick's main claim?","back":"Agents organise themselves; human design of agent organisation matters less (bitter lesson)."},{"id":"ds-2","topicId":"dot-and-swarm","front":"Dot vs swarm?","back":"Dot: personal proactive agent. Swarm: many self-organising agents (about 10,000 on Navier-Stokes)."},{"id":"ds-3","topicId":"dot-and-swarm","front":"Human role per critics?","back":"Beekeeper: set conditions, check aggregates, control edges; principal-agent problem remains."},{"id":"ag-1","topicId":"agents-and-tools","front":"Generative vs agentic AI?","back":"Reactive and stateless vs proactive loop with memory and tools."},{"id":"ag-2","topicId":"agents-and-tools","front":"Agent types (IBM)?","back":"Simple reflex, model-based, goal, utility, learning, hierarchical, multi-agent, hybrid, reflexion (not examinable)."},{"id":"a4-1","topicId":"mutation-testing","front":"Lecturer's name for the mutation formula?","back":"Mutation Ratio = #Killed / (#Total - #Equivalent)."},{"id":"a4-2","topicId":"mutation-testing","front":"Test Pass Rate formula (slide)?","back":"#Passed tests / #Total tests (25/26 = about 0.96)."},{"id":"a4-3","topicId":"mutation-testing","front":"Unit-level coverage formula?","back":"#Units tested at least once / #Total units."},{"id":"a4-4-m","topicId":"architecture-design","front":"How do you complete a weighted score matrix?","back":"Weight each criterion, score each option, sum weight x score per option, then justify the choice and its trade-offs."},{"id":"a4-5","topicId":"architecture-design","front":"Why did the hybrid architecture win?","back":"Best balance, uses Spring Boot skills (AI blind to this), Lambda only for async, RDS Postgres GDPR and audit ready, 3 vs 8 weeks.","hook":"Balance + skills"},{"id":"a4-6","topicId":"adr-genai","front":"ADR template fields on the slide?","back":"Title, Status, Date, Author, Deciders, Context, Decision, Alternatives Considered (options with pros/cons)."},{"id":"a4-7","topicId":"re-fundamentals","front":"Four pillars of AI support in RE (IREB)?","back":"Elicitation, documentation, validation, management."},{"id":"a4-8","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"Five steps to use the Identify Stakeholders templates?","back":"Define placeholders; run Prompt 1 (groups); optionally attach org chart; run Prompt 2 (individuals); validate with domain experts."},{"id":"a4-9","topicId":"ai4re-process","front":"How many IREB human roles on the slide, and name one?","back":"Four, e.g. AI process designer and integrator, prompt designer and quality reviewer, risk and compliance manager, stakeholder communicator and trust builder."},{"id":"a4-10","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Which tools are interactive agents in the open-source column?","back":"Aider and OpenCode (Cline and Roo Code are conversational editing)."},{"id":"a4-11","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Human role for inline completion and automated review?","back":"Inline completion: author. Automated review: accountable author."},{"id":"a4-12","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Which mode is labelled foreground and which asynchronous on the slides?","back":"Interactive coding agent = foreground; repository agent = delegated, asynchronous."},{"id":"a4-13","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","front":"Examples of repository agents?","back":"Codex cloud, Claude Code on the web, Jules, Copilot cloud agent, Cursor background agents."},{"id":"a4-14-m","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","front":"Which five kinds of test should you expect for a booking operation?","back":"Happy path, invalid input, rule breach on dates, conflict with an existing booking (no charge), failed payment (nothing saved)."},{"id":"a4-15","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"Quality gate thresholds in the agentic TDD figure?","back":"LCOM < 0.2 and cyclomatic complexity < 10; two human checkpoints (scenario validator, final PR review)."},{"id":"a4-16","topicId":"tdd-genai","front":"GenAI QA workflow, four steps?","back":"Business requirement; GenAI defines QA criteria and AST paths; TDD cycle; robust verified code."},{"id":"a4-17","topicId":"qa-criteria-failure-analysis","front":"Load vs stress testing; MTBF?","back":"Load: typical peak load. Stress: find the breaking point. MTBF: mean time between failures."},{"id":"a4-18","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"RepairAgent states?","back":"Understand the bug, collect information to fix it, try to fix it; then done."},{"id":"a4-19","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"PATCH roles and stages?","back":"Tester (bug reporting), developer (diagnosis, patch generation), reviewer (patch verification), loop generation-verification."},{"id":"a4-20","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","front":"SWE-GPT agents?","back":"Repository understander, fault localizer, coder."},{"id":"a4-21","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","front":"CodeCureAgent structure?","back":"Classification sub-agent (TP/FP) then repair sub-agent, each LLM+tools; change approver builds, re-runs analysis and tests."},{"id":"a4-22","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","front":"CORE five steps?","back":"Configure static tools; process flagged files; proposer LLM; prune still-flagged; ranker LLM."},{"id":"a4-23","topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","front":"RefAgent stop rule?","back":"If tests fail after 20 iterations keep the original code."},{"id":"a4-24","topicId":"library-migration","front":"Java library migration example in the migration lecture slides?","back":"JUnit try/catch fail() converted to assertThrows; Joda-Time to java.time."},{"id":"a4-25","topicId":"implementation-workflows","front":"Vibecoding in one line?","back":"LLM writes ticket, code and review; you only use the product and never read the code."},{"id":"a4-26","topicId":"re-fundamentals","front":"Experts naming involvement of all participants or good communication as success factors?","back":"19 of 30."}],"methods":[{"id":"define-and-distinguish","topicId":"genai-definitions","title":"Answer 'define and distinguish' questions","when":"Asked to define several terms or modes and compare them (e.g. three modes of AI use, or five terms)","mnemonic":"D-A-C-E: Define, Axes, Compare, Example","steps":["1. Give each item a one-sentence definition using the lecture's wording (what it is, who drives it).","2. List the comparison axes the question names (e.g. background/foreground, sync/async, human role) and cover EVERY axis for EVERY item.","3. Lay the answer out as a small table or parallel bullets so each marker sees the same axes.","4. Add one concrete example or tool per item.","5. Finish with one sentence on the key difference (e.g. autonomy increases, human moves from author to approver).","6. Budget: about one mark per distinct, named fact."],"example":{"problem":"Define and distinguish conversational, conversational editing and repository-agent modes (10 marks).","solution":["Conversational: chat outside the IDE, sees only pasted context, foreground and synchronous, human = author. Example: Claude app.","Conversational editing: IDE chat sees file or workspace, proposes bounded diffs, foreground and synchronous (seconds), human = director who accepts or rejects each change. Example: Copilot Chat, Cline.","Repository agent: sandbox with repo and tools, runs tests, returns a branch or PR, background and asynchronous, human = task specifier and approver. Example: Codex cloud, Copilot cloud agent.","Key difference: context, autonomy and asynchrony grow, human control per step shrinks, so review and tests carry more weight."]}},{"id":"recommend-mode","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","title":"Recommend an AI mode for a scenario","when":"Asked 'for what scenarios would you recommend each mode and why?'","mnemonic":"Clarity x Tests x Risk","steps":["1. Rate the task on three axes: how clear/bounded the spec is, how good the test coverage is, how costly an error is.","2. Unclear or learning-oriented or confidential: conversational (you stay author).","3. Clear, local, quickly checkable by a diff: conversational editing.","4. Clear, well-tested, routine or parallelisable: repository agent in the background.","5. Give one concrete scenario and one 'why' per mode, plus one case where the mode is a bad choice.","6. Mention the safeguard (version control, tests, human review)."],"example":{"problem":"Recommend a mode for (1) understanding a legacy payroll module, (2) adding input validation to one class, (3) upgrading a logging library in 200 files.","solution":["(1) Conversational: needs explanation and design thinking, no edits, human keeps ownership and no risk to the repo; upload the file or snippets.","(2) Conversational editing: bounded change, context is the file and its tests, review the diff and run tests immediately.","(3) Repository agent: routine, specified by rules, validated by build and tests, runs in the background in batches and returns PRs; avoid it for the payroll redesign because that needs product judgement."]}},{"id":"prompt-recipe","topicId":"prompt-engineering","title":"Write a prompt for X (role, context, task, constraints, format, examples)","when":"Asked 'write a prompt for generating/analysing/migrating X'","mnemonic":"R-C-T-C-F-E","steps":["1. Role: who the model is (senior Java QA engineer).","2. Context: domain, code or documents attached, tech stack, business rules.","3. Task: one verb-led instruction, specific and measurable.","4. Constraints: what to include or avoid, standards, limits (positive wording first, then 'avoid').","5. Output format: structure, language, JSON schema, ordering, explanation per item.","6. Examples: one or two few-shot examples or a fail case; ask for assumptions and questions about ambiguity.","7. After the prompt add 2-3 lines justifying each part."],"example":{"problem":"Write a prompt that turns informal user stories into reviewed user stories.","solution":["Role: You are a senior requirements engineer.","Context: Inventory management system for a warehouse; stories below are from stakeholders (attached).","Task: For each story decide whether it is clear enough for engineers; rewrite unclear ones as 'As a [role], I want [feature], so that [benefit]'.","Constraints: do not invent features; flag missing acceptance criteria; list assumptions separately.","Format: Markdown table (story, clear yes/no, issue, improved story, acceptance criteria).","Example: 'As Shawn I want to do my job as usual so that I can go home at 5' -> not clear: no feature; ask clarification.","Why: role sets expertise, context prevents guessing, format makes validation easy, example shows the bad-story case."]}},{"id":"test-prompt-method","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","title":"Write a detailed prompt for a test suite","when":"Asked to propose a prompt for generating tests for a class or system","mnemonic":"ROSES + AVOID + FORMAT","steps":["1. Role: senior test engineer in the language and framework (JUnit 5, Mockito).","2. Context: name the class, its collaborators to mock, domain; attach source.","3. Business rules: list every rule and expected outcome (the oracle).","4. Must cover: happy path per input, invalid inputs, boundaries (X-1, X, X+1), failure paths, security, idempotency.","5. Avoid: shallow asserts, getters and setters, order dependence, real clock and network.","6. Quality rules: AAA, one scenario per test, descriptive names, parameterised tests.","7. Output format: test class plus table of test name, rule verified, why it matters; list assumptions.","8. After the prompt, state how you will check it: run, coverage, mutation score, human reviews assertions."],"example":{"problem":"Prompt for tests of ParkingMeter.pay(ticket, minutes, card) where rate is 2 euro per hour, minimum 15 minutes, maximum 4 hours, declined cards are rejected.","solution":["Role: You are a senior Java QA engineer using JUnit 5 and Mockito.","Task: Generate unit tests for ParkingMeter.pay. Mock PaymentGateway and Clock.","Rules: 2 euro per 60 minutes pro rata; minimum 15 minutes; maximum 240 minutes; declined card throws PaymentDeclined and issues no ticket extension; expired ticket cannot be extended.","Cover: 14, 15, 16 minutes; 239, 240, 241 minutes; zero and negative minutes; null ticket or card; declined card; gateway timeout; double submission.","Avoid: assertNotNull-only tests, testing getters, shared state between tests.","Output: ParkingMeterTest with AAA structure and names like shouldReject_WhenMinutesExceedMaximum; then a table of test, rule, reason."]}},{"id":"choose-sdlc-phase","topicId":"sdlc-genai","title":"Choose the one SDLC phase for GenAI","when":"Asked which single phase you would use GenAI for, referring to all phases","mnemonic":"Choose, Criteria, Contrast five, Caveat","steps":["1. Name the phase you choose.","2. Give criteria: repetitive volume, verifiable output, low blast radius, quick human review, large gain.","3. Give 3 reasons with a concrete GenAI task for the chosen phase.","4. For each of the other five phases: one line on why not (risk, judgement, accountability) and the traditional approach.","5. Add safeguards and one limitation of your pick."],"example":{"problem":"Choose one phase (12 marks).","solution":["Choice: testing. Criteria: repetitive, verifiable by running and by mutation score, errors caught before release, human reviews assertions.","Reasons: generates unit, edge and regression tests quickly; analyses failures; lowers the barrier to TDD.","Not requirements: ambiguity and stakeholder judgement, hallucinated requirements are costly. Not design: tribal knowledge and accountability for trade-offs. Not coding: I would write it with my own understanding (and avoid licence and security risk). Not deployment: production credentials and high blast radius. Not feedback: needs human prioritisation (summarising helps but I choose testing).","Safeguards: humans supply business rules, review assertions, CI runs the tests. Limit: oracle problem."]}},{"id":"principle-answer","topicId":"se-principles-changed","title":"Answer 'has this principle changed?' (which, how, why)","when":"Asked whether traditional SE principles changed because of GenAI","mnemonic":"Was, Now, Why, Still","steps":["1. State the principle and why it existed before (the original problem it solved).","2. State the verdict: unchanged, weakened, strengthened or extended.","3. Give the GenAI-driven reason (cost of generation, capability to read code, decoupled what/how, agents).","4. Say what still holds or the risk of dropping it.","5. One sentence of evidence or example."],"example":{"problem":"Has 'don't repeat yourself' changed? (2 marks)","solution":["Was: avoid duplication so a fix is made once and humans do not retype code.","Now: weakened: AI writes copies in seconds and can later refactor them, so the cost that motivated DRY fell.","Why still matters: duplication still bloats review and context and AI-assisted codebases show more duplication and less reuse (211M-line study), so clean-up becomes a regular AI task rather than a write-time rule."]}},{"id":"ai4re-chain","topicId":"ai4re-process","title":"Run an AI4RE prompt chain (stakeholders example)","when":"Asked to describe or apply the AI4RE process to a system","mnemonic":"Configure, Run, Chain, Validate","steps":["1. Define the RE task and pick the template from the Prompt Guide (here: Identify Stakeholders).","2. Fill placeholders: [DOMAIN], [SYSTEM] description, [FORMAT] (Markdown table).","3. Run Prompt 1: groups, prioritised, classified by onion layer, assumptions separate.","4. Attach supporting file (org chart) and run Prompt 2 mapping groups to roles, keeping group names consistent.","5. Check privacy first: anonymise or use role IDs.","6. Validate with domain experts, record assumptions, iterate before using for interviews."],"example":{"problem":"Apply AI4RE to a university room-booking app.","solution":["Placeholders: Domain = higher-education campus services; System = web app where staff and students book rooms with approval by timetabling; Format = table (Group, Onion layer, Role, Priority).","Prompt 1 yields groups such as students and staff (System layer), timetabling office and IT (Immediate), estates, accessibility office and data protection officer (Containing and Wider), regulators (Wider).","Prompt 2 with the org chart (names replaced by role IDs) maps groups to roles; ambiguous mappings are stated as assumptions.","Validation: review with the timetabling manager; correct the missing 'accessibility office' priority before planning interviews."]}},{"id":"weighted-matrix","topicId":"architecture-design","title":"Weighted trade-off score matrix","when":"Asked to compare design alternatives or write the trade-off section of an ADR","mnemonic":"Weights sum to 1, multiply, add, then sanity-check","steps":["1. List criteria relevant to the requirements (performance, security, maintainability, cost).","2. Assign weights that sum to 100%.","3. Score each alternative 0-10 per criterion with a one-line reason.","4. Total = sum of weight x score.","5. Pick the highest but apply human judgement for factors the AI is blind to (team skills, budget).","6. Record in ADR: context, decision, alternatives, consequences."],"example":{"problem":"Weights: performance 0.4, security 0.3, cost 0.3. A: 7, 6, 9. B: 9, 8, 5. C: 6, 9, 7.","solution":["A = 0.4x7 + 0.3x6 + 0.3x9 = 2.8 + 1.8 + 2.7 = 7.3","B = 0.4x9 + 0.3x8 + 0.3x5 = 3.6 + 2.4 + 1.5 = 7.5","C = 0.4x6 + 0.3x9 + 0.3x7 = 2.4 + 2.7 + 2.1 = 7.2","Winner B (7.5), but only 0.2 ahead of A: record the margin and the cost risk in the ADR consequences."]}},{"id":"mutation-score-calc","topicId":"mutation-testing","title":"Compute a mutation score and act on survivors","when":"Given mutant counts and asked for the score or what to do next","mnemonic":"Killed over (Total minus Equivalent)","steps":["1. Remove equivalent mutants from the denominator.","2. Divide killed by the remaining mutants.","3. Survivors = total - equivalent - killed.","4. For each survivor, find the behaviour no test checks; write a test that fails on the mutant.","5. Re-run; repeat until the target score."],"example":{"problem":"40 mutants, 5 equivalent, 28 killed. Score and survivors?","solution":["Denominator = 40 - 5 = 35.","Score = 28 / 35 = 0.80 = 80%.","Survivors = 40 - 5 - 28 = 7: ask the LLM for tests that fail on each of the 7 mutants, review the assertions, re-run."]}},{"id":"bva-test-cases","topicId":"test-oracle-quality","title":"Derive test cases from a requirement (BVA)","when":"Asked to list test cases for a rule, e.g. in TDD","mnemonic":"Valid, Invalid, X-1 X X+1, Null","steps":["1. Extract each rule and threshold from the requirement.","2. Write a happy-path case.","3. For each threshold X add X-1, X, X+1.","4. Add invalid, empty, null, wrong-type and negative inputs.","5. State the expected output per case and category.","6. Order for TDD: write all as failing tests first."],"example":{"problem":"Rule: orders of 100 euro or more get free delivery; basket must not be empty.","solution":["Happy: 150.00 -> free delivery.","Boundary: 99.99 -> charged; 100.00 -> free; 100.01 -> free.","Invalid: empty basket -> rejected; null basket -> rejected; negative total -> rejected.","Each case starts red (no code yet), then minimal code turns it green, then refactor."]}},{"id":"modification-answer","topicId":"modification-types","title":"Modification types: choose three, give two GenAI uses each","when":"Asked to select modification request types and outline GenAI contributions","mnemonic":"Type, Meaning, Two uses, Check","steps":["1. Pick three types you know well (corrective, preventive, adaptive).","2. One-line definition.","3. Two different, concrete GenAI contributions numbered 1) and 2).","4. One safeguard (tests, review).","5. Keep each type to about 3 marks."],"example":{"problem":"Corrective, preventive, adaptive (9 marks).","solution":["Corrective: fix faults. 1) Analyse stack traces and logs to find root cause; 2) retrieve similar past bugs and propose a patch plus regression test.","Preventive: restructure to avoid future faults. 1) Scan dependencies for vulnerable or outdated libraries; 2) rank refactoring candidates by risk and suggest refactorings.","Adaptive: respond to environment change. 1) Monitor deprecations and compatibility; 2) locate all code affected by an API change and migrate it.","All changes validated by tests and human review."]}},{"id":"repair-diagram","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","title":"Draw the fault localisation and repair loop","when":"Asked to discuss a repair approach with a diagram identifying human and AI roles","mnemonic":"Report, Understand, Localise, Patch, Validate, Approve","steps":["1. Draw boxes left to right: Human report -> GenAI structure report -> repo understanding and plan -> fault localisation -> patch generation.","2. Add a Tools box (tests, linters, build) after patch generation.","3. Draw arrows: tool failure back to patch generation (loop), pass to GenAI verifier.","4. Verifier to Human review and approve; rejection arrow back to the start.","5. Label each box Human, GenAI or Tool.","6. Under the diagram write one line per role and the stopping rule (iteration limit, escalate)."],"example":{"problem":"Describe the loop for a failing unit test in a Java service.","solution":["Human files the failing test and report. GenAI structures it (environment, expected, actual).","GenAI agent reads repo, ranks suspect files from the stack trace, narrows to a method, explains it line by line.","GenAI generates a patch; Tool runs the test suite and linter; failures return to the generator (max 5 loops).","GenAI reviewer checks the patch against intent; Human reviews the diff, approves or rejects."]}},{"id":"migration-prompt-method","topicId":"library-migration","title":"Library migration answer and prompt","when":"Asked for an example of GenAI library migration with task details and sample prompt","mnemonic":"Task, Pipeline, Prompt, Validate","steps":["1. State the migration (old library to new, size of codebase).","2. Describe pipeline: find, transform, validate, review in batches.","3. Write the prompt: persona, mapping rules, 'change nothing else', output format, TODO rule.","4. Name the validators (compile, tests, lint, diff filter).","5. Mention pacing and human review."],"example":{"problem":"Migrate 3000 Python files from requests to httpx.","solution":["Task: replace requests.get/post calls, Session usage and exception types with httpx equivalents.","Prompt: You are a senior Python engineer. Migrate the files from requests to httpx. Rules: requests.get(url, timeout=t) becomes httpx.get(url, timeout=t); requests.Session becomes httpx.Client; requests.exceptions.RequestException becomes httpx.HTTPError; add import httpx and remove import requests. Do not change formatting, names or comments. If unsure add TODO(httpx). Output only the migrated file.","Validate: run mypy and the unit tests per file, reject diffs with unrelated lines, retry or escalate failures; merge 50 files per PR."]}},{"id":"agent-diagram","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","title":"Draw the agentic static-analysis triage and repair pipeline","when":"Asked to outline an agentic approach to classify and repair static analysis warnings","mnemonic":"Warn, Classify, Plan, Fix, Validate, Rank, Review","steps":["1. Box: static analyser produces warning.","2. Box: classifier agent with tools (read code and docs, find references) decides true or false positive.","3. FP branch to justified suppression for human confirmation; TP branch to planner then fixer agent.","4. Box: validation (compile, tests, re-run analyser); fail loops back to fixer.","5. Box: ranker/reviewer agent scores candidate patches.","6. Box: human approves PR; label roles and what is passed along each arrow."],"example":{"problem":"Warning: 'class variable fields should not have public accessibility'.","solution":["Classifier reads the class, finds references: a field used by 12 classes: true positive.","Planner proposes: make field private, add getter/setter, update callers; keep change minimal.","Fixer edits 13 files; validation compiles, tests pass, analyser shows the warning is gone with no new ones.","Reviewer scores 3 (only warning-related edits); human approves the PR."]}},{"id":"legal-risk-answer","topicId":"legal-risks","title":"Legal risk with mitigation answer","when":"Asked for two legal risks of a GenAI-built product and mitigation for each","mnemonic":"Risk, How it arises, Consequence, Mitigation","steps":["1. Choose two distinct categories (IP, GDPR, AI Act, liability).","2. Name the risk and the relevant law or licence.","3. Tie it to the scenario in one sentence (loan decisions, personal data).","4. State the consequence (fines, forced open-sourcing, harm).","5. Give 2-3 specific mitigations.","6. 4 marks per risk: about 1 for naming, 1 for linking, 2 for mitigation."],"example":{"problem":"Irish loan-approval startup using GenAI: two risks.","solution":["Risk 1: EU AI Act high-risk (credit scoring): needs technical documentation, decision logging, bias testing and human oversight; non-compliance brings fines. Mitigation: classify early, log inputs, rule version and thresholds per decision, add human override and a tested suspension path, run bias tests.","Risk 2: GDPR: developers paste applicants' financial data into an external LLM and generated code collects excess data; automated decisions need safeguards. Mitigation: DPIA, zero-retention or private LLM, PII-filter proxy, data minimisation review, documented lawful basis and human review route."]}},{"id":"opinion-two-reasons","topicId":"bitter-lesson","title":"Opinion answer with a fixed number of reasons","when":"'In your opinion... provide two reasons'","mnemonic":"Stance, Reason 1, Reason 2, Caveat","steps":["1. Define the concept briefly (2-3 lines) if asked to explain it first.","2. State a clear stance in one sentence.","3. Give exactly the number of reasons asked, each with a concrete example from the course.","4. Add one caveat or counter-argument showing balance.","5. Conclude in one line."],"example":{"problem":"Does the bitter lesson apply to SE with GenAI? Two reasons.","solution":["Definition: general methods using computation (search and learning) beat built-in human knowledge in the long run.","Stance: largely yes.","Reason 1: elaborate scaffolding (prompt chains, hand-built retrieval, multi-agent org designs) is overtaken as models search code, plan and spawn agents themselves.","Reason 2: coding is verifiable, so compute for RL and parallel search keeps improving ability.","Caveat: specification, verification, guardrails and accountability stay human and durable."]}},{"id":"verifiable-judgement","topicId":"verifiable-tasks","title":"Judge whether an SE task is verifiable","when":"Asked to identify other tasks and justify verifiability","mnemonic":"What is the automatic check?","steps":["1. Define verifiable: automatic, objective, cheap correctness check.","2. For the task ask: what is the check (tests, compiler, formal checker)? is it complete?","3. Verdict: yes, partly or no.","4. Justify with the oracle and its limits.","5. State the consequence for AI autonomy."],"example":{"problem":"Two tasks: bug fixing with a failing test; architecture decision.","solution":["Bug fix: verifiable largely: the test must flip to green and the suite stay green; limit: tests may be incomplete or overfitted.","Architecture decision: not verifiable: trade-offs depend on team, budget and future change; no objective oracle, so humans decide and AI proposes."]}},{"id":"weighted-score-matrix","topicId":"architecture-design","title":"Score architecture alternatives with a weighted matrix","when":"Asked to compare architectural options against weighted criteria and pick one","mnemonic":"Weight x score, sum, pick, then add what AI is blind to","steps":["1. List criteria with weights summing to 100% (e.g. performance 30, security 25, maintainability 25, cost 20).","2. Score each alternative 0-10 per criterion.","3. Multiply weight by score for each cell.","4. Sum per alternative.","5. Pick the highest, then check human-only factors (team skills, budget).","6. Record it in an ADR (Proposed, then Accepted)."],"example":{"problem":"Slide scores: Monolith 8,7,6,9; Micro 6,9,7,6; Serverless 7,8,9,7; Hybrid 8,8,8,8.","solution":["Monolith: 0.3x8 + 0.25x7 + 0.25x6 + 0.2x9 = 2.4+1.75+1.5+1.8 = 7.45","Micro: 1.8+2.25+1.75+1.2 = 7.00","Serverless: 2.1+2.0+2.25+1.4 = 7.75","Hybrid: 2.4+2.0+2.0+1.6 = 8.00, so Hybrid wins."]}},{"id":"mutation-ratio","topicId":"mutation-testing","title":"Compute a mutation ratio","when":"Given counts of killed, surviving and equivalent mutants","mnemonic":"Killed over (Total minus Equivalent)","steps":["1. Count total mutants.","2. Remove equivalent mutants (undetectable) from the denominator.","3. Divide killed by the remaining total.","4. Surviving non-equivalent mutants show which tests to add."],"example":{"problem":"20 mutants: 14 killed, 3 equivalent, 3 survived.","solution":["Ratio = 14 / (20 - 3) = 14/17 = 0.824 (82.4%).","Write tests that kill the 3 survivors."]}},{"id":"stakeholder-prompt-steps","topicId":"ai4re-process","title":"Run the AI4RE Identify Stakeholders templates","when":"Asked to describe or apply the AI4RE process on a system description","mnemonic":"Fill, Group, Attach, Individuals, Validate","steps":["1. Define placeholders: [DOMAIN], [SYSTEM], [FORMAT].","2. Run Prompt 1: groups sorted by influence, classified System / Immediate / Wider, assumptions listed separately.","3. Optionally prepare an org chart file (anonymised) to attach.","4. Run Prompt 2: map groups to individuals or roles, state assumptions, keep group names identical.","5. Validate with domain experts before planning interviews."],"example":{"problem":"Restaurant adds delivery with Google Maps routing and e-bike riders.","solution":["Prompt 1 gives Customers, Owner, Riders (System, High); Payment providers (Immediate, High); Owner's son (Wider, Low).","Without an org chart Prompt 2 output is illustrative only; confirm with the owner."]}}],"practice":[{"id":"sample-q1a","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","source":"Sample Paper Q1(a)","marks":10,"question":"Define and distinguish between different modes of AI use in coding: (i) conversational, (ii) conversational editing, (iii) repository-agent. Refer to background versus foreground, synchronous versus asynchronous modes, and the human's role.","outline":["Conversational: separate chat, pasted/uploaded context only, explanations and snippets, foreground and synchronous, human = author (full control)","Conversational editing: IDE assistant sees file/workspace, proposes or applies bounded edits as diff, foreground, synchronous short loop, human = director approving each change","Repository agent: sandbox on repo snapshot with tools and tests, branch/commits/PR, background, asynchronous, human = task specifier and approver","Foreground = you watch and can interrupt; background = works while you do other things","Sync = turn-taking wait; async = delegate and return later","Context grows and autonomy increases from (i) to (iii)","Examples of tools for each","Risks: review bottleneck for repo agents, deskilling"]},{"id":"sample-q1b","topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","source":"Sample Paper Q1(b)","marks":5,"question":"With reference to (a), for what scenarios (if any) would you recommend each of the three modes (i-iii) and why?","outline":["Conversational: learning, explaining legacy code, error interpretation, design brainstorming, confidential code; why: human keeps ownership, low risk","Conversational editing: bounded edits such as add a function, write unit tests, small refactor; why: fast with immediate diff review","Repository agent: well-specified, well-tested routine tasks (dependency bump, bug with failing test, migration, docs), parallel work; why: background, scales, PR audit trail","Where not to use each (agent for product judgement or weak tests)","Safeguards: version control, tests, review"]},{"id":"sample-q1c","topicId":"genai-definitions","source":"Sample Paper Q1(c)","marks":10,"question":"Define each of these five terms: Generative AI, Machine Learning, Guardrails, Self-play, Human in the Loop.","outline":["Generative AI: generates new content (text, code, images) resembling training data; LLMs","Machine learning: Samuel 1959, learning from data without being explicitly programmed","Guardrails: constraints and controls keeping AI within safe bounds: tests/types in prompts, permissions, PII filters, version control, audit trails","Self-play: model improves by competing with or verifying itself, outcome reward, no human data (AlphaGo, RL on verifiable code)","Human in the loop: human reviews or approves in the workflow and is responsible for quality and outcomes; skill and rubber-stamp caveat","One example each (2 marks per term)"]},{"id":"sample-q2a","topicId":"ai4re-process","source":"Sample Paper Q2(a)","marks":10,"question":"We studied and used the AI for Requirements Engineering (AI4RE) process during lectures and labs. Describe the process and the resources AI4RE provides.","outline":["IREB AI4RE concept: pragmatic and responsible AI, human-in-the-loop, built-in risk management","Supports four RE phases: elicitation, documentation, validation, management","Process: choose RE task and template, configure placeholders, run prompt chain (e.g. Prompt 1 stakeholder groups with onion model, Prompt 2 individuals with org chart), validate with experts, iterate","Resources: AI4RE Prompt Guide with templates per phase, six-part page structure (task, AI approach, conclusion, templates, filled example, pitfalls), syllabus, CPRE certification","Prompt templates contain AI role, user role, task, output format and [PLACEHOLDERS]","Pitfalls: privacy (org charts, GDPR), file limits, transcribe recordings","Human roles: prompt designer/quality reviewer, risk and compliance manager etc.","Output is draft for review, not final"]},{"id":"sample-q2b","topicId":"programmatic-prompts","source":"Sample Paper Q2(b)","marks":3,"question":"Describe a scenario where we might use a programmatic prompt (use an LLM as an API).","outline":["Program builds the prompt and consumes the output, no human in between","Concrete scenario, e.g. CI script classifying bug reports into JSON labels and assigning them","Mention output format, validation, retry"]},{"id":"sample-q2c","topicId":"adr-genai","source":"Sample Paper Q2(c)","marks":4,"question":"What are the main components of an Architectural Design Record (ADR)?","outline":["Title/number","Status (proposed, accepted, deprecated, superseded), date, author/deciders","Context (problem, requirements, constraints)","Decision","Alternatives considered with pros and cons","Consequences / trade-offs"]},{"id":"sample-q2d","topicId":"adr-genai","source":"Sample Paper Q2(d)","marks":8,"question":"Describe the advantages of using GenAI for the following four aspects of ADRs: (i) Consistency; (ii) Research; (iii) Onboarding; (iv) Governance.","outline":["Consistency: enforces template and terminology, auto-detects conflicts with earlier decisions (vs manual and error prone)","Research: instant pros/cons, alternatives and trade-offs from technology trends (vs hours of manual analysis)","Onboarding: semantic search or Q&A on past decisions ('why not NoSQL?') rather than reading hundreds of files","Governance: alerts when code drifts from ADR intent, documentation kept current, audit trail","2 marks each, each with a contrast with the traditional approach","Optional caveat: hallucination, accountability gap, human approval"]},{"id":"sample-q3a","topicId":"sdlc-genai","source":"Sample Paper Q3(a)","marks":12,"question":"If you had to use GenAI for only one phase of the Software Development Life Cycle, doing all other phases in the traditional way, which would you choose and why? Refer to all six phases in your answer.","outline":["Name six phases: requirements, design, coding, testing, deployment, feedback","Clear choice with criteria (repetitive, verifiable, low blast radius, reviewable)","At least three reasons for chosen phase with examples (e.g. testing: generate unit and edge tests, mocks, analyse failures)","One line per other phase on why not and the traditional approach","Safeguards and limitation (oracle problem, human review)","Acceptable alternative choices if well argued (coding, feedback)"]},{"id":"sample-q3b","topicId":"se-principles-changed","source":"Sample Paper Q3(b)","marks":8,"question":"Here are four principles of traditional Software Engineering. Of these, which have changed due to GenAI, and in what way, and why? (i) Don't repeat yourself; (ii) abstraction / information hiding; (iii) requirements before code; (iv) infrastructure as code.","outline":["DRY: weakened, copies cheap to generate and to refactor; but duplication still harms review and context (211M-line study)","Abstraction: shifts to specs and prompts; AI reads large code but finite context still needs modularity; guardrails of types/interfaces","Requirements before code: strengthened; LLMs cannot guess the what; prompt is specification; tests as requirements","IaC: extended; AI generates IaC, agents act on infrastructure so IaC/version control gives audit; prompts and skills as code","How and why for each (2 marks each)"]},{"id":"sample-q3c","topicId":"rag-context","source":"Sample Paper Q3(c)","marks":5,"question":"Briefly describe the goals and workings of Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG).","outline":["Goals: ground answers in relevant, current or private knowledge, reduce hallucination, fit finite context window, no retraining","Setup: chunk documents, embed, store in vector store","Query: embed question, retrieve nearest chunks by similarity, add to prompt, LLM generates","Example: code search in Cursor or Claude Code","Keep knowledge base updated"]},{"id":"sample-q4a","topicId":"tdd-genai","source":"Sample Paper Q4(a)","marks":9,"question":"Identify and explain three different ways in which GenAI is used to augment the cycle of test-driven development.","outline":["TDD red-green-refactor stated","Prompt-to-test in RED: AI writes test cases from requirements","Auto-implementation in GREEN: AI writes code to pass tests, loops on failures","AI review and refactoring in REFACTOR with tests as safety net","Human verification of assertions and architecture; risk of AI editing tests","3 marks per way: name, explanation, example or benefit"]},{"id":"sample-q4b","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","source":"Sample Paper Q4(b)","marks":12,"question":"You are in charge of developing a test suite for a Java-based system for 3D printing. A core class, ManagePrint, allows users to select a print file, printer type, print material type, deadline (day and time), and then pay. Propose a detailed prompt for generating the test suite.","outline":["Role (senior Java QA, JUnit 5, Mockito)","Context: class, collaborators to mock, source attached","Business rules: file validity, printer/material compatibility, feasible deadline using injected clock, payment after validation, idempotency, audit","Must cover: happy paths, invalid inputs, boundaries (file size, deadline), time zone/DST, payment failure/timeouts, unauthorised user","Avoid shallow tests (assertNotNull), getters, order dependence","Quality rules: AAA, naming, parameterised tests, one scenario per test","Output format: test code plus table of test, rule, reason; list assumptions","Verification: coverage, mutation, human review"]},{"id":"sample-q4c","topicId":"test-suite-prompting","source":"Sample Paper Q4(c)","marks":4,"question":"In your experience, when using GenAI to generate unit tests, what prompt changes have the most significant impact?","outline":["Stating business rules and expected outcomes (oracle)","Explicit edge cases and failure paths; 'not only happy paths'","Forbidding shallow assertions","Specifying framework and style (JUnit 5, Mockito)","Giving real source code and asking for rationale per test"]},{"id":"sample-q5a","topicId":"modification-types","source":"Sample Paper Q5(a)","marks":9,"question":"Software modification requests can be classified as: (i) corrective; (ii) preventative; (iii) adaptive; (iv) perfective; (v) additive. Select three of these. For each one that you selected, outline two different ways in which GenAI can contribute.","outline":["Three types each defined","Corrective: log and stack-trace analysis; similar past bugs / patch","Preventive: dependency scan; refactoring prioritisation by risk","Adaptive: compatibility monitoring; find affected code on dependency change","Perfective: optimisation suggestions; boilerplate","Additive: candidate features from usage; impact assessment","Two distinct contributions for each of the three chosen"]},{"id":"sample-q5b","topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","source":"Sample Paper Q5(b)","marks":10,"question":"Discuss an approach to fault localisation and repair with GenAI, supporting your discussion with a diagram if appropriate. As part of your discussion, clearly identify the role of the human software engineer and the roles of the GenAI.","outline":["Diagram: human report, GenAI structure report, repo understanding, localisation, patch generation, tests/linters validation loop, verifier, human approval","Human: report, reproduce, supply tests and intent, prioritise, review and approve, accountable","GenAI: summarise bug report, repo understanding and plan, localise fault, explain code, retrieve similar fixes, generate patch, verify patch","Information: error message plus issue most helpful; contrastive tests; agents","Validation by tests and lint, repeat to limit","Risks: wrong patch passing weak tests, hallucination; human review"]},{"id":"sample-q5c","topicId":"library-migration","source":"Sample Paper Q5(c)","marks":6,"question":"Provide an example of how GenAI can be used for library migration in a large codebase, including details of the migration task and a sample prompt that could be used.","outline":["Concrete task (e.g. JUnit 3 to 4 or Joda-Time to java.time) at scale","Pipeline: locate, LLM transform, validate (compile, tests, lint, diff filter), review in batches","Sample prompt with persona, mapping rules, 'change nothing else', output format","Traditional validation catches stray edits; pacing for reviewers","Prompts as versioned artefacts"]},{"id":"sample-q6a","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","source":"Sample Paper Q6(a)","marks":9,"question":"Outline an agentic approach to classification and repair of issues detected using a static analysis warnings approach, illustrating the approach with a diagram that highlights the roles of the different agents.","outline":["Diagram: analyser -> classifier agent (with tools) -> TP/FP branch -> planner -> fixer -> validation loop -> ranker/reviewer -> human","Classifier uses tools: read code/docs, find references, search patterns; decides true or false positive","Fixer constrained to minimal change, one warning at a time, avoid new warnings","Validation: compile, tests, re-run analyser; loop back","Ranker/reviewer scores patches (CORE 0-3)","Agent history as memory, structured JSON output","Failure modes and human approval"]},{"id":"sample-q6b","topicId":"bitter-lesson","source":"Sample Paper Q6(b)","marks":8,"question":"Briefly explain what Sutton's 'bitter lesson' is. In your opinion, does the bitter lesson apply to software engineering with GenAI tools? Provide two reasons in support of your viewpoint.","outline":["Definition: general methods leveraging computation (search, learning) beat built-in human knowledge in the long run; examples chess, Go, speech, vision; bitter because it defeats human-centric approach","Clear stance","Reason 1: hand-built scaffolding (prompt chains, retrieval, agent org designs) replaced by stronger general models","Reason 2: coding is verifiable so compute scales RL, search and agent swarms","Caveat: specification, verification and accountability remain human"]},{"id":"sample-q6c","topicId":"legal-risks","source":"Sample Paper Q6(c)","marks":8,"question":"Consider the case of an Irish startup company that is developing software for loan approvals, and is using GenAI tools in its software development processes. Provide two examples of legal risks that might arise. For each one, outline a mitigation strategy.","outline":["Risk 1 (e.g. EU AI Act high-risk credit scoring) with specific obligations and mitigation (documentation, logging, bias testing, human oversight)","Risk 2 (e.g. GDPR: personal data in prompts, data minimisation, automated decisions) with mitigation (DPIA, private or zero-retention LLM, PII filter)","Alternatives: copyright/licence contamination (SCA scanning, SBOM, review); liability (human review, testing)","Each tied to the loan context"]},{"id":"sample-qb-a","topicId":"mutation-testing","source":"Sample Paper Bonus Q(a)","marks":6,"question":"Explain how mutation testing is used as part of unit testing. Building on this, describe a typical way in which GenAI supports mutation testing.","outline":["Mutants (small faults) injected, tests re-run","Killed vs surviving vs equivalent; mutation score = killed/(total - equivalent)","Evaluates test quality beyond coverage","GenAI: generate realistic mutants, triage equivalent mutants, write tests that kill survivors in a loop","Human review of tests"]},{"id":"sample-qb-b","topicId":"static-analysis-agents","source":"Sample Paper Bonus Q(b)","marks":5,"question":"Many traditional tools exist for static code analysis. In your opinion, is there a need for GenAI in static code analysis? Justify your answer.","outline":["Stance: yes as complement","Traditional limits: false positives, no context, inconsistent, no fixes, rigid rules","GenAI adds triage, context-aware classification, explanation, soft rules, repair","Limits of GenAI: non-determinism, hallucination, cost; so hybrid with deterministic validation"]},{"id":"sample-qb-c","topicId":"bias-fairness","source":"Sample Paper Bonus Q(c)","marks":8,"question":"What does it mean to say that GenAI tools may exhibit biases and stereotypes? Give a specific example of a bias or stereotype that you have observed in a GenAI tool, and explain how it may have arisen. Can you mitigate against biases or stereotypes by hosting an open-weights model locally rather than using cloud-hosted models? Explain your reasoning.","outline":["Meaning: outputs reflect stereotypes and skews of training data","Specific example (e.g. CEO always male, mandatory family name field) and how it arose from data and optimising likelihood","Local open-weights hosting does not remove bias as weights carry training data bias","What it does give: control, inspection, fine-tuning, privacy; downside: lacks provider guardrails","Mitigation needs evaluation, fine-tuning, prompts, review, diverse teams"]},{"id":"sample-qb-d","topicId":"verifiable-tasks","source":"Sample Paper Bonus Q(d)","marks":6,"question":"One of the reasons that GenAI tools have rapidly improved their capability to develop software code is because coding is a 'verifiable task'. What does that mean? Identify two other tasks in software engineering, and for each of them, justify whether you believe it is a verifiable task or not.","outline":["Verifiable: correctness can be checked automatically, objectively and cheaply (tests, compiler)","Gives reward signal for RL and self-play, and agent feedback loops","Task 1 with justification (e.g. bug fix with failing test: largely yes)","Task 2 with justification (e.g. architecture design or requirements elicitation: no oracle)","Caveat on test quality"]}],"mocks":[{"id":"mock1","title":"Mock Paper 1","duration":120,"instructions":"Section 1: answer any 2 of the 3 questions. Section 2: answer any 2 of the 3 questions. Each question is worth 25 marks. Duration 2 hours. (You need 40% in the written exam to pass the module.)","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"version-control-ai","text":"Explain why version control is important when using AI coding tools, and describe a good-practice git workflow for letting an AI agent make a change to a repository. [8]","outline":["Version control gives history (who, when, why), rollback, multi-person work, auditability: adds guardrails to AI","Treat AI like a junior developer","Commit before starting so working tree is clean and git diff shows exactly the AI's changes","Give the agent its own branch; one branch per feature or fix","Review its diff (diff is the unit of review) and run tests/CI","Open a pull request (unit of decision), human approves; resolve conflicts","If wrong: git restore for uncommitted, git revert for shared commits","Auditable record of AI and human changes; declare AI use in commit messages"]},{"label":"b","marks":10,"topicId":"implementation-workflows","text":"Describe the 'LLM as a junior developer' and 'LLM in test-driven development' implementation workflows. For each give the steps, two advantages, two disadvantages and a situation in which you would recommend it. [10]","outline":["Junior: ticket assigned to LLM, it interprets, implements, opens PR, explains; automatic checks; human reviews; revisions with LLM; approve","Junior pros: very rapid, control through issue and review; cons: review is the bottleneck, reviewers rarely validate everything so hallucinations slip through","Junior best when: strong quality safeguards, well-specified incremental tasks, clear context, system prompts aligned with team values, speed over quality","TDD: developer writes (and reviews) tests, LLM or agent orchestra implements until tests pass, then PR, human review","TDD pros: resolves review bottleneck, requirements enforced by tests, less need for human-readable code; cons: needs thorough tests upfront, LLM may cheat tests, LLM lock-in, token expense","TDD best when: requirements are precisely testable, tests are strong, tasks are verifiable","Examples of tools and orchestration (LangChain)"]},{"label":"c","marks":7,"topicId":"llm-concepts","text":"Define each of the following: (i) foundation model; (ii) context window; (iii) chain of thought; (iv) hallucination; (v) jagged intelligence. Add a sentence on why each matters when developing software with an LLM. [7]","outline":["Foundation model: deep network pre-trained for one task and adaptable; matters because one model serves many coding tasks","Context window: limited tokens the model reads; matters because large codebases do not fit, middle content is forgotten","Chain of thought: extra tokens to reason before answering; improves complex tasks, costs tokens, hidden in many UIs","Hallucination: confidently wrong or fabricated output (non-existent function); need tests and review","Jagged intelligence: superhuman on some tasks and failing easy ones; cannot assume reliability from one success","About 1.4 marks each: definition plus relevance"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"prompt-engineering","text":"Name the four elements of a prompt. Distinguish few-shot prompting from chain-of-thought prompting, giving one advantage and one disadvantage of each. Give a good-prompt example for a function that validates IBANs. [8]","outline":["Elements: instruction, context, input data, output indicator","Few-shot: 1-5 examples in prompt; adv: better performance, format fixed, low effort; dis: sensitive to example choice, weak for complex tasks","CoT: model reasons step by step before answering; adv: solves complex problems and exposes errors; dis: long output, cost, may loop","Distinction: examples show the pattern vs reasoning steps shown or requested","Good prompt: role, language, rules (length per country, checksum mod 97), examples of valid/invalid IBANs, output as function plus tests, handle spaces and lowercase, say what to do on unknown country"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"rag-context","text":"A codebase of 2 million lines cannot fit into an LLM's context window. Explain how context-on-request tools, RAG and graph RAG help, and give two practices for keeping context clean. [8]","outline":["Problem: finite context, primacy/recency, too little or too much causes hallucination, code changes","Context on request: LLM calls a lookup or grep tool; result is inserted; model decides","RAG: chunk, embed, store in vector DB; at query embed question, retrieve nearest chunks, paste, generate; Cursor indexes the project","Graph RAG: knowledge graph of entities and relationships (triplets) answers relational questions (what depends on X, what did we reject)","Clean context: update docs, restart long chats with notes (TODO.md), prefer plain text/markdown, add niche library docs","Context must be complete, correct, well presented"]},{"label":"c","marks":9,"topicId":"ai4re-process","text":"Describe the opportunities and risks of using generative AI in requirements engineering, and the human roles that the IREB AI4RE concept expects. [9]","outline":["Opportunities: mine requirements from reviews, interviews and documents; cluster feedback; interview guides and personas; summarise meetings; draft user stories; check ambiguity and consistency; classify and trace","Risks: hallucinated or fabricated requirements, bias in voice-of-customer data, data privacy/GDPR and confidentiality, over-reliance, review overhead","Human-in-the-loop; AI as assistant never final authority","Roles: AI process designer and integrator, prompt designer and quality reviewer, risk and compliance manager, stakeholder communicator and trust builder","RE engineer becomes governor of AI-supported processes","Output is a draft to validate with experts"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":25,"scenario":"A pharmacy chain plans an online prescription-ordering system: 50,000 customers, 5,000 orders a day, P95 latency under 300 ms, 99.9% uptime, strict GDPR compliance (health data) and audit logging.","parts":[{"label":"a","marks":10,"topicId":"architecture-design","text":"Describe how you would use GenAI to generate and evaluate architectural alternatives for this system. Given weights Performance 35%, Security 35%, Cost 30% and scores (Performance, Security, Cost) of Monolith (8, 6, 9), Microservices (6, 9, 5) and Hybrid (8, 8, 7), compute the weighted totals and state the winner. [10]","outline":["Inputs: requirements, use cases, constraints; prompt for 3-5 alternatives with C4 container diagrams in Mermaid, tech stack, trade-offs, deployment complexity","Context via RAG/graph RAG, add architectural styles knowledge","Trade-off analysis: criteria, weights, scores, totals, consequences","Monolith = 0.35x8 + 0.35x6 + 0.30x9 = 2.8 + 2.1 + 2.7 = 7.6","Microservices = 0.35x6 + 0.35x9 + 0.30x5 = 2.1 + 3.15 + 1.5 = 6.75","Hybrid = 0.35x8 + 0.35x8 + 0.30x7 = 2.8 + 2.8 + 2.1 = 7.7; winner Hybrid (7.7), margin only 0.1 over Monolith","Human judgement: team skills, budget, regulatory health data; AI blind to these; validate scores"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"adr-genai","text":"List the main components of an ADR recording the chosen decision and describe three risks of letting GenAI write the ADR. [8]","outline":["Components: title, status, date, author/deciders, context, decision, alternatives with pros and cons, consequences (+ AI analysis note)","Workflow: proposed draft by AI, team discussion, accepted final","Risk 1: technical hallucination (invented features, deprecated patterns)","Risk 2: contextual blindness (tribal knowledge, skills, budget, legacy debt)","Risk 3: accountability gap and rubber-stamping","Co-pilot not pilot: humans decide and sign"]},{"label":"c","marks":7,"topicId":"architecture-design","text":"Design a multi-agent approach with a supervisor for this architecture task. Name each agent, its responsibility and what it hands on. [7]","outline":["Supervisor coordinates and decides if output is good enough or must be revised","Requirements agent: normalises input into a structured form","Alternatives agent: generates 3-5 options","Trade-off analyst: evaluates performance, security, maintainability, cost with score matrix","Reviewer agent: checks NFR coverage, risks, consistency (GDPR and audit)","ADR writer agent: produces ADR draft","Human final decision; benefits: specialisation, explicit reasoning, traceability"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"test-oracle-quality","text":"What is the test oracle problem? Explain why AI-generated tests can be shallow or misleading and describe a human-guided workflow that improves test adequacy. [8]","outline":["Oracle problem: difficulty of deciding whether an observed result is correct","AI generates inputs and assertions but may not know intended business behaviour","Shallow tests: assertNotNull, status code only, interaction only, happy path only; copying implementation; trivially passing","False confidence; checking vs testing","Workflow: human defines goals, AI drafts tests, human reviews assertions, human adds edge cases, AI refines and expands","Humans provide domain rules, expected outcomes, edge cases, risk areas, invariants","Property-based checks and mutation testing to check strength"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"mutation-testing","text":"A mutation testing run on a module produced 60 mutants of which 6 were equivalent and 36 were killed. Compute the mutation score and the number of surviving mutants, and describe how GenAI could help next. [8]","outline":["Mutation score = killed / (total - equivalent)","Denominator = 60 - 6 = 54","Score = 36 / 54 = 0.667 = 66.7%","Survivors = 60 - 6 - 36 = 18","Meaning: tests miss 18 behaviours; weak tests","GenAI: feed survivors, code and tests; generate tests that fail on the mutants; triage whether any survivors are actually equivalent; human reviews assertions; re-run until target"]},{"label":"c","marks":9,"topicId":"test-suite-prompting","text":"Write a detailed prompt to generate integration tests for an online food-ordering service (REST API, PostgreSQL, payment provider, e-mail notification). Explain the purpose of each part of your prompt. [9]","outline":["Role: senior QA engineer specialising in integration testing","Context: components (REST, DB, payment, email) and integration points","Integration points to validate: order persisted, payment success updates status, failed payment cancels order, email sent after success, duplicate order rejected","Scenarios: happy path end to end, payment failure, database failure, invalid input, duplicate, auth failure","Quality rules: verify DB state and side effects, not just HTTP status; avoid shallow tests; independent tests; framework named","Output format: code with clear names plus table of rule verified","Justification of each part (role, oracle, scenarios, quality, format)"]}]},{"n":5,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"maintenance-debt-triage","text":"Explain technical debt and how AI-generated code can increase it. Describe three 'AI faces' of technical debt and the argument that AI makes debt cheaper to repay. [8]","outline":["Technical debt (Cunningham): not-quite-right code causing extra work later; kinds: code, design, documentation, test","AI risk: uncontrolled growth, duplication up and reuse down (211M lines study), churn, black-box code; maintenance 60-80% of cost; permanent maintenance penalty","AI faces: governance (over-reliance), prompt (undocumented prompts), model (variability), fast integration (unvalidated)","Technical deflation: cleaning is cheaper, borrow human hours and repay with AI hours","Caveat: friction accumulates, need tests and structure"]},{"label":"b","marks":9,"topicId":"code-smells-refactoring","text":"Define the code smells Feature Envy, Shotgun Surgery and God Class. Describe a GenAI-supported refactoring workflow and three failure modes you must guard against. [9]","outline":["Feature envy: method uses another class's data more than its own","Shotgun surgery: one change touches many classes","God class: too many responsibilities, poor cohesion","Workflow: determine as-is, to-be, check tests/coverage, implement in small steps with tests; agents (planner, generator, compiler, tester)","Failure modes: semantic changes, unnecessary edits, deleted code, wrong renames, hallucinated APIs, incomplete refactor","Guards: tests, compile check, diff review, structured prompts, traditional tools"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","text":"Describe a collaborating-agents approach to automatic bug fixing (such as PATCH). Name the agents, what each produces and how the human engineer is involved. [8]","outline":["Agents: bug reporter (root cause), code explainer (method intent, line-by-line), pattern summariser (retrieved similar fixes), patch generator, patch verifier (reviewer vs desired goal)","Each output feeds the next prompt (feedback information)","Dependence context and commit message supply intent","Collaboration beats single prompt","Validation with tests, lint, loop","Human: provides issue, tests, intent; reviews and approves; accountable"]}]},{"n":6,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":9,"topicId":"privacy-security-risks","text":"Describe three ways to prevent leakage of proprietary code and personal data to a GenAI provider. For each give one advantage and one disadvantage, and recommend a combination. [9]","outline":["Private LLM instance: absolute data control, fine-tuning; high cost, complexity, performance gap","Zero-retention API: frontier models, compliance DPAs; data leaves network (trust), lock-in, no customisation","PII-filtering proxy: proactive, cheap; loses context, false negatives, latency","Recommendation: hybrid, zero-retention API behind PII proxy, policy defined by legal","Mention shadow AI and Samsung case","No approach bullet-proof"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"privacy-security-risks","text":"Explain how a 'hallucinated dependency' attack works and describe three mitigations. Mention one other security risk of AI-generated code. [8]","outline":["AI suggests a non-existent package name; attacker pre-registers it with malicious code; developer installs; build runs malware and steals credentials","Mitigations: verify provenance, allow-list or internal mirror, lock files and hashes, SCA tools, least-privilege build credentials, human review of dependency changes","Other risk: SQL injection or hard-coded credentials, reintroduced unpatched vulnerabilities (Log4Shell), data poisoning","Scanning with Snyk or SonarQube, none 100%"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"ai-code-review","text":"Describe how the Claude Code team scales code review for AI-generated code, and give two criticisms of the approach. [8]","outline":["Goal: less human time per change without more reverts; human reading is a poor proxy for risk","Layers: pre-PR checks and fresh-context reviewer, AI review on every PR, security review, CI, risk-scaled human approval, merge queue, feature flags, monitoring and fast rollback","Risk tiers: none/one/two approvals; agent approval never counts as person's; prompter must review","CLAUDE.md and REVIEW.md instruction files; convergence rules","Criticisms: same-model reviewers not independent; self-reported figures from a vendor; no complete mental model; no circuit breakers; governance such as SOC 2"]}]}]},{"id":"mock2","title":"Mock Paper 2","duration":120,"instructions":"Section 1: answer any 2 of the 3 questions. Section 2: answer any 2 of the 3 questions. Each question is worth 25 marks. Duration 2 hours. (You need 40% in the written exam to pass the module.)","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":10,"topicId":"modes-of-ai-use","text":"Six modes of AI support for software development are: advisory chat, inline completion, conversational editing, interactive coding agent, repository agent and automated review. For each mode state the human's role, the context available to the AI and the typical artefact produced. [10]","outline":["Advisory: author; pasted code, uploads; explanation, snippet","Inline completion: author; current file and local context; inline completions","Conversational editing: director; file or workspace; diff or modified file","Interactive agent (foreground): supervisor; full repo plus terminal and tests; multi-file edits and test results","Repository agent (background, async): task specifier and approver; repo snapshot and sandbox; branch, commits, PR","Automated review: accountable author; diff plus repo, standards, CI; review comments and risk summary","Roughly 1.5 marks per mode"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"agents-and-tools","text":"Distinguish generative AI from agentic AI, and explain why agentic systems need stricter guardrails. [5]","outline":["Generative: reactive prompt-response, content creation, stateless per call unless RAG, outputs for human review","Agentic: proactive perceive-plan-act loop, multi-step goals, persistent memory, calls tools and APIs","Risk: generative informational (hallucination), agentic operational (acts in the world)","Guardrails: permissions and auto-approve limits, sandbox, version control and branches, audit trail, human approval points, tests"]},{"label":"c","marks":10,"topicId":"genai-definitions","text":"Define each of the following and give an example: (i) RLHF; (ii) self-play; (iii) foundation model; (iv) human in the loop; (v) large language model. [10]","outline":["RLHF: reinforcement learning with human feedback to make the model take turns, be polite, stay on topic; example chat assistants","Self-play: model improves by competing with or checking itself with outcome reward; AlphaGo or RL on code that passes tests","Foundation model: pre-trained network adaptable to related tasks; GPT/Claude/Llama","Human in the loop: human reviews or approves AI output and carries responsibility; code review of agent PRs","LLM: deep-learning model predicting next token; chatbots, code assistants","2 marks each: definition plus example"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":10,"topicId":"ai4re-process","text":"Using the IREB AI4RE approach, show how you would identify the stakeholders of a hospital appointment-booking app. Give the two prompts (with placeholders filled), describe the Onion Model and state how you would validate the output. [10]","outline":["Prompt 1: AI role domain expert in healthcare IT stakeholder analysis; my role requirements engineer; purpose: prepare interviews; task: identify groups, sort by influence, classify with Onion Model, list assumptions separately; format Markdown table (Group, Layer, Interest, Priority)","Prompt 2: map each group to individuals/roles using attached org chart (anonymised/role IDs), state assumptions, keep group names identical to prompt 1","Onion Model: product (core), system (users, operators, maintainers), containing system (customers, sponsors, neighbouring systems), wider environment (regulators, public, competitors)","Example groups: patients, receptionists, doctors (system); hospital management, IT (immediate); health authority, data protection regulator (wider)","Validation: review with domain experts, check assumptions, privacy check before upload, treat as draft","Pitfalls: org charts with personal data, file size limits"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"programmatic-prompts","text":"Explain what a programmatic prompt is, and describe a scenario in a software company where one is appropriate. State two design considerations. [5]","outline":["Prompt built by a program, output consumed by a program: LLM as an API","Scenario: nightly script that sends each new support ticket to an LLM and gets JSON {category, severity, suggested team} that updates the tracker","Considerations: output format and validation with retry, few-shot examples, low temperature, privacy of data sent, cost/latency, prompt versioning and tests"]},{"label":"c","marks":10,"topicId":"re-fundamentals","text":"Describe the multi-agent approach to requirements elicitation and analysis (Product Owner, Developer, QA and Manager agents). Include the four stages and two limitations of the evidence. [10]","outline":["Simulates a Scrum requirements meeting; agents negotiate in a loop","Stage 1 PO agent drafts epics and user stories (title, description, acceptance criteria) from the project brief","Stage 2 QA agent assesses stories against INVEST and ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148, records issues","Stage 3 PO, Dev and QA agents prioritise the backlog; Dev from technical feasibility, QA from coverage","Stage 4 Manager agent merges rankings into a final ranking with justification","Limits: no baseline comparison with human or single prompt; similarity is not quality; LLM grades its own output; four practitioners and undefined scale; hallucinations and thin acceptance criteria","Marked non-examinable but a good discussion example"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":12,"topicId":"sdlc-genai","text":"Name the six phases of the software development life cycle. For each, give one way GenAI can contribute and one risk or limitation. [12]","outline":["Requirements: summarise meetings, draft user stories; risk hallucinated or biased requirements, privacy","Design: alternatives, ADR drafts, C4; risk blind to team skills, hallucinated patterns","Coding: generation, completion, review; risk vulnerabilities, licence contamination, duplication","Testing: generate unit and edge tests, data; risk shallow tests, oracle problem","Deployment: devops config review and generation; risk credentials, blast radius","Feedback: summarise and prioritise user feedback and bugs; risk misclassification, bias to vocal users","2 marks per phase: contribution + risk"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"se-principles-changed","text":"Consider four traditional principles: (i) code review as a quality gate; (ii) document decisions, not just code; (iii) automate repetitive tasks; (iv) version control everything. For each say whether GenAI changes it and how. [8]","outline":["Code review: changed in who reads: AI reviews every PR, human approval scaled to risk, agent approvals do not count as human","Document decisions: strengthened and easier (AI-drafted ADRs) but accountability stays human; decisions made by AI must be recorded","Automate repetitive: extended to boilerplate, tests, docs, migration; but automation of intellectual core is discouraged","Version control: more critical as guardrail: commit before agents, branch per agent, revert; prompts and skills also versioned","How and why for each, 2 marks each"]},{"label":"c","marks":5,"topicId":"prompt-engineering","text":"Explain chain-of-thought prompting and few-shot prompting. When would you prefer each for a code-generation task? [5]","outline":["CoT: reason step by step before answering; use for algorithmic or multi-step problems, debugging; costs tokens","Few-shot: examples in the prompt; use when a specific style, output format or naming convention must be followed","Advantages and disadvantages (sensitivity to examples, cost, loops)","Can combine; modern models need fewer examples"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"tdd-genai","text":"Requirement: 'A password must have at least 8 and at most 64 characters, and contain at least one digit.' Using TDD, list at least eight test cases (input, expected result, category), and say which would be written first. [8]","outline":["Valid: 'Abcdefg1' (8 chars with digit) true; 64-char valid string with digit true","Boundary below minimum: 7 chars with digit false; above maximum: 65 chars false","No digit: 'Abcdefgh' false","Null, empty string false","Digit only: '12345678' true (no other rule) or per rules","Whitespace/unicode edge case","First test: simplest failing one (7 chars false) to drive the first minimal implementation (RED then GREEN then REFACTOR)","Mention AI can draft the list; human reviews expected values"]},{"label":"b","marks":9,"topicId":"qa-criteria-failure-analysis","text":"Write a prompt that asks GenAI to act as a QA architect and produce QA criteria for a requirement, and a second prompt to analyse a failing JUnit test. Explain what each asks the model to produce. [9]","outline":["Prompt 1: Act as senior QA architect; analyse requirement; for each criterion list conditions and outcomes (flag undefined), scenario, decision rules covered, severity (Critical/Major/Minor), automation feasibility (Unit/Integration/E2E)","Benefits: brainstorming partner, blind spots, standard definition of done","Prompt 2: Act as senior Java test engineer; stack trace, test code, production code; explain root cause, whether bug is in test or code, corrected snippet","Example: plusDays(365) leap-year; fix plusYears(1)","Human verifies diagnosis and fix"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"test-oracle-quality","text":"Explain what test doubles are and distinguish mock, stub, fake and spy. State four properties of a high-quality test and why AI-generated tests may violate resilience to refactoring. [8]","outline":["Test doubles replace real collaborators in tests","Mock: asserted against; stub: predefined responses, not asserted; fake: simplified working implementation (in-memory DB); spy: records calls and can be asserted","Qualities: trustworthy (no flakiness), maintainable and readable (AAA, naming), resilient to refactoring, thorough (BVA, branch coverage)","AI tests may mirror implementation (verify internal calls, private helpers) so refactoring breaks them","Mitigation: prompt to test behaviour not implementation, review"]}]},{"n":5,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":9,"topicId":"fault-localisation-repair","text":"Describe the three stages of a process-centric approach to automated issue resolution, and explain the fact selection problem and the idea of contrastive test pairs in program repair. [9]","outline":["Stage 1 repository understanding: structure tree, navigation, summaries, resolution plan with root cause","Stage 2 fault localisation: search file/class/function, context and dependencies, buggy region, reflection","Stage 3 patch generation with validation by lint tools and error messages, repeat up to a limit","Fact selection: which information to give the model (issue, declaration, docs, tests, error, stack trace, runtime values); mostly error and issue matter; context specific","Contrastive test pairs: failing test with a similar passing test help the model see the cause (ContrastRepair)","Agents and collaborating agents extensions"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"library-migration","text":"A company must migrate 1,500 JavaScript files from moment.js to date-fns. Design a GenAI-supported migration pipeline and write the core of the prompt. [8]","outline":["Discovery: search for moment imports and calls; classify trivial vs complex","LLM transform per file with prompt: persona, mapping rules (moment() to new Date(), format to date-fns format with token differences, add/subtract to addDays etc.), do not change anything else, TODO for unclear, output only migrated file","Validation: build, type check, unit tests, lint, diff filter for unrelated edits","Failures retried or sent to human; improve prompt from failures","Batches and PR pacing; human review; prompts versioned","Why split into steps: reliability and cheaper traditional checks"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"reverse-engineering-recovery","text":"What is architecture recovery, and how can GenAI help in combination with traditional tools? Include one limitation. [8]","outline":["Reconstruct actual architecture from code and artefacts; reverse engineering recovers design, requirements, formats; basis for migration","Manual process labour intensive and error prone; tools limited to lexical information and miss patterns/intent","GenAI summarises and classifies, recognises design patterns, produces diagrams","Indicators workflow: define patterns and criteria, search strategy, run traditional tools, LLM interprets; chain-of-thought prompting for deductive recovery","Limitations: ambiguity ('model/view'), context limits, hallucination, frameworks; domain knowledge needed"]}]},{"n":6,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":9,"topicId":"bias-fairness","text":"A start-up uses GenAI to generate a CV-screening tool. Explain three sources of bias that could arise (representation, historical, proxy) with an example each, and give three mitigations. [9]","outline":["Representation bias: groups under-represented in data, e.g. few CVs from older workers or non-Western names","Historical bias: data reflects past discrimination, e.g. leadership roles mostly male so model prefers men","Proxy variables: postcode, hobbies or university name correlate with protected attributes","Generated code itself may embed assumptions (family name required)","Mitigations: remove proxies, bias testing across groups, diverse team review, fine-tune on balanced data, human oversight, monitoring, documentation","Legal: CV screening is high-risk under the EU AI Act"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"professional-environment","text":"Discuss the environmental impact of GenAI. Explain why estimates of per-query water and energy vary so widely and give three practical ways a developer can reduce impact. [8]","outline":["Data centres about 415 TWh in 2024 (1.5% global), Irish data centres about 20% of metered electricity; GPT-4 training about 42.4 GWh","Inference across all users outweighs training once deployed","Estimates vary (519 ml per email vs 0.26 ml per prompt, about 2000x) due to scope (training vs inference, direct vs indirect water, model, hardware, year, who measured)","Efficiency per prompt improves but total demand grows","Practices: smallest model that works, fewer better prompts, limit output length, avoid image/video, use search for simple questions, avoid needless transcription"]},{"label":"c","marks":8,"topicId":"dot-and-swarm","text":"Summarise Mollick's argument in 'The Dot and the Swarm' and evaluate it with two criticisms. [8]","outline":["Claim: agents organise themselves, careful human design of agent organisation matters less; applies the bitter lesson to the org chart","Dot: personal proactive agent; swarm: about 10,000 agents, 88 hours, 2.7M messages, Navier-Stokes","Small-scale example: Codex starts three then thirteen agents unprompted","Limits acknowledged: cannot replace much human work, rogue behaviour (Hugging Face), principal-agent problem","Criticism 1: agents are copies of one model, shared errors look like consensus; human is beekeeper","Criticism 2: case was verifiable with one principal; real teams have many people and unverifiable work; cost and review of claims"]}]}]},{"id":"mock3","title":"Mock Paper 3","duration":120,"instructions":"Section 1: answer any 2 of the 3 questions. Section 2: answer any 2 of the 3 questions. Each question is worth 25 marks. Duration 2 hours. (You need 40% in the written exam to pass the module.)","questions":[{"n":1,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":10,"topicId":"implementation-workflows","text":"A five-person team must choose among five AI workflows (LLM as reviewer, as support, as junior developer, in test-driven development, vibecoding). Recommend a workflow, with reasons, for each of: (i) a weekend hackathon prototype; (ii) a payment module with strict regulation; (iii) adding 500 similar REST endpoints with an existing thorough test suite. [10]","outline":["(i) Vibecoding: rapid prototype, no reuse, little expertise needed, hackathon; accept low quality","(ii) LLM as support (or reviewer): developer stays in loop, high quality and expertise, low risk, accountable humans, regulation; avoid autonomous agents","(iii) LLM in TDD or as junior developer: well-specified incremental, tests enforce requirements, review bottleneck removed by tests; guard against cheating tests","Pros and cons of each choice named","Safeguards: version control, review, tests"]},{"label":"b","marks":5,"topicId":"implementation-workflows","text":"Explain the 'embodied agents' and 'agents as an orchestra' perspectives on LLMs in software engineering. [5]","outline":["Embodied: with shell tools (cd, ls, cat) the agent exists in a place, can move and interact, can have location-specific instructions; actor not stateless advisor","Orchestra: multiple agents combined with software such as LangChain, each with roles, instruments, locations; outputs passed on; developer is conductor","Example: agent per failed test, merging agent, implementing agent","Implication: guardrails and orchestration design needed"]},{"label":"c","marks":10,"topicId":"llm-concepts","text":"LLM output is non-deterministic and sometimes wrong. Explain why, and describe four practices that make LLMs useful and safe in software development. [10]","outline":["Next-token prediction with sampling: plausible not necessarily true; hallucination like a student guessing; sounds confident; jagged frontier; context limits","Practice 1: good prompts and context (instruction, context, input, format; RAG)","Practice 2: chain of thought and iterative prompting","Practice 3: tools and tests as feedback (run code, red-green TDD) so verification is automatic","Practice 4: human review and version control, guardrails, security scanning","Others: try again, newer model, multiple models"]}]},{"n":2,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":10,"topicId":"re-fundamentals","text":"Why is requirements engineering a critical success factor in software projects? Describe the classical RE activities and state for three of them how GenAI can help and whether the output is a draft or a finished result. [10]","outline":["Standish CHAOS 2020: 31% successful, 50% challenged, 19% failed; requirements-related success factors (detailed requirements analysis, involvement, communication, scope and change management)","Activities: elicitation, interpretation, negotiation, documentation, validation (analysis) and change management, tracing (management)","GenAI for elicitation: interview guides and review mining: draft","Documentation: user stories and SRS drafts: draft needing review","Validation: ambiguity and consistency checks: advisory feedback","Management: classification, tagging, prioritisation, trace links: draft for human confirmation","All AI outputs drafts for human validation"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"ai4re-process","text":"Write an AI4RE-style validation prompt that asks an LLM to find ambiguous or conflicting requirements in a draft SRS. Show placeholders, role, task, output format and one pitfall. [8]","outline":["Role: AI is a requirements quality reviewer with expertise in [DOMAIN]; my role: requirements engineer","Input: [REQUIREMENTS] attached; criteria: ambiguity, vagueness (fast, user friendly), conflicts, missing acceptance criteria, untestable statements","Task: for each requirement list issue type, quote, why, improved wording; list assumptions separately","Format: Markdown table [FORMAT]","Constraint: do not invent new requirements, ask when unclear","Pitfall: confidential requirements to public tools, hallucinated issues, must be validated by humans"]},{"label":"c","marks":7,"topicId":"re-fundamentals","text":"Identify three risks of using LLMs for requirements work and a mitigation for each. [7]","outline":["Hallucinated or fabricated requirements: expert validation, trace to source","Bias in voice-of-customer data (over-represents vocal users): sample diverse sources, review","Privacy and confidentiality (GDPR): anonymise, private or zero-retention tools, policy","Optionally review overhead and traceability: structured templates, log prompts","Human in the loop throughout"]}]},{"n":3,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":8,"topicId":"architecture-design","text":"Describe the four levels of the C4 model and say how GenAI and Mermaid can be used to produce them for a new e-commerce platform. [8]","outline":["Context: system with users and external systems","Containers: executable apps, services, databases (React SPA, API, PostgreSQL)","Components: structure inside a container (controllers, services)","Code: classes and relationships","GenAI prompt: requirements and style, ask for C4 text in Mermaid (C4Container, Person, System_Ext, Container, ContainerDb, Rel)","Render in Mermaid tools; human checks consistency between levels and with requirements","Use as input to ADR and trade-off analysis"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"architecture-design","text":"Explain how graph-based RAG can improve GenAI support for architecture decisions. Give the graph structure and two questions it can answer that plain text search finds difficult. [8]","outline":["Architectural knowledge is a network: requirements, ADRs, C4 components, incidents, trade-offs, constraints","Pipeline: documents, entity/relationship extraction, knowledge graph, graph retrieval, precise prompt, alternatives, ADR draft and matrix","Nodes typed (Person, Project, Risk), edges verb-based (DEPENDS_ON), descriptions on all; triplets subject-predicate-object","Questions: what solutions did we already reject? which incidents relate to performance? which C4 components are affected by this change? similar systems' trade-offs","Benefit: better context, alternatives, ADRs"]},{"label":"c","marks":9,"topicId":"adr-genai","text":"Describe the life cycle of an ADR produced with GenAI assistance, from first draft to accepted record, including who is accountable at each step and the advantages for governance and onboarding. [9]","outline":["Inputs: requirements, alternatives, trade-off matrix; AI drafts ADR with status Proposed","Team discussion, AI may contribute, iterate, even change the decision","Final ADR: status Accepted, context, decision, alternatives with pros and cons, consequences, AI analysis note; deciders named","Accountability: human deciders sign; AI is co-pilot; risk of rubber-stamping","Later: status Deprecated or Superseded when changed","Governance: alerts when code drifts from ADR, audit trail, consistency checks","Onboarding: semantic search of decisions ('why not NoSQL?')"]}]},{"n":4,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":12,"topicId":"test-suite-prompting","text":"A Java class BankAccount supports deposit(amount), withdraw(amount) with an overdraft limit of 500, transfer(to, amount) between accounts, and a daily withdrawal limit of 1000. Propose a detailed prompt for generating a unit test suite. [12]","outline":["Role: senior Java test engineer; JUnit 5, Mockito for collaborators (Clock, AuditLog, FraudService)","Context and source attached","Business rules: positive amounts only, overdraft limit 500, daily limit 1000 reset by date, transfer atomic, audit entries, thread safety if relevant","Boundaries: deposit 0, 0.01, negative; withdraw to exactly -500 and -500.01; daily total 999.99, 1000, 1000.01; across midnight","Failures: null target account, same account transfer, insufficient funds with rollback, fraud service rejects","Avoid shallow tests, getters, order dependence","Quality rules: AAA, naming, parameterised tests, BigDecimal exactness","Output format: tests plus table of test, rule, why; assumptions; check by mutation testing"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"tdd-genai","text":"Draw (as a labelled diagram or step list) an agentic TDD workflow with human in the loop. Identify what the human and the agents do and two risks. [8]","outline":["Human writes or approves tests (requirements as tests), possibly AI-drafted","Agents: implementer per test, reviewer, integrator, coordinator that escalates to human; loop until tests pass","CI checks, PR, human review","Red-green-refactor mapped to agent steps","Risks: AI cheats tests (edits them or special-cases), expensive tokens, lock-in, weak tests give false confidence","Guardrails: protect test files, mutation testing, human review of tests"]},{"label":"c","marks":5,"topicId":"mutation-testing","text":"Explain the coverage levels (class, method, line, branch, path) and why mutation testing complements coverage metrics. [5]","outline":["Coverage levels increasingly strict: class, method, line, branch (each decision outcome), path (each route; e.g. 6 branches, 8 paths)","Coverage only shows code was executed, not that assertions would catch faults","Mutation testing injects faults to see if tests fail, giving mutation score","Killed, surviving, equivalent","GenAI tests can have high coverage but be shallow; mutation reveals this"]}]},{"n":5,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":10,"topicId":"modification-types","text":"Classify each of the following requests as corrective, preventive, adaptive, perfective or additive, and give one GenAI contribution for each: (i) fix a crash on null input; (ii) support a new payment API after the old one is retired; (iii) add a loyalty programme feature; (iv) reduce a page load from 4 s to 1 s; (v) split a 3,000-line class to reduce future defects. [10]","outline":["(i) corrective: analyse stack trace and propose patch with regression test","(ii) adaptive: find all code using old API, generate migration","(iii) additive: draft user stories, assess impact, generate code/tests","(iv) perfective: profile and suggest query or caching optimisations","(v) preventive: detect smells, plan and apply refactoring with tests","2 marks each: correct class plus GenAI use"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"static-analysis-agents","text":"Design the 'ranker' step of an automated static-analysis repair pipeline: what it receives, the scoring rubric, which unrelated changes are acceptable or not, and why such a step is needed. [8]","outline":["Input: warning title and description, recommended fixes, original code, candidate patch diff","Rubric 0 to 3: 0 strong reject (unrelated unnecessary changes), 1 weak reject, 2 weak accept, 3 strong accept (only edits that fix the warning with least impact)","Acceptable unrelated edits: deleting comments, a = a + 1 to a += 1, version-specific syntax","Not acceptable: adding or deleting print statements, optimising computation, renaming variables, typos","Output reason and score only","Why: LLM patches contain unnecessary or hallucinated edits; ranking selects best of several; precedes human review"]},{"label":"c","marks":7,"topicId":"maintenance-debt-triage","text":"'Technical debt is becoming technical deflation.' Evaluate this claim in the context of AI-generated code. [7]","outline":["Claim: cost of cleaning code falls (refactoring a month takes a day), so borrow expensive human hours now and repay with cheap AI hours; debt as investment","Counter: AI accelerates duplication and churn, reuse falls, black-box code, permanent maintenance penalty, AI debt kinds (prompt, model, governance, fast-integration)","Conditions for deflation: good tests, structure and documentation so AI can refactor safely; failure modes of AI refactoring","Conclusion: partially true with tests and discipline; maintenance remains 60-80% of cost"]}]},{"n":6,"marks":25,"parts":[{"label":"a","marks":10,"topicId":"legal-risks","text":"An Irish company builds an appointment-scheduling app for medical clinics using GenAI coding tools. Analyse the legal issues under (i) intellectual property and licensing, (ii) GDPR and (iii) the EU AI Act. Suggest one mitigation for each. [10]","outline":["IP: regurgitated licensed code (GPL contamination), missing attribution, unclear ownership of AI output; mitigation licence/SCA scanning, review, SBOM","GDPR: health data is special category; personal data in prompts to external LLM; generated code collecting excess data; DPIA, data minimisation, DPO; mitigation private or zero-retention LLM, PII filter, DPIA","AI Act: scheduling alone is probably not high-risk (may be minimal or limited), but triage or diagnostic features would be high-risk (medical devices); assess definition of AI system; chatbot features need transparency disclosure; mitigation classify features, keep documentation, human oversight","General: liability remains with the company; documentation of AI use"]},{"label":"b","marks":8,"topicId":"bitter-lesson","text":"Explain Brooks' and Dietterich's criticisms of the bitter lesson and Sutton's own 2025 view of LLMs. Which practices from this course would you expect to be durable and why? [8]","outline":["Bitter lesson summary: general methods and computation win over built-in knowledge","Brooks: human ingenuity moved not vanished (CNN design), cost excludes researchers, Moore's law slowing","Dietterich: best systems combine knowledge engineering and compute; need to encode knowledge that excludes wrong but not right solutions","Sutton 2025: LLMs learn from human data, another human-knowledge approach","Durable: tests and verification, specification and requirements, version control, guardrails, legal and ethical accountability, architecture decisions","Likely redundant: elaborate prompt chains, hand-built retrieval, multi-agent org designs"]},{"label":"c","marks":7,"topicId":"verifiable-tasks","text":"For each of the following tasks state whether it is a verifiable task and justify: (i) fixing a failing test; (ii) writing user stories; (iii) generating unit tests. [7]","outline":["Verifiable defined: automatic objective check","(i) largely verifiable: test passes, suite green; caveat overfitting tests, incomplete tests","(ii) not verifiable: no ground truth, depends on stakeholder intent; only review and INVEST-style rules help","(iii) partly: tests can be run, coverage and mutation score measured, but assertion correctness (oracle) needs human or specification","Consequence: more autonomy where verifiable"]}]}]}]};
